-- allium: 3
-- rtdb-multi.allium
-- Writable checkpoint worlds store collision probes in their local scope alongside shadows.
-- A shadow miss probes the root registry at the world's retained archived cut.
-- The world registration record retains its internal UUID address.
-- Hosted fork selects a registered capture in the authenticated writer's deployment and derives its basis from the verified archive root.
-- Hosted world writes use the retained schema and the ordered registration's lifetime.
-- A held world point handle keeps one ordered local capture and one verified archive region until bounded expiry or close.
-- A hosted row-erasure owner can observe whether one exact ciphertext carrier exists anywhere in valid time under its held Root cut. The reply contains only a Boolean.
-- The installed live Original writer can enumerate the sealed search inventory under one canonical unit alias at its held Root cut.
-- The inventory page releases the exact sorted text identities and issues the relation frontier witness only on completion.
-- The captured registration fences new opens after ordered drop while an earlier handle stays inside its retained read admission.
-- A held point condition preserves the local shadow revision, including a tombstone, or verified local absence.
-- The protected shared column census binds the archived Root parent, frozen ancestors and selected overlay to the original ordering witness.
-- The installed Root shared-column relation uses complete Binary carrier identities scoped by the raw BFCP2 prefix.
-- The census enumerates that archived Root prefix and the text World-slot prefix across the frozen and local overlays.
-- A single census completion binds the Root carrier purge frontier, the World-slot relation purge frontier and the selected overlay relation frontier.
-- Ordered conditional world writes validate the witnessed placement, deadline and all local conditions before applying effects.
--
-- Scope: hosting an unbounded number of deployments on one shared, autoscaling infrastructure.
-- A deployment is one RTDB database belonging to one customer application.
-- Every cost a deployment imposes is proportional to its activity and its stored bytes.
-- An idle directory deployment has only its directory record and objects in storage.
-- Directory rules apply to directory deployments, and installed-journal obligations have their own authority invariants.
--
-- Status: target design, ahead of the implementation.
-- EpochScopedStorage describes the storage shared by default and Multi mode.
-- The directory-managed storage and reaper rules describe the deployment path still in service.
-- The opt-in stream driver uses the Logkeeper `journal-contract` boundary specified in Logkeeper's verification/target-journal.md.
-- A `logkeeper-consensus://` journal URL selects keeper-hosted consensus streams behind the same boundary, as described in dev/doc/rtdb-multi-consensus-keeper.md.
-- With immutable stream history, a stream ID names one committed history and remains bound to that mode.
-- A journal seal binds its writer epoch to the claimant's session and records the sealed head.
-- Appends and trims require that session's current fence.
-- Recovery past a lost quorum restores a new incarnation and journals under a fresh stream ID.
-- Consensus streams place each stream on three keepers in distinct failure domains and move one voter at a time off draining keepers.
-- With installed keeper trust they prove timed originals from signed syncs on two voters, so funded appends work on them.
-- The Multi writer checkpoints and trims before its retained journal suffix reaches the consensus stream's record bound.
-- With deferred checkpoints the writer captures that checkpoint under its database lock, builds and uploads it without the lock, and announces it later, so commits continue while it builds.
-- The standalone FileJournal enables deferred checkpoints by default.
-- The writer pipeline owns the build and cancels its upload future when the pipeline stops.
-- The stream driver submits contiguous append groups in LSN order and acknowledges the group after every record in it commits.
-- An unavailable append can leave a committed prefix, which the driver recovers from the journal before resuming writes.
-- After an operator recovery cuts a legacy consensus stream back to its survivor's head, the next writer acknowledges the recovery, records it in the journal before any transaction, and resumes, unless its node is configured to hold writes until someone else acknowledges it.
-- A candidate's process counts an acknowledgement when the journal confirms that candidate's atomic recovery marker.
-- A typed keeper recovery occupies the original fence's next position, and replay authenticates an application marker against that fence when the entry carries one.
-- A typed administrator acknowledgement supplies the recovery boundary directly from the keeper metadata.
-- A node whose replayed state the recovered journal no longer holds rebuilds it from the journal floor.
-- A cluster of the opt-in stream driver runs one node role per process and takes one journal URL with a client identity and trust bundle.
-- A development process opens FileJournal and a filesystem object store under its selected data directory.
-- The node uses the persisted database identity and key generations to recover the same journal after reopening the directory.
-- FileJournal requires the URI identity record format for development journal replay.
-- Each installed node takes an exclusive credential slot before opening its Logkeeper transport.
-- The same node image runs the default driver with Kafka source and replica logs when the cluster selects default mode.
-- dev/doc/rtdb-v1.allium defines the stateless consumer surface shared with default mode.
-- ScopedRowVisibility defines the target contract for labelled rows served by either mode.
-- dev/doc/rtdb-multi-plan.md stages the implementation and names the code each part grows from.
-- The target serving protocol uses scoped attempt receipts and a loglet read index.
-- The implementation still exposes numeric intake positions, fences on member lease loss and lacks the subscriber read index.
-- An installed deployment uses direct commits for ordinary synchronous SQL.
-- The native writer journals the ordinary attempt's admission witness and resolved outcome in the same replica record.
-- Applied read-back supplies the synchronous receipt.
-- Explicit asynchronous and timed SQL keep the placed receiver reservation protocol.
-- verification/redesign/authority-boundaries.md describes the migration and proof premises.
-- Where this spec and an existing spec describe the same mechanism, the existing spec describes the code as it stands and this one describes where it is going:
--   - election and the term fence: log-processor-lifecycle.allium
--   - the source log, the replica log and the block cycle: db.allium
--   - database membership on a node: database-lifecycle.allium
--   - trie and block garbage collection: gc.allium, block-gc.allium
--   - where compaction runs: compaction.allium (its job selection and merge algorithm are kept unchanged)
--
-- Includes:
--   - cells, and the map from deployment to cell;
--   - the directory, the authority for directory deployments' placement, epoch and checkpoint;
--   - node membership and liveness;
--   - the deployment log, its loglets and the log servers that hold them;
--   - the single-writer fence, at every surface where a writer has effect;
--   - object-storage namespaces, manifests, packs, boot objects and the reaper;
--   - residency: active, resident-idle and parked;
--   - the read tiers, read freshness and basis tokens;
--   - cache affinity, cache fairness and per-deployment quotas;
--   - gateways and sessions;
--   - activation, planned handoff, failover, scale-out and scale-in;
--   - in-deployment data branches, administrative deployment lineages, deletion and encryption;
--   - format and engine upgrades, quarantine, and moving a deployment between cells.
-- Excludes: transaction resolution, the live index's structure, the trie format, compaction's job selection and merge, query planning and execution.
-- Those are the existing specs', and this spec treats them as given.
--
-- Vocabulary, used throughout.
--
-- - CELL -- one independent unit of infrastructure: its own directory, log servers, nodes, gateways, workers and buckets, sized to a bounded envelope.
--   An unbounded number of deployments is reached by adding cells.
--
-- - MEMBER -- a node, gateway, logkeeper or worker registered with a cell.
--   A restart creates a new compute member and a new cache lifetime for affinity.
--   A logkeeper retains its identity with the journal.
--
-- - HOLDER -- the member that is a deployment's writer for one epoch.
--   At most one member's writes take effect per epoch, and epochs only rise.
--
-- - EPOCH -- the writer fence.
--   For directory deployments, the directory allocates one claimant per epoch and the log, directory and object storage check that epoch at their effect surfaces.
--
-- - LOGLET -- one epoch's segment of a deployment's log, held by an ensemble of log servers.
--   A deployment's log is the chain of its loglets.
--
-- - CHECKPOINT -- the current authority's pointer to a deployment's last published manifest and the log position it covers.
--   Everything at or below it is in object storage, and everything above it is the tail.
--
-- - TAIL -- the committed log records above the checkpoint.
--   Short by construction: flushing keeps it under a replay budget.
--
-- - RESIDENCY -- how much of a deployment a node holds in memory.
--   active: tail live index and work in flight.
--   resident_idle: holder retained, catalogs loaded, no tail, no tasks.
--   parked: no holder anywhere; the deployment is its directory record and its objects.
--
-- - LINEAGE -- a root deployment and every fork descended from it within one organisation.
--   Objects are shared within a lineage and never across one.

-- Catalog readers register nothing in the directory.
-- Superseded files remain readable for the garbage-collection window, and an installed retirement advances the serving residence's retirement floor without waiting for reader claims.
-- Legacy reader claims in a deployment record are ignored and leave with their storage region.

------------------------------------------------------------
-- External Entities
------------------------------------------------------------

external entity ObjectStore {
    -- Durable, immutable-object storage (S3, GCS, Azure Blob).
    -- Supplies put-if-absent, ranged get, versioned delete and inventory.
    -- Supplies scoped credentials: a credential can be restricted to a key prefix, to put-if-absent, and to a lifetime.
}

external entity KeyService {
    -- A key-management service (KMS).
    -- Wraps and unwraps data keys under a key bound to an encryption context, and destroys keys irreversibly.
    -- The regional deployment key vault is outside the payload backup and tracks destroyed identities.
    -- A local assertion stage has its own wrapping capability, bound to the deployment, incarnation and single-use stage identity.
    -- The broker opens a stage envelope only while that capability is live.
    -- A retired stage stays tombstoned, including when it never held a wrapping capability.
    -- The S3 provider gives assertion capabilities a separate prefix and conditionally publishes each permanent stage tombstone.
    -- The hosted S3 vault uses the same custody entry for a first residence whether its key identity omits the initial incarnation or spells it out.
}

external entity Clock {
    -- A member's wall clock.
    -- Used for system time, leases and timeouts.
    -- No safety property in this spec depends on clocks agreeing; system-time monotonicity and lease expiry do, and say so.
}

external entity Client {
    -- An application connecting over pgwire, Flight SQL or HTTP, with credentials for one deployment.
}

external entity Resolver {
    -- The transaction resolver of db.allium and live-index.allium, opaque here.
    -- Given a live index at a basis and a submitted transaction, produces a resolved transaction or an abort, deterministically.
}

external entity CompactionPlanner {
    -- compaction.allium's deterministic job selection, opaque here.
    -- Given a manifest, yields the same set of jobs, with the same output names, on every member that computes it.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Value Types
------------------------------------------------------------

value DeploymentId {
    -- Minted at creation, never reused, never derived from a name.
    uuid: String
}

value OrgId {
    uuid: String
}

value LineageId {
    -- The DeploymentId of the lineage's root.
    uuid: String
}

value CellId {
    name: String
}

value MemberId {
    -- Logkeepers retain their member identity with their durable journal.
    -- Other roles register an identity for each process lifetime.
    uuid: String
}

value Epoch {
    -- The writer fence for one deployment.
    -- 0 means never held.
    value: Integer
}

value Lsn {
    -- A position in one deployment's log.
    -- Dense from 1 across every loglet of the deployment.
    value: Integer
}

value Basis {
    -- What a commit acknowledgement returns and a read may require.
    -- A read at a basis sees every transaction at or below lsn.
    epoch: Epoch
    lsn: Lsn
}

value IdempotencyKey {
    -- Minted by the gateway for each submitted transaction, carried through retries of that transaction.
    value: String
}

value StorageRoot {
    -- The only handle from which object keys can be built.
    -- Issued by the directory; engine code receives it and never composes a prefix of its own.
    deployment: DeploymentId
    org: OrgId
    lineage: LineageId
    incarnation: Integer
    -- Derived: hash16(deployment) "/" org "/" lineage "/" deployment "/" incarnation
    prefix: String
}

value ObjectRef {
    -- A reference to one immutable object, as a manifest records it.
    -- The key always carries the epoch of the member that wrote it.
    key: String
    size: Integer
    checksum: String
    owner: DeploymentId
}

value Checkpoint {
    -- The directory's record of a deployment's last published manifest.
    manifest: ObjectRef
    boot: ObjectRef
    lsn: Lsn
    -- The hash of the log record at lsn, so recovery verifies the tail it replays continues this history.
    chain_hash: String
    format_version: Integer
    recovery_manifest_digest: String?
}

value CheckpointMetadataRead {
    id: String
    first_block: Integer
}

value ReplicaReplayHold {
    id: String
    first_required: Lsn
    owner: MemberId
}

value CertifiedReplicaPrefix {
    through: Lsn
    digest: String
}

value Loglet {
    -- One epoch's segment of a deployment's log.
    epoch: Epoch
    ensemble: List<MemberId>
    start_lsn: Lsn
}

value Capability {
    -- A short-lived, signed grant minted by a gateway for one session.
    -- Names exactly one deployment and the access it grants.
    deployment: DeploymentId
    principal: String
    access: read | read_write | admin
    expires_at: Timestamp
}

value StorageCredential {
    -- A credential a holder uses to write objects.
    -- Restricted to put-if-absent, under its own epoch's prefix only, and expiring within credential_ttl.
    deployment: DeploymentId
    epoch: Epoch
    prefix: String
    expires_at: Timestamp
}

value LoadVector {
    -- What placement balances, per deployment and summed per node.
    write_bytes_per_second: Integer
    tail_bytes: Integer
    query_cpu_millis_per_second: Integer
    cache_bytes: Integer
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Enumerations
------------------------------------------------------------

enum MemberRole {
    gateway | node | log_server | worker | reaper | controller
}

enum MemberState {
    -- joining:  registered, warming, receives no new placements.
    -- eligible: may hold, serve and receive placements.
    -- draining: keeps what it holds and serves it, receives nothing new, hands everything off.
    -- retired:  holds nothing and has left.
    -- dead:     its lease expired or it was suspected and confirmed; everything it held is unheld.
    joining | eligible | draining | retired | dead
}

enum Lifecycle {
    -- live:        the ordinary state.
    -- quarantined: activation crashed members repeatedly; placed only on the isolation pool.
    -- moving:      being moved to another cell; writes are fenced while the cutover completes.
    -- deleting:    fenced; its objects, log and keys are being destroyed.
    -- deleted:     a tombstone carrying the deletion record.
    live | quarantined | moving | deleting | deleted
}

enum Residency {
    active | resident_idle | parked
}

enum IsolationClass {
    -- shared:    placed on any eligible node of the cell's general pool.
    -- pinned:    placed only within a fixed small node set named on the record.
    -- dedicated: placed on nodes reserved for it, with a loglet ensemble reserved for it.
    -- isolation: placed only on the isolation pool; every quarantined deployment is here.
    shared | pinned | dedicated | isolation
}

enum ReadTier {
    -- writer:     the holder serves the read from its tail and cache.
    -- subscriber: a member that subscribes to the deployment's log and holds a tail live index serves it.
    -- snapshot:   any member serves a read at a basis at or below the checkpoint from object storage and cache alone.
    writer | subscriber | snapshot
}

enum ReadFreshness {
    -- at_basis:     sees at least the session's basis token; the default.
    -- linearizable: sees every transaction acknowledged before the read began.
    -- as_of:        sees exactly an explicit basis, which may be historical.
    at_basis | linearizable | as_of
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Configuration
------------------------------------------------------------

config {
    -- Cell envelope.
    cell_max_deployments: Integer = 2000000
    cell_max_nodes: Integer = 500

    -- Liveness.
    member_heartbeat: Duration = 500.milliseconds
    member_lease: Duration = 3.seconds
    failed_serving_probe_rounds: Integer = 2

    -- The log.
    ensemble_size: Integer = 3
    write_quorum: Integer = 2
    group_commit_window: Duration = 1.milliseconds
    log_max_retention: Duration = 7.days

    -- Flushing.
    flush_tail_bytes: Integer = 33554432
    flush_replay_budget: Duration = 500.milliseconds
    flush_max_age: Duration = 15.minutes
    block_max_rows: Integer = 100000
    clean_after_idle: Duration = 60.seconds

    -- Residency.
    min_residency: Duration = 5.minutes
    resident_idle_target_bytes: Integer = 65536

    -- Storage fence and garbage.
    credential_ttl: Duration = 15.minutes
    max_snapshot_lifetime: Duration = 30.minutes
    -- reaper_grace must exceed max_snapshot_lifetime + credential_ttl.
    reaper_grace: Duration = 1.hours
    boot_inline_limit: Integer = 1048576

    -- Transactions.
    idempotency_window: Duration = 1.hours
    command_replay_window: Duration = 30.seconds
    replay_permit_age: Duration = 30.minutes
    system_time_skew_bound: Duration = 500.milliseconds

    -- Reads.
    subscriber_basis_wait: Duration = 20.milliseconds
    max_read_width: Integer = 8
    read_sessions_per_node: Integer = 0
    read_width_quiet_samples: Integer = 3

    -- Control.
    activations_per_node_per_second: Integer = 200
    moves_per_node_per_pass: Integer = 8
    -- Zero leaves journal balancing off.
    journal_moves_per_pass: Integer = 0
    handoff_lag_threshold: Duration = 10.milliseconds
    move_reservation: Duration = 70.seconds
    quarantine_crashes: Integer = 2
    quarantine_window: Duration = 10.minutes
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Contracts
------------------------------------------------------------

contract CellMap {
    -- The only global state: which cell a deployment lives in.
    -- A placement change is conditional on the currently assigned cell, and the committed placement survives a cell-map process restart.
    -- Near-immutable: a row changes only when a deployment moves cells.
    -- Encoded in the deployment's hostname where the client protocol allows, so the request path makes no global lookup.
    cell_of: (deployment: DeploymentId) -> CellId

    @invariant OneCellPerDeployment
        -- A deployment is live in exactly one cell at a time.
        -- A move flips the row only after the source cell has fenced the deployment (MoveCutover).

    @invariant SuccessorHasExclusiveServingAuthority
        -- A separately commissioned immutable activation journal records the current incarnation and whether it can renew serving grants.
        -- A commissioned application's retained journal authenticates its exact authority and database name, including across trim.
        -- A fresh linearizable read of the binding authorizes a grant under reader credentials.
        -- Its holder expires the grant from before that read using a suspend-aware clock and a conservative rate bound.
        -- Closing grant issuance applies to one expected incarnation and activation revision.
        -- A durable recovery records one operation, expected binding, destination cell and loss policy before publication.
        -- Recovery freezes the exact operation. Supersession compares its identity and preserves any committed freeze and final lossless source cut.
        -- Lossless recovery waits out source serving grants and obtains the final committed position from a source quorum seal.
        -- A recorded installation must cover that final source position or carry explicit acceptance of an unknown tail at its verified cut.
        -- The authority retains the verified installation and commits publication against that exact frozen operation.
        -- Publication retries read the retained result and the predecessor revision fences older operations after a later publication.
        -- Publication compares that same binding, verifies the installed successor, and measures a full conservative grant window after observing the committed closure.
        -- The successor's identity is derived from the name, next activation revision and recovery operation.
        -- The local activation broker authenticates operator peers and owns its journal writer credential.
        -- The broker takes the expected binding and operation identity from the operator. It verifies a configured local destination or a signed receipt from the registered destination verifier.
        -- Cross-cell successor identity includes the selected cell. Its journal scope and serving grant bind that same cell.
        -- Cross-cell publication informs older readers by advancing the authority protocol to a version those readers refuse.
        -- A restarted publisher observes the committed closure and measures its own grant window.
        -- A serving operation acquires a grant for the incarnation it admitted and checks its deadline before releasing a result.
        -- A retained follower or writer handle acquires only grants for its own incarnation.
}

contract RecoveryGenerationCustody {
    -- The generation's key provider orders recovery holds against retirement under the same generation authority.
    -- A recovery hold binds the activation authority, recovery operation, destination generation and verified installation digest.

    @invariant RecoveryHoldPrecedesRetirement
        -- The provider admits a hold only on an existing generation with no retirement decision.
        -- An admitted hold prevents retirement until the exact hold is released.
        -- The provider reopens the same hold after restart and rejects substitutions.

    @invariant RecoveryHoldReleaseIsTerminal
        -- Release retains the exact hold identity, including after generation retirement and destruction.
        -- A released hold cannot be reacquired.
        -- A stopped-destination attestation with key custody follows a durable hold for the exact pending recovery.
        -- The activation record retains the held generation and the issuing authority connection identity with the verified installation.
        -- The provider releases the hold only after reading publication or supersession from that same activation authority.
        -- For a published hosted installation, the retained activation decision identifies the required custody evidence.
        -- The key custodian requires that decision and the original journal's replacement checkpoint before releasing the published hosted generation hold.
}

contract RecoveryJournalCustody {
    @invariant ExactDestinationCutRemainsRetained
        -- The destination quorum retains the selected checkpoint and its journal suffix under the recovery's exact order.
        -- The attestation binds that order and the original destination journal identity to the same installation as the key hold.
        -- The broker reads the original keeper hold before recording the installation and retains that evidence with the publication.
        -- The custodian releases only the matching hold after an authoritative publication or supersession.
}

contract Directory {
    -- A cell's linearizable, transactional, sharded key-value store.
    -- Holds deployment records and member records.
    -- Serves activation, placement, flush publication and lifecycle checks.
    read: (deployment: DeploymentId) -> DeploymentRecord
    cas: (deployment: DeploymentId, expected_epoch: Epoch, change: DeploymentChange) -> DeploymentRecord
    watch: (member: MemberId) -> Stream<MemberRecord>

    @invariant EveryEpochHasOneClaimant
        -- An epoch value is written into a record by exactly one successful cas, naming exactly one member.
        --   Every fence in this spec is the epoch, so this is the root of single-writer safety.

    @invariant EpochBoundChangesAreConditional
        -- Every change to holder, loglets, checkpoint, lifecycle, isolation class or engine pin is a cas conditional on the record's current epoch.
        --   A holder whose epoch has been superseded can change nothing about its deployment.

    @invariant EpochsOnlyRise
        -- A record's epoch never decreases, and every change of holder increments it.

    @invariant WatchesMayLagButNeverRewind
        -- A watcher receives each record's changes in epoch order, possibly late, and discards any change older than the one it holds.
        -- Sessions in one process that follow the same deployment share the feed and safety-net polls.
        -- A process consuming only keyed watches retains watch state for the deployments it follows.
        -- When a DynamoDB feed starts or reconnects, keyed watches refresh their own records after the stream is ready.
        -- A watch observation earlier than a confirmed route or holder epoch keeps that route or holding valid.
        -- A holding follows the shared deployment watch from its claim version onward.
        -- A newer placement can retire a local holding.
        --   A late change costs a redirect, never a wrong answer, because no watcher authorises anything by what it has seen.

    @invariant StaticStability
        -- A directory outage stalls activations, moves, flush publication and lifecycle changes.
        -- An installed holder continues to commit while its own loglet quorum accepts its epoch and has capacity.
        -- A node may serve while it can establish the requested freshness and its storage entitlement covers the read.
        -- Flush backpressure limits the unpublished tail.

    @invariant DirectoryFormatsPreservePlacement
        -- During the parked-row format rollout, upgraded readers accept both layouts.
        -- Both layouts carry the same deployment state and use the item's version for CAS.
        -- A catalog admission floor persists in both layouts, scoped to its residence, storage root, object namespace and replica journal.
        -- The current serving writer advances the floor from a durable installed trie-removal receipt.
        -- Readers register no holds and never block floor advancement; only a closed residence or superseded writer refuses an advance, and legacy reader claims left in the record are ignored.
        -- Enable packed writes after all readers can decode them.

    @invariant PlacementWorkScalesWithHoldings
        -- Rebalancing and compaction placement find candidates through live members' holding indexes.
        -- A claim reads its claimant, recorded holder, move target, ensemble and placed journals by key.
        -- Only a claim over a degraded ensemble reads the whole member set, to choose its repair journals.
        -- An indexed candidate's current record is checked before it affects placement.
        -- An index entry that has yet to appear is picked up on a later pass.
        -- A compaction worker checks the current record before treating an absent index entry as a revocation.
}

contract LogService {
    -- What a cell's log servers supply, per deployment.
    -- The log servers are logkeepers, built for this contract; the loglet kind is recorded per loglet, so another kind can back a deployment from its next epoch.
    -- A deployment's log is a dense sequence of records addressed by Lsn, carried by a chain of loglets.
    append: (deployment: DeploymentId, epoch: Epoch, lsn: Lsn, records: List<LogRecord>) -> Lsn
    seal: (deployment: DeploymentId, loglet: Loglet, epoch: Epoch) -> SealReply
    check: (deployment: DeploymentId, epoch: Epoch) -> Epoch
    activate: (deployment: DeploymentId, loglet: Loglet, epoch: Epoch) -> ()
    read_index: (deployment: DeploymentId, loglet: Loglet, epoch: Epoch) -> Lsn
    read: (deployment: DeploymentId, from: Lsn) -> List<LogRecord>
    subscribe: (deployment: DeploymentId, from: Lsn) -> Stream<LogRecord>
    trim: (deployment: DeploymentId, through: Lsn) -> Lsn
    drop_stream: (deployment: DeploymentId, epoch: Epoch) -> ()

    @invariant AppendIsConditionalOnEpochAndPosition
        -- A log server accepts an append for a deployment only if its epoch equals the server's promised epoch for that deployment, the server is synced at that epoch, and its lsn is one above the server's last.
        -- A server is synced at e once e's writer has copied its whole recovered history onto it.
        -- A partial copy leaves the server synced at the epoch it was synced at before.
        -- A server refuses an overwrite at e that would drop anything e's writer has already placed on it.
        --   A deposed writer's append is refused at the server, so no window exists in which two writers both commit.

    @invariant ReplicaIdentityFollowsTheJournal
        -- An ensemble continues to name the same durable journals when their processes restart.
        -- A logkeeper serves only while it holds the journal's exclusive lock and its member lease.
        -- A replacement must reacquire the journal and obtain a new lease after the prior lease ends.
        -- A stable advertised address remains reserved for that journal.

    @invariant JournalReplayUsesCompleteFrames
        -- Replay consumes consecutive records with a complete length and checksum header, the exact declared body, a matching checksum, and a decoded journal record.
        -- A decoded activation preserves the journal's recorded publication mode.
        -- Replay stops at the first incomplete or damaged frame.
        -- A read at a retained record location consumes exactly the recorded frame extent.

    @invariant WriterReplacementRetainsInstalledJournals
        -- A worker opening an installed deployment uses its installed journal ensemble.
        -- A separate durability-placement decision authorises a change of journals.
        -- For a directory ensemble the decision is the deployment's journal placement, which only its next holder applies.

    @invariant InstalledJournalIdentityExcludesDirectoryAdmission
        -- A cutover provisions a fresh deployment identity for the installed journal.
        -- A mixed cell permanently reserves the installed identity before sharing its media with directory administration.
        -- The reservation occupies the identity without supplying a directory deployment record, epoch or version.
        -- Directory creation and staging require an unreserved identity, and effects on an existing directory deployment require its versioned record.
        -- A worker sends an installed name through authenticated journal activation and uses directory control only for its directory deployments.
        -- Execution-memory pressure selects only holdings under directory authority.
        -- The installed grant and custody decisions authorize serving, recovery, checkpoint publication, trim and retirement of journal-owned media.

    @invariant RuntimeJournalPublicationRequiresDurableInstallation
        -- A voter admits a runtime group after synchronising its definition and initial voter media.
        -- The tenant installation reserves the exact deployment identity and provisions its two keys after a strict majority persists the group.
        -- Publication binds the tenant name and organisation to the group's route.
        -- The gateway acknowledges installation and admits route discovery through a matching majority after that publication.
        -- A retry for the same deployment derives the same placement.
        -- A worker verifies the published route against the activation's deployment and placement before opening the attachment.

    @invariant RuntimeJournalRetirementRequiresFinalCheckpoint
        -- The terminal seal names the selected checkpoint at the head of the journal.
        -- The seal succeeds in the first decision slot after observing the exact final checkpoint, a vacant grant, drained holds and no pending preparation.
        -- A durable retirement marker removes the group from worker and gateway discovery.
        -- The retirement marker preserves the sealed media for reconciliation.

    @invariant InstalledJournalOwnsWriterAndRecovery
        -- The installed journal chooses a replacement grant against its exact observed predecessor and the candidate's current enrollment.
        -- The chosen result binds the original paid append window and a protected recovery cut.
        -- A worker reports readiness only after materialising that exact checkpoint and applying the chosen suffix through the grant cut.
        -- An offloaded record retains its replay hold through the read from its original writer's object scope.
        -- The loaded body matches the digest in the chosen record.
        -- Replica suffix inspection preserves term admission and source offsets when it reads transaction coordinates from the recorded bytes.
        -- A gateway authenticates readiness against its invocation and the current installed journal grant.
        -- An installed journal route owns its session authority independently of the cell directory.

    @invariant InstalledReplicaPromotionFollowsEmptyRead
        -- After an empty replica read, a following worker with leadership enabled, a complete applied and recognized replica prefix through the received position, and a positive current-grant check can promote.
        -- An unrecognized replica record withholds promotion and installed-prefix publication.
        -- The worker opens the successor of the highest term it has read at sequence zero.
        -- A newly delivered record below the highest observed term is fenced at admission.

    @invariant InstalledReplicaMaintenanceChargesBlockProgress
        -- Installed replica appends use the maintenance lane for block boundaries, block uploads, and fenced terminals that settle inherited receiver debt.
        -- The lane charge carries through when an oversized payload is represented by a reference.

    @invariant InstalledJournalPayloadUsesItsGrantGeneration
        -- The installation provisions an independent journal payload key for its deployment incarnation and initial key generation.
        -- An installed writer seals each record under the key generation on its grant.
        -- A worker or custodian opens a chosen record under the key generation on that record's authenticated grant.
        -- Writer replacement preserves the current key generation, including across a vacant writer state.
        -- An operation that needs a revoked journal payload key returns rtdb/erased-under-view.

    @invariant InstalledObjectsUsePublicationKeySlots
        -- The installed journal placement, immutable provider namespace, purpose and object name select each slot.
        -- The key vault conditionally provisions the slot before object publication and preserves tombstones.
        -- Detached replica objects use their original writer namespace.
        -- Checkpoint preparation seals the manifest and copied members under new slots scoped to the allocation.
        -- A selected checkpoint opens against its exact manifest and the allocation's own slots.
        -- Checkpoint retirement closes the original selection and withdraws its listed key dependencies after its existing holds drain.
        -- Local and S3 checkpoint and serving-prefix retirement close their key namespace and settle its exact slot census.
        -- The S3 vault registers a slot before its key create can effect and freezes every registration shard before settling its slots.
        -- The terminal slot tombstone excludes delayed provider creates at that identity.
        -- A checkpoint capture combines each independently held slot with its capture authority before opening a file or replay record.
        -- Ordered transfer manifests, replica segments and detached bodies use distinct slots under destination custody.
        -- Serving storage releases bodies only within a journal-issued serving lease.
        -- A detached replica object reader uses the lease captured at its admission for cryptography.

    @invariant OriginalAssertionFamilyCustody
        -- The original operation vault keeps current assertion family records outside data archives.
        -- The retiring source publication orders the immutable logical family fence together with the exact derived Root erasures.
        -- A pending vault claim freezes the enrolled inventory until the original source confirms its marker or a durable abort horizon.
        -- Ordinary and archive releases resolve pending claims against current original custody.
        -- A recoverable response hold is staged and promoted only under its original authenticated carrier registration and a live current marker check.
        -- A carrier registration observation covers the exact original transaction sampled when its journal authority was opened.
        -- An exact Root response hold binds the activated member, stage, receipt, address and extent, disclosure and envelope digests to its original response part and cutoff.
        -- The family compare-and-swap excludes delayed promotion before the original vault acknowledges a terminal response hold.
        -- An expired release holds custody until exact private cancellation or authenticated transport disposal.
        -- A landing enrolls its destination and separate private link stages in a live family before provisioning either capability.
        -- A fenced family denies activation and current admission for every enrolled occurrence.
        -- Either a bound world original or an active landed occurrence can close the same family.
        -- A world retirement authenticates the current local carrier position and slot as well as the registered member.
        -- A controlled Proposal retirement with Root derivatives co-orders the registered receipt, retirement digest, marker and exact Root erasures.
        -- The first family fence fixes the live positions and includes both capabilities of every pending landing in its destruction inventory.
        -- Family custody supplies a current admission check for installed publication and release owners.
        -- A family-sealed Root carrier opens only at the exact activated position and envelope under the live family.
        -- The placed Original carrier route withholds plaintext when its current order contains the archive source fence.
        -- Retired Original evidence binds the source and target installations, custody binding and archive handoff to the transaction that records the closed writer.
        -- Pre-fence carrier creator evidence uses the exact registry transaction and the original installation visible at that transaction.
        -- The authenticated Original recovery owner endorses the selected approved replay scope with the installed cell secret after checking the accepted capture and current key namespace.
        -- The current Original order binds a cell-endorsed custody grant and its exact held replay approval in one transaction after the retired frontier.
        -- The grant authenticates the exact Root activation and distinct reader and Original control credentials.
        -- The enrolled reader makes custody requests through an authenticated session with read access to the current Original attachment.
        -- The reader can open the exact carrier only through a promoted response hold whose registration and disposition use the Original order.
        -- The Original controller can order a family fence on the retired history with the same live member and exact inventory predicates.
        -- The Original controller can acknowledge a positively disposed pre-fence response for the selected landing using its exact historical creator and address.
        -- A retired family release rechecks its current grant and marker through plaintext handoff.
        -- A founding World rank transfer binds the complete authenticated Original census to one exact destination hold.
        -- The Original adds the transfer to every contributing family before the destination can become readable.
        -- A readable transfer has an accepted destination replay receipt and positive copy drains on both sides.
        -- Dependent ranks stay under the Original stages and recheck the installed destination generation and all contributing Original fences at delivery.
        -- An inherited Held Root rank binds its current destination generation and ordered world registration to the exact retained Root publication.
        -- Each admitted Root rank matches the live Original landing and co-ordered carrier, journal and derivative occurrences at the selected valid instant.
        -- Delivery rechecks the same registration, the applied receipts and the current erasure projection.
        -- Family custody settlement waits for each enrolled copy to report an installed or discarded terminal and positive drains.
        -- The private Root landing link binds the enrolled member and the reviewed source winner.
        -- The release owner authenticates the prepared switch receipt and recovers an interrupted activation from the original ordered position.
        -- A native bridge durably enrolls both Original stages with the native review, keyed order and paired witness locations.
        -- A native bridge keeps the inner carrier closed until the Original vault records activation of the exact applied native placement.
        -- A native-backed family hold rechecks both current authorities before release.
        -- The Original deadline waits for positive native release and checkpoint-copy drain as well as the Original drains.
        -- The private deadline journal requires the exact stage inventory and all enrolled release drains before retiring the family capabilities.
        -- A commissioned single-vault holder binds the original vault namespace, exact family fence and closed stage inventory to the erasure order before effect.
        -- The holder requires signed release drains and drained family custody in its own vault before confirming a permanent tombstone at every enrolled capability, including unused stages.
        -- The original vault records the exact signed holder disposition by compare-and-set. Replacement recovers that disposition or resumes tombstoning from the bound order without source data.
        -- Signed terminal settlement preserves independent denials by original position and drops private family membership.
        -- A protected checkpoint transfer binds an exact source pin and cut to one prepared destination and the full original contributor inventory.
        -- A managed checkpoint reserves one copy owner while the selected destination is still private.
        -- The original vault retains the reciprocal agreement and puts the same forward entry in every contributing family before the agreement can bind.
        -- Each forward entry remains in the original fence inventory through family settlement.
        -- A destination can use an inherited original envelope only under the exact installed reverse binding and current admission of all contributing original families.
        -- Protected delivery checks the selected destination generation together with every contributing original family.
        -- An exact copy terminal can release the export pin and its copy holds while the installed destination remains subject to the original family fence.

    @invariant NativeDependentCheckpointCustody
        -- The native source forward binds the pinned source cut, complete group inventory and exact restored destination.
        -- A destination owner durably enrolls its application scope and accounts for every retained native placement, reserved placement, carrier and link at that cut.
        -- The source orders the exact reciprocal enrollment for each contributing group before the managed catalog marks the destination ready.
        -- A dependent disposition names that enrollment and the source group's applied revocation key, basis and inventory.
        -- The source orders a terminal only with positive reader and response-part closure for each enrolled key.
        -- The destination destroys its own keys and positively releases its references to keys under Original custody.
        -- The source group's release drain requires its own releases and every dependent disposition.
        -- The installed source writer checks the commissioned destination owner and exact observation request before ordering a positive receipt.
        -- The destination metadata reader authenticates the applied private restore and returns its reverse census and commissioned group metadata.
        -- The private destination selector uses the ready native restore to observe one enrolled physical placement at the caller's valid instant.
        -- The selector returns the destination generation, branch creation, and the winning occurrence's validity and native enrollment coordinates from the checked restore.
        -- The source operator recovers a disposition only by its exact retained order and receipt.
        -- The native inherited attachment withholds plaintext until it has an exact destination response-release authority.

    @invariant ClassicMigrationSourceSeal
        -- The authenticated recovery observer returns the exact capture, the current checkpoint and the live writer epoch from the same directory observation.
        -- A seal request overtaken by a later checkpoint can return its exact scratch closure once the directory excludes a fence for that attempt.
        -- The operator selects the original deployment, residence, log epoch, writer epoch, retained capture, attempt and native destination identity.
        -- The Classic holder closes its submission gate and waits for admitted transactions to apply before the directory orders the fence.
        -- The directory retains the applied source frontier with the fence and keeps the selected capture and scratch stage pinned.
        -- The source recovery owner drains admitted storage and catalog readers, seals the original replica journal and verifies the checkpoint and certified suffix through the drained source frontier.
        -- An identical retry observes the same retained source terminal.
        -- The verifier can retain independently sealed replay frames and detached bodies under the exact scratch stage with the terminal transaction and lifetime floors preserved.
        -- An import inventory reader authenticates the staged checkpoint, every replay segment and detached body, and the ordered replay commitment before reporting the exact source relation inventory.
        -- The source relation inventory commits each original event in inspection order with its original coordinates and canonical document, independently of physical recency.
        -- The reader holds scratch storage while examining the replay and observes the original fence and retained obligations without acquiring original journal or object write authority.
        -- The authenticated Original can reopen the independent application writer closure at its exact committed cut from the selected sealed replay.
        -- The Original installed application layout fixes the closure and writer points and the disposition relations before any retained read.
        -- The same replay proves the matching SQL release cutoff, closed authority row and fixed application disposition census while external Original obligations remain under their owners.
        -- A separate bounded point-history read returns only selected literal co-written application rows from that certified replay, preserving their typed addresses, valid intervals and Original event order.
        -- The migration scratch stage stays private and cannot seed a Classic target writer.

    @invariant ClassicMigrationProviderDrain
        -- The Classic transfer fence retains the exact admitted directory catalog reader cohort for the enrolled Local residence.
        -- A separate Original directory revision records the drain after the registered provider regions close and their catalog readers release.
        -- Private status binds the retained directory drain to the selected Begin and application writer closure through the verified scratch replay.
        -- Private release status joins one exact registered family response part, its ordered application settlement, and its immutable Original vault terminal.
        -- An exact status retry reopens the same provider terminal while the Original and scratch authorities retain its evidence.

    @invariant ClassicMigrationRetainedWorldReadback
        -- Before the source fence, authenticated Original Recovery selects the commissioned Begin, Original catalog, source capture and one active retained root World pin.
        -- The provider verifies the selected archive and its World-owned suffix at the exact transaction key under the installed archive authority.
        -- A positive observation records the unchanged retention and source selection after the archive read drains.

    @invariant ClassicMigrationOrdinaryFlightDrain
        -- The Original can reobserve the exact ordered transfer fence from its capture and private scratch before the keeper seal completes.
        -- The Original directory orders admission of each ordinary gateway and receiver generation against the same source revision as the transfer fence.
        -- The Original may replace the commissioned capture and scratch before fencing when the exact prior scratch is durably excluded and the replacement selects the current overtaking checkpoint.
        -- The rebind preserves the selected Begin, Original catalog, ingress coverage, retained path and all generation admissions and joins.
        -- The exact transfer fence freezes the bounded roster and its independently commissioned ingress coverage.
        -- A commissioned Original can fence only the currently selected transfer intent.
        -- The installed gateway, subscriber and receiver exclude unclassified FreshFlight for the commissioned Original.
        -- A generic gateway route authenticates a retained read before forwarding its bounded action, and the holder verifies its caller's identity and attachment.
        -- A trusted transport disposition authority can join a previously enrolled generation with positive evidence at the exact fence.
        -- An immutable generation join records positive disposal after that owner's ordinary admission closes and its work and transport output join.
        -- The final join and the stored order of all earlier joins form the Original terminal for that exact fence.
        -- A retained live Flight read requires the exact live Original holder, independently authenticated retained scope and permission for its action and body.
        -- Its handles retain that scope through every derived read and delivery.
        -- Status defers while the Original lacks a retained terminal for the complete ordinary fresh Flight population.

    @invariant ClassicMigrationNativeCandidate
        -- The authenticated recovery operator selects the exact fenced source name and authority, capture, scratch artifact and migration request.
        -- The recovery envelope authenticates the Original tenant name and the sealed stage binds its independently determined scratch name.
        -- The Original owner prepares the verified projection and exact candidate binding while retaining its own source, scratch and receipt authority.
        -- A current operator grant and the separate Original attestation authorize one exact candidate operation, binding the same Begin, destination, terminal, replay artifact and projection.
        -- The enrolled native peer admits the private operation under its own runtime authority and resolves install to the selected native stream writer.
        -- Native catalog reservation compares both name and physical stream identity and commits the exact candidate binding and projection.
        -- Candidate admission stays closed while the selected writer appends the exact binding and restored checkpoint under its private stream lease.
        -- The import preserves admitted literal event times and retains their exact transaction cuts under the candidate's native coordinates.
        -- Canonical receipt imports preserve their original namespace, key, fingerprint and outcome under the mapped cut.
        -- Authenticated Original receipt read-through selects the exact control row at the certified scratch replay and returns its retained event coordinates.
        -- Registered receipt read-through can select the companion coverage control from the same certified scratch replay.
        -- Original receipt readback depends on Original custody and can proceed during native catalog unavailability.
        -- The retained replay, its covered relations and remaining Original custody remain distinguishable in the private proof.
        -- A fresh exact status grant reopens the same native manifest through the ordered restore terminal after an interrupted append or reply, using the retained Original selectors without replaying scratch.
        -- Expiry or an unavailable response leaves the ordered outcome to be resolved by exact status.
        -- Held readiness requires the exact Begin, candidate binding, reopened physical installation, mapped application cut, expected endpoint and Bijection holder, assignment and epoch.
        -- The ordered native catalog compares the original reservation and holds one immutable readiness record while ordinary attachment, workload claim and application writes stay closed.
        -- A fresh exact readiness status returns the same held coordinates and catalog basis after a lost append response.
        -- Grant expiry leaves a committed hold intact.
        -- Opening the held candidate requires independently authenticated evidence of the exact applied and current Bijection route decision.
        -- Bijection holds that route and holder until an authenticated terminal resolves the pending native opening.
        -- The operator explicitly requests opening, exclusion or status for one exact decision and held-ready record under a distinct authenticated identity.
        -- Each authenticated writer redirect preserves the opening request and commissioned peer identity and is bounded within that invocation.
        -- Operator readback reports a terminal only when the exact witness, subsequent catalog basis and any required initial workload fence agree.
        -- Native opening and exclusion contend for the exact closed held-ready version, and an ordered opening reservation prevents later exclusion.
        -- The native writer recovers or installs the exact initial workload fence at the mapped application cut before the native catalog orders opening.
        -- Opening admits only the matching commissioned workload, holder, assignment and epoch, and the Original remains closed.
        -- The active writer verifies a later workload fence on the same native stream from its exact ordered receipt and current authority row.
        -- Later placement advances require an ordered closed writer or vacant predecessor at or beyond the imported fence.
        -- Exact attempt settlement on the imported stream exposes only terminals ordered after that fence.
        -- Commissioned Original branch provisioning and family placement require the current ordered writer at or beyond the imported fence.
        -- The commissioned named family starts at a creation Basis after that writer's ClaimWriter, and its retained receipts remain strictly after the imported fence.
        -- Commissioned Original family readback verifies the current writer and import fence and limits receipt-bearing projections to the named family after its creation.
        -- The mounted Original admits private replay against the current writer, named creation and selected cursor, paired same-session review proof and Claim, and exact author receipts.
        -- The independent reader claims the ordered hold once under the current group and cutoff.
        -- Positive independent comparison for the exact claim authorizes consumption by one same-session retained reservation with the committed reserve and acceptance keys.
        -- Native family drain waits for the positive authenticated disposition of each private replay hold.
        -- Parent observation returns only the Basis and system time reached by the authenticated named Fork.
        -- Historical readback returns the exact ordered opening or exclusion independently of current authority.

    @invariant InstalledJournalErasureCommit
        -- An enrolled writer reports the first committed SQL erase under its exact replica position and custody history.
        -- Ordinary checkpoint selection preserves replay of that committed marker.
        -- The broker authenticates the paired fork origins and the source cut for each dependent erasure.
        -- Each dependent successor uses its own certified head.
        -- The cohort provides signed readable successors before any root requests rotation.
        -- The custody authority closes new serving and copy admissions and retains that fence through restart until the erasure attempt is durably resolved.
        -- The incumbent drains outstanding appends before the custodian fixes the journal head.
        -- The clean successor at that head preserves surviving history and excludes targeted history and retired key dependencies from the full recovery closure.
        -- The selected catalog carries enough confirmation evidence for recovery to start at the exact head.
        -- The next journal payload key is provisioned before the custodian orders publication of the clean successor at the rotation cut.
        -- Rotation compares the exact current writer grant and published checkpoint at the current journal head.
        -- An uncertain rotation reuses the request identity persisted with that exact grant and checkpoint.
        -- The successor grant keeps the enrollment and advances both the writer epoch and payload key generation.
        -- Appends under the previous grant are fenced at the rotation cut.
        -- The erasure commit requires durable evidence of a clean root at or after that cut and a clean suffix in every crash-selectable slot.
        -- Subsequent recovery slot selection preserves that evidence's exclusion of older roots.
        -- The commit publishes its identity, closes admission and durably records the old generation's destruction obligations at the same decision.
        -- A view admitted before the fence can finish within its original exclusive grant deadline.
        -- A fixed deadline requires a bounded custody drain followed by a bounded destruction and evidence tail.
        -- A held view requiring withdrawn material returns rtdb/erased-under-view.

    @invariant InstalledCheckpointPrecedesTrim
        -- A checkpoint custodian supplies the exact immutable provider manifest for one reconstructable RTDB checkpoint and journal cut.
        -- A custodian can identify the next native checkpoint by its chosen boundary, upload and exact catalog digest independently of the writer grant.
        -- A stale inspection cannot authorize staging under a different base.
        -- Local checkpoint staging and readers share the allocation lock until exclusive retirement.
        -- A custodian synchronizes a reused local object and its directory entries before treating it as durable.
        -- The journal orders publication under the custodian before accepting trim through that cut.
        -- An ordinary native checkpoint cut is the inclusive replica boundary plus one.
        -- With deferred checkpoints enabled, a confirmed installed writer may resolve and apply later untimed transactions while the sealed checkpoint uploads.
        -- The custodian selects the sealed boundary and its certified root, and trim preserves every committed record after that boundary.
        -- The current installed writer may protect its applied catalog for transaction resolution, live SQL reads and receipt lookups through the selected keeper checkpoint and the exact suffix covering its newer adopted block.
        -- An exact reader names its captured checkpoint root and independently protects its captured suffix.
        -- Protected replay that crosses the boundary holds both live generations until adopting the sealed checkpoint from the matching upload's admitted files.
        -- The custodian names the selected checkpoint and its exact cut when advancing the retained floor.
        -- An immutable Multi journal can append and select its checkpoint announcement and advance the logical floor in one keeper decision.
        -- Replay uses the selected announcement when several retained checkpoints cover the same cut.
        -- To select an already committed clean checkpoint, the writer proves the exact retained announcement in an applied journal view covering its observed head.
        -- The keeper verifies the announcement position and retained bytes under the current writer fence before ordering that cut.
        -- The certified trim retains its announcement and is scoped to the stream and writer session.
        -- A live replay reservation protects its exact interval, and an independent checkpoint hold protects its original root.

    @invariant InstalledServingPrefixRetirement
        -- Each writer grant writes its blocks and offloaded payloads below its own serving prefix, named by the installation, journal group and generation, and grant epoch, and records its exact grant there when it opens.
        -- The custodian retires the prefix of an earlier grant only when the journal names a later grant, the selected checkpoint is independent, the journal is trimmed to its cut, and no retained record or pending preparation comes from that grant.
        -- Retirement first records an intent bound to the grant, the physical provider and its inventory kind, and the time the fence was first observed.
        -- A local prefix retires under an exclusive lock that no reader or writer holds, records a permanent closed marker, and is then removed; later opens find the marker and are refused.
        -- An exact-version S3 prefix retires after the scoped write horizon from the observed fence, with its uploads drained and every version and delete marker removed.
        -- An enrolled never-versioned S3 prefix retires after the same horizon by conditional tombstones on every member, its grant record last, with its uploads drained.
        -- An unqualified provider or a prefix without its grant record is refused.
        -- A settled receipt records the evidence, and a retry resumes from the recorded intent.

    @invariant InstalledServingObjectReclamation
        -- A serving writer submits a reclamation candidate only for an observed object in its own grant's prefix.
        -- The create-only candidate binds the exact grant, provider namespace and key, and includes the observed provider version when available.
        -- Equivalent submissions retain one candidate, and the candidate survives a worker restart.
        -- The queue retains each candidate independently of checkpoint selection and conveys no permission to remove its object.
        -- Physical removal requires an exact withdrawal decision ordered against reference admission.
        -- Every selectable checkpoint and pending preparation, every retained typed replay suffix, and every live journal hold protect their dependencies from withdrawal.
        -- Installed S3 reads of reclaimable serving objects register before testing the physical admission fence and retain their registrations through conclusive provider completion.
        -- The withdrawal owner closes the object's physical admission before observing an empty provider census.
        -- Ambiguous provider completion retains the read's registration.
        -- The withdrawal owner reports the identity of each registration it observes blocking reclamation.
        -- A registration remains pending until conclusive completion permits release.
        -- Physical removal follows the withdrawal fence and the drain of previously admitted operations for that object.
        -- Exact stable absence settles the nominated publication even when a different current publication is visible.
        -- An authorized retirement of a superseded grant's prefix covers that prefix's candidate records as well.

    @invariant FreshJournalReadConsumesAnInvocation
        -- The journal admits a fresh barrier under the exact enrolled worker identity at its chosen head.
        -- The chosen read result authenticates the exact placement, reader enrollment, invocation and applied cut.
        -- An empty journal's read cut selects an empty catalog with no transaction basis.
        -- A published empty catalog names the exact replica floor and the current storage version and epoch.
        -- The receiving database applies the protected cut before the fresh read can proceed.
        -- Current SQL on the active installed writer can protect a later published cut in the same history once it covers the barrier.
        -- An exact certified read retains its selected cut.
        -- Every read is a fresh admission under a new invocation, and the journal never replays an earlier read's result.
        -- The reader can retry a conflicting installed barrier using a new route and invocation.
        -- A reader that loses a read's outcome releases it by invocation identity, and that release settles once the invocation can no longer be admitted.
        -- Mutations keep exact retry through their original receipts.

    @invariant InstalledWorkerHoldExpiryFollowsItsServingLease
        -- A worker admits each read within its journal-issued serving lease.
        -- The journal accepts expiry disposal from the administrator or custodian for a withdrawn enrollment once the last lease end plus the clock drift bound is at or before the chosen decision time.
        -- A withdrawn enrollment that never held a lease is immediately eligible for expiry disposal.
        -- The disposal decision releases the holds of that exact enrollment.

    @invariant CellCustodyRunsIndependently
        -- The dedicated cell custodian maintains the configured deployments using their installed custody authority.
        -- A directory reservation nominates a discovery candidate, and a matching voter majority supplies its published route.
        -- The custodian uses that route and its custody credential to dispose expired withdrawn owners, trim the selected cut, and replenish the append window.
        -- It can discover and publish a chosen native checkpoint and advance retention when the writer grant is vacant.
        -- Its checkpoint copies carry an exact ordered worker hold for the base cut through provider completion.
        -- The installed S3 serving participants share a durable read census before reclaiming an exact publication.

    @invariant CustodianDisposesExpiredWorkerHoldsBeforeTrim
        -- The journal exposes a custodian-only bounded page of exact withdrawn enrollments that own holds in the selected state and their expiry disposal eligibility.
        -- The custodian checks those enrollments before attempting trim on its maintenance pass.
        -- The custodian attempts a fresh disposal when its clock or the selected journal time reaches the eligibility time, including while the journal is idle.
        -- The journal stamps a fresh decision with its own time and rechecks eligibility.
        -- A lost outcome within the retry horizon keeps the same invocation.
        -- A chosen Unavailable allows a fresh invocation on a later pass.

    @invariant QuorumsCountDistinctJournals
        -- Each loglet slot names a distinct durable journal.
        -- The client verifies the journal on each session before sending a journal operation.
        -- A journal verifies that a bound operation addresses its identity before acting.
        -- A failed or different identity contributes no vote to recovery, append, repair, read confirmation or retention.
        -- Copy reservation and disposal retain journal identities for the full inventory, including members that have left the serving ensemble.
        -- A live deployment's retained copy leaves that inventory only under JournalCopyLeavesCustodyOnlyByRelease.
        -- A legacy address inventory requires verified custody recorded at the inspected directory version before it authorizes a cell transfer or retirement.

    @invariant LocalJournalHasProcessBoundTransport
        -- A standalone authority addresses its journal by the retained journal ID.
        -- Only the process holding the journal can reach that transport.

    @invariant VisibilityRequiresAnInstalledDecision
        -- Payload votes authenticate distinct physical journals, the active configuration and the exact recoverable prefix digest.
        -- The decision certificate binds the predecessor and admissible evidence.
        -- Each installer durably records the full compatible certificate in its local journal order.
        -- Append receipts require a quorum of installations. Transaction replies and external effects also require exact application.
        -- Sealing retains installed certificates and potentially chosen ordinary values, and activation installs the recovered prefix and dependencies on the successor quorum.

    @invariant GuardedAppendRequiresALiveUpperSample
        -- Before a guarded append takes a log position or dispatches a record, the writer samples its live clock in append order.
        -- The upper sample is one microsecond above the sampled clock and must be representable, positive, at least the retained staging sample, and strictly before the guarded cutoff.
        -- A supplied journal cutoff must be at least the guarded cutoff.
        -- The writer records its sampled clock as the append timestamp.

    @invariant GuardedSyncObservationsFollowTheOriginalAppend
        -- A guarded record's durable frame records whether it was originally appended here or copied.
        -- A journal samples its clock after the durable sync containing an original guarded append.
        -- It persists the sample with that exact retained entry before replying.
        -- A journal refuses to overwrite a locally original guarded entry with a copy at the same LSN and entry epoch, even when its sample is absent.
        -- Compaction and replay preserve the local origin marker and any persisted sample of each retained entry.
        -- A copy placed into a journal, including by a move or overwrite, starts without a local sample.
        -- An exact guarded-entry report carries the journal's local origin marker.
        -- A physically bounded append records its exclusive journal cutoff in the entry. A sample at or above that cutoff can never vote, including during recovery.
        -- The sealed acceptance stage carries the journal cutoff and the digest of the exact receiver clock certificate together, or omits both.
        -- A present journal cutoff is positive and at least the stage cutoff, and its digest has 32 bytes with at least one nonzero byte.
        -- The writer derives the guarded append's clock evidence from the sealed stage.
        -- Recovery preserves that evidence, and the exact-record audit accepts a frame only when its clock evidence equals the sealed stage's evidence.
        -- An original append carries no original entry digest.
        -- A resealed copy carries the original stream's digest of the exact appended entry.
        -- The raw record binds its original physical stream, distinct journal identities and quorum size across moves and archives.
        -- Current placement agreement establishes the exact retained entry.
        -- An original observation lookup addresses the original stream and position and requires the digest of its exact original entry.
        -- Each journal in the recorded original roster contributes its earliest positive original sample strictly below the recorded cutoff for that entry.
        -- The sync bound is the recorded write-quorum-th smallest of those per-journal samples, and is absent if fewer journals qualify.
        -- Its physical interpretation depends on the installed journal clock bounds.

    @invariant TimedAppendRequiresProviderSupport
        -- A request with a journal cutoff can stage an accepting record only if its installed provider can enforce timed original quorum durability during append and recovery.
        -- A funded DecisionClock acceptance commits the exact canonical admission and original certificate under the active writer's timed quorum.
        -- An Original funded appointment activation and each accepting terminal use the original timed quorum under the same retained receiver certificate.
        -- The activation uses a cutoff at or before the advertised reading, and the terminal intersects the retained bounds with any shorter final request and writer bounds.
        -- The managed appointment writer verifies the exact live placement and namespaced ClaimWriter against the durable prefix before admission and again before the effect-bearing append.
        -- The managed timed original journal cutoff is bounded by the appointment credential's exclusive expiry.
        -- The native stream verifies the proof against the stream, writer epoch, position, sealed payload digest and exclusive signed cutoff on append and replay.
        -- A writer-local staging sample carries only staging authority.

    @invariant GuardedTailRequiresOriginalQuorum
        -- A guarded append owns the pending tip in an identified, intersecting ensemble.
        -- Each original append requires this ensemble's activation in the same journal decision as the epoch and position checks.
        -- The owner first attests an exact quorum of original appends, then durably records its decision on a write quorum before release.
        -- Copies preserve the admission kind but supply no original reply or observation.
        -- A sealed tip can be retained on a durable commit witness for the exact record or on timely original observations from distinct journals of its recorded roster at its recorded write quorum.
        -- Moves seal the predecessor with its own quorum threshold.
        -- Before transferring a retained guarded tip, recovery durably records its commit on a quorum of the predecessor journals.
        -- Recovery drops the guarded tip only if the journals still compatible with a timely original vote cannot form that quorum.
        -- Trimmed holders, non-responding journals, and originals with missing samples remain possible voters.
        -- A journal proved to hold only an exact copy or an original with a late sample is excluded from the possible voters.
        -- If neither decision follows from the evidence, the successor remains closed and the outcome stays unknown.

    @invariant SealIsAPromise
        -- A seal at epoch e durably raises the server's promised epoch for the deployment to at least e, and returns the epoch it is synced at, its last lsn, and the epoch of every record above the deployment's trim point.
        -- Once write_quorum servers of a loglet have answered a seal at e, no append below e reaches a quorum of that loglet.
        -- The promise applies to appends already in flight when the seal was made durable.
        --   Any commit quorum and any seal quorum of one ensemble intersect.

    @invariant NothingCommittedIsLost
        -- Every record committed in a loglet is held by at least one server of any seal quorum of that loglet.
        -- Recovery can always locate a committed record from a seal quorum (RecoverTail).
        -- Certifying an unsettled guarded tip can also require journals outside that sealing quorum.

    @invariant ReadsAreDemultiplexed
        -- A read or subscription for one deployment is served from that deployment's own index and returns only its records.
        --   Records of many deployments share physical segments; no reader of one deployment reads another's.
        --   A server holds a deployment's index in memory and its records in the segments, so its memory grows with how many records it retains, not with their bytes.

    @invariant GroupCommitSpansDeployments
        -- One durable round on a log server carries the pending appends of every deployment its writers have sent, within group_commit_window.
        --   A deployment writing one small transaction shares its replication round with every other deployment on the same writer.

    @invariant IdleCostsNothing
        -- A deployment with no appends causes no log traffic: no heartbeat, no no-op, no timer.

    @invariant SealsAreAnnounced
        -- A holder confirms its epoch from a quorum of positive replies to requests sent during the confirmation round.
        -- A positive reply establishes that the server's promised and synced epochs were both equal to the holder's epoch when the server decided the request.
        -- An idle holder sends an epoch check that waits behind preceding journal work and writes no event of its own.
        -- A refusal reporting a higher epoch deposes the holder.

    @invariant ReadIndexUsesTheActiveLoglet
        -- Before writing, the holder activates a durable write quorum for the epoch, the member identities and the configured quorum size.
        -- A journal acknowledges activation only at its promised and synced epoch and records at most one ensemble for that epoch.
        -- A higher promise revokes the activation.
        -- A positive reply requires that activation and binds the fresh invocation nonce to the greatest installed decision in the same local order.
        -- The reader selects the greatest compatible decision from an intersecting quorum of distinct positive replies.
        -- The selected cut must have quorum installation before exposure. The reader can complete installation of its exact certificate.
        -- An installer already ahead can attest a verified extension without changing the reader's selection.
        -- The admitted read index binds the stream, history, epoch, ensemble, cut and exact prefix digest.
        -- A serving attachment acquires replay protection and waits for same-history application through the cut before capturing that exact snapshot.
        -- Fresh basis capture retains protection through execution and transfer. Raw staged tips do not constrain selection.

    @invariant BrokeredDetachedBodyUsesTheCommittedPosition
        -- A sealed directory journal frame commits the digest of its detached reference.
        -- Its publication identity selects the original key slot and the seal authenticates the reference digest.
        -- The broker admits one position under a current replay ticket and retains its own directory region through delivery and provider drain.
        -- It matches the supplied reference to the quorum-certified frame and verifies the exact provider publication and body digest.
        -- The detached storage namespace and unit-key vault belong to the broker.
        -- A cross-cell successor reseals detached bodies under its broker and binds the returned publication into the new committed frame.
        -- The broker grants a body version after the directory fence covers its original position and matching provider uploads and readers have drained.
        -- Settlement withdraws the unit key and verifies absence of that provider version. Physical journal trim progresses independently.
        -- A writer reserves a detached publication before uploading and registers the exact pointer before enqueue.
        -- A prepared pointer retains its unread journal interval until a certified frame consumes it or a newer journal epoch excludes it.
        -- A transfer instead prepares one destination position under its original bootstrap or generation.
        -- The broker retains that publication until the destination certifies the pointer or the directory closes the destination and its admitted operations drain.
        -- No pointer can be issued for a draft once the original writer's admission is closed.
        -- The broker retires an excluded draft only after upload drain and records the exact provider grant before key withdrawal.

    @invariant TrimFollowsPublication
        -- A deployment's records at or below its checkpoint's lsn may be trimmed; nothing above it may be.
        -- The boundary must belong to this deployment's log, including when its first readable block came from a parent.
        -- A holder saves an agreed digest through the last position it intends to discard before requesting a trim.
        -- The directory advances the digest only when a durable write quorum independently computes the same committed history.
        -- A replica that cannot extend the saved digest across contiguous positions cannot contribute proof.
        -- A physical segment is reclaimed once every deployment's records in it are trimmed.
        -- A deployment whose tail pins a segment past log_max_retention is force-flushed (FlushOnRetention).
    -- The hosted directory path stores receipt L0 data and metadata as separate publications.
    -- Exact retirement of covered receipt tries and compacted primaries requires a certified recovery exclusion through the installed removal and an independent published recovery root.

    @invariant DroppedStreamsAreTerminal
        -- A drop at the deletion epoch durably removes the stream from the server's readable and writable state.
        -- Acknowledged and uncommitted entries are both released.
        -- A dropped stream remains closed after restart and journal compaction.
        -- An acknowledgement from every addressed replica is required before deletion advances.
        -- A copy records its source and destination endpoints before transferring bytes.
        -- Deletion addresses both the current ensemble and the recorded endpoints of earlier copy attempts.

    @invariant DurabilityIsConfiguredNotHoped
        -- Expected of the operator: each ensemble spans three availability zones, and a server acknowledges only after fsync.
        -- New assignments prefer distinct zones, and repair keeps surviving endpoints while using distinct zones for available slots.
}

contract ObjectStorage {
    -- What the cell requires of object storage, and how it uses it.
    put_if_absent: (credential: StorageCredential, key: String, bytes: Bytes) -> ObjectRef
    get: (key: String, from: Integer, to: Integer) -> Bytes
    delete_all_versions: (key: String) -> Integer
    inventory: (prefix: String) -> List<String>

    @invariant ObjectsAreImmutable
        -- Each published payload has an immutable identity.
        -- A terminal physical key accepts one payload and retains its tombstone after retirement until residence disposal.

    @invariant TerminalAllocationPolicy
        -- The provisioning authority binds the exact provider root and its lifetime storage policy before enrollment.
        -- The provider fences ordinary writes before activation and enforces create-once publication afterward.

    @invariant WritersCarryTheirEpoch
        -- Every object a holder writes is under prefix + "/e" + epoch, written with put-if-absent, using a StorageCredential scoped to that epoch's prefix.
        --   A deposed holder cannot overwrite a successor's object, and cannot write at all once its credential expires.

    @invariant OnlyTheReaperDeletes
        -- No node, gateway or worker holds a credential that can delete.
        -- The reaper deletes objects after a reachability release or a completed ordered-page retirement fence.
        -- Directory-managed exact removal consumes a grant obtained from the lineage revision authorizing the same retirement token and publications.

    @invariant VisibilityIsByReference
        -- An object has effect only once a committed log record or a published manifest references it.
        -- An object nothing references is an orphan, invisible to every reader, and reclaimed by the reaper.

    @invariant NoListingOnADataPath
        -- Recovery, activation, reads, compaction and garbage collection find objects through manifests.
        -- Listing (inventory) is used by the scrubber and by deletion proofs only.

    @invariant RequestsSpreadAcrossPartitions
        -- The hash at the head of every StorageRoot prefix spreads deployments over the store's request partitions.
        -- A deployment whose request rate exceeds one partition's adds a second hash level inside its prefix.
}

contract FencedStreamWriter {
    @invariant OrderedAuthority
        -- A node may attempt takeover if rendezvous ranking selects it and the previous writer released its lease or the lease has lapsed beyond the clock drift allowance.
        -- A writer that observes a new preferred node settles pending work and orders release against its current lease.
        -- No later record in the released epoch can change the applied state.
        -- A new stream with an empty committed prefix is eligible for its first seal.
        -- A candidate replays through the end returned by its seal before it publishes its lease.
        -- A journal record authenticates its writer epoch and position.
        -- A candidate that observes a competing seal without a committed lease waits a lease interval and the drift allowance before advancing that epoch.
        -- A writer stops admitting and acknowledging commits before its lease expires.
        -- A writer drops its allocation and in-flight authority when fenced.

    @invariant TimedTransactionSettlement
        -- An accepted timed transaction has a committed Settle record following its Tx record.
        -- The writer appends Settle only after the original durable acknowledgement arrives before the exclusive cutoff under the clock bound.
        -- Recovery appends Refuse for a pending timed transaction before admitting writes.
        -- A pending transaction has no visible effects.
        -- The writer may decide later requests against the tentative settled state while the original append is in flight.
        -- The terminal slot precedes every later append.
        -- Refuse returns the unsent suffix for redecision against the refused state.

    @invariant ActivationEffectAdmission
        -- A commissioned incarnation requires funded effect authority before contacting the receiver clock for a funded command.
        -- A commissioned incarnation requires derivative custody authority before staging or publishing a ranked catalogue.

    @invariant SpeculativeWriteSuffix
        -- The writer resolves ordinary requests in order against a private overlay of its durable prefix and staged writes.
        -- An ordinary transaction has one journal append.
        -- A reply that depends on staged writes follows their durable acknowledgements.
        -- A rejected append withdraws the dependent unacknowledged suffix.
        -- A writer that still holds its epoch recovers the committed suffix before resuming at the next journal position.
        -- Receipt identity includes both the receipt namespace and the idempotency key.
        -- Reads and status lookups use the committed fold.

    @invariant CheckpointRecovery
        -- A checkpoint record names its cut, the last position its image covers, at or before the record itself.
        -- A writer trims only through the cut of a committed checkpoint record.
        -- A classic checkpoint's cut is the position immediately before its record; a deferred checkpoint's cut is the head when the writer captured it, and the records between the cut and the announcement stay retained.
        -- A deferred checkpoint is announced only under the epoch and key generation it was captured in, with no erasure, rotation or restore started since the capture.
        -- A checkpoint image carries the applied transaction cut, active pins and the publishing writer's lease.
        -- The base of a retained suffix is the first retained checkpoint whose cut is the position before the floor; a suffix from position one has no base.
        -- A follower installs the base and applies every retained record from the floor in position order; the base's own record only announces it.
        -- That replay equals replay from position one, so followers, fresh processes and the writer agree.

    @invariant AssignedFollowerReplay
        -- An installed follower process opens a prepared, ready incarnation under one cell assignment.
        -- The process replays the selected journal and stays outside writer election and writer recovery through local teardown.

    @invariant AppliedServingCut
        -- A linearizable query captures a fresh journal head and reads the applied branch view at that prefix.
        -- A tracked query authenticates the branch, layer and applied basis used by the query.
        -- Changes reports only settled effects, with coverage derived from the retained journal interval.
}

contract EpochScopedStorage {
    @invariant UniqueObjectCreation
        -- Each database writer epoch has one allocation sequence for files and manifests.
        -- In default mode the epoch is the log processor's leader term.
        -- A writer restart acquires a higher epoch before allocating.
        -- Each creation has its own key, and the digest verifies the ciphertext read at that key.

    @invariant CommittedReachability
        -- A checkpoint publishes its complete file set and carries the active pins at its cut.
        -- A ranked catalogue record roots its manifest and complete immutable file set for as long as the record is retained.
        -- The record binds the writer epoch, database incarnation, applied basis, system clock and storage key generation.
        -- An export or cross-deployment fork is acknowledged after its source pin commits.
        -- The source pin covers the exact manifest and files until ordered release.
        -- A pin deadline closes admission, and admitted work drains before Unpin commits.
        -- An in-database branch's base history remains in the current checkpoint's file set.

    @invariant RankedAdmissionClosure
        -- The original object provider orders each ranked publication and admission closure on the same conditional scope root.
        -- The scope binds the commissioned activation identity and source incarnation.
        -- A newer writer epoch drains the recorded previous upload before reserving its own.
        -- The writer projects a ranked catalogue into the source journal only after the original provider admits its exact manifest.
        -- A prepared manifest binds a cut and writer epoch; retries under that epoch reuse it, and a replacement epoch may rebuild the cut.
        -- A published sequence slot retains the exact catalogue and remains discoverable without its operation ID.
        -- Closure is irreversible and exposes the final immutable inventory through sequence pages.
        -- A closure receipt requires the digest of every committed sequence slot in order to match the closed provider ledger.
        -- A staged ranked object has a provider upload recorded against the scope before payload submission.
        -- The original provider confirms that any recorded upload has drained before the custodian returns a closure receipt.
        -- The closure receipt also binds the complete inventory of staged versions, with each version's exact build cut and key generation.
        -- A recorded upload that the provider completes appears in that inventory even when its reply or catalogue admission is lost.
        -- Outcome pages expose staged versions only after their inventory matches the receipt.
        -- A staged slot outside the committed prefix grants no admission.
        -- An exact retry observes its original sequence even after closure.
        -- The activation broker commissions an exact original provider allocation against the active binding.
        -- Recovery freezes the predecessor's grants before obtaining closure from that allocation.
        -- Publication requires the durable closure of each commissioned predecessor ranked scope.
        -- Replacing the pending operation preserves the predecessor's closure; publication retains it for outcome discovery.
        -- The broker discovers closed outcomes by the exact predecessor binding, including dispositions preserved across later recoveries.
        -- The portable checkpoint moves database state and ordinary file roots; the successor rebuilds ranked catalogues.

    @invariant PermanentUnreachability
        -- In Multi mode the writer captures all retained records through a committed head after sealing.
        -- In default mode the confirmed leader supplies the retained checkpoint, replica records, pins and inputs of admitted reads and compaction under its term.
        -- The collector deletes database objects from earlier epochs outside the closure of the retained records, checkpoints and pins.
        -- After a certified trim through a checkpoint covering the authorized store floor, the Multi writer may also delete the exact manifests of superseded checkpoints outside that closure.
        -- A replica that raced the trim loads the replacement base and applies its retained suffix.
        -- Publication inherits earlier-epoch references from the current committed closure and introduces new objects from the current epoch.
        -- A delayed delete targets the same key and that key stays unreachable after handoff.
        -- An incomplete reference closure defers collection.

    @invariant TimedWriteDependencies
        -- A timed Tx holds its storage dependencies while its record is retained.
        -- Settle and Refuse refer to the original Tx by journal position and introduce no new storage dependencies.

    @invariant KeyGenerationErasure
        -- A file has an independent data key wrapped under the database key generation.
        -- Journal payloads are encrypted under a database key generation before append.
        -- The incarnation's claims and page continuations use a separate token key wrapped by the same generation custody.
        -- Page resumption preserves its generation and its retirement deadline on every route.
        -- Erasure publishes a clean checkpoint recoverable under the next generation and then commits rotation at its cut.
        -- A clean checkpoint drops the previous generation's ranked catalogue and its derivative roots.
        -- The writer arms the retiring generation's key-grant deadline and trims to the clean checkpoint.
        -- Key custody refuses old-generation delivery at the deadline and destroys the retiring generation once the deadline has elapsed.
        -- A replacement resumes an incomplete rotation from the adjacent checkpoint and rotation records.
        -- The original durable rotation time fixes the deadline across retries and writer replacement.
}

contract PortableApplicationBackup {
    @invariant HostedSourceExport
        -- A hosted export opens the commissioned source by its exact incarnation and reads its original journal, object allocation and generation keys.
        -- It records the observed cut in the encrypted archive without acquiring a source writer session.
        -- Hosted export, staging and attestation use the selected database name in their commissioning scope.

    @invariant AuthenticatedCut
        -- The writer captures one settled applied cut and binds its source incarnation, basis, system time and journal position to the encrypted checkpoint.
        -- An immutable stream can capture its settled application state at a quorum-confirmed head with read authority and without provisioning any source streams.
        -- The checkpoint contains its inline application state and exact retained receipt index.
        -- A portable cut requires ordinary receipt and row custody state and a closed export-pin inventory.
        -- The archive authenticates each ordinary checkpoint file under its exact source object identity.
        -- The writer reads each file from the current generation's custody and verifies its stored identity before capture.
        -- Publication follows durable archive creation under the source generation's independently available key custody.

    @invariant IndependentInstallation
        -- The destination verifies the archive with independent key custody and requires that generation to remain readable through checkpoint publication.
        -- A fresh destination installs the authenticated cut under a fresh incarnation and retains its source basis, source journal position and archive digest.
        -- The destination seals each captured file under a new destination identity and includes the relocation index and every relocated file in its committed checkpoint closure.
        -- Later checkpoints retain the installed file closure.
        -- Installation readback reopens the selected replay checkpoint from its backing store and verifies the installed relocation index and every relocated file named by that checkpoint.
        -- A hosted object-hold check binds the commissioned staged checkpoint, every direct member and the token key envelope to the provider versions that supplied their authenticated ciphertext.
        -- The hosted attestor matches the selected checkpoint announcement and journal head to the authenticated object readback.
        -- The original journal quorum retains that exact checkpoint announcement before the attestor issues the installation proof.
        -- The activation broker confirms the retained checkpoint through the registered destination reader and records the signed object inventory digest.
        -- An enrolled hosted recovery cannot fall back to an installation without object, journal and key custody.
        -- The check requires an affirmative provider hold for each of those versions and the same staged journal cut at readback.
        -- A saved object readback selects only its recorded physical versions when reopening that staged cut.
        -- Reopening verifies the same archive, incarnation, journal head, checkpoint and complete object inventory and rereads each provider hold.
        -- The retained destination journal selects the checkpoint used to recover the installation receipt.
        -- Installation and activation are separate decisions.
        -- An independent destination verifier checks its scoped committed install and dependency closure before signing the authority, database, predecessor binding, operation, destination cell, candidate, archive digest and exact source cut.
        -- Verification at the authority uses the registered destination public key and the selected recovery intent. It can complete without reading destination resources.
        -- Destination custody retains the staged journal, objects and keys until activation or an authority decision that supersedes the recovery intent.
        -- An active hosted candidate keeps its temporary object and generation holds until its original journal selects a later serving checkpoint covering the staged announcement.

    @invariant HostedStaging
        -- The independent activation journal supplies the pending recovery and its destination cell and incarnation.
        -- The archive identifies the recovery predecessor and, for a lossless recovery, the sealed final source journal position.
        -- The destination key provider retains the exact generation for the authority connection, operation and installation proof before the staging writer publishes relocated files or the checkpoint.
        -- The staged installation receipt requires both the original active key hold and the pending recovery to remain observable after checkpoint publication.
        -- A retry of the installed archive verifies the original hold and reopens the checkpoint without advancing the destination journal.
        -- A combined object and key readback requires the original active key hold on the generation of the authenticated checkpoint.
        -- The object custodian starts acquisition under the original pending recovery and active generation hold.
        -- Acquisition uses the S3 allocation recorded by the staged installation receipt.
        -- The custodian records one exact operation-exclusive provider inventory before placing any object holds.
        -- Acquisition retries use that same inventory even when a serving name resolves to a different version.
        -- Acquisition confirms each hold with the provider and reopens the recorded inventory before returning its verified readback.
        -- The activation operator completes acquisition and preserves its signed installation proof before recording the staged installation.
        -- A hosted attestor requires the staged serving names to resolve to the recorded physical versions.
        -- The attestor rechecks each provider hold and the original key hold before signing.
        -- The attestor checks the exact pending recovery through the activation connection enrolled in that key hold.
        -- The resulting installation proof signs the digest of the verified object readback and identifies the original key hold for settlement.
        -- Destination object custody preserves serving-name visibility of the held versions through handoff.

    @invariant HostedAbandonment
        -- The activation broker proves abandonment at the original binding before authorizing settlement.
        -- The staging writer records the exact intent, destination journal and vault before creating a stream or allocating its generation.
        -- Each tracked staging upload is ordered at the original allocation before it receives payload bytes.
        -- Closing staging upload admission drains every recorded upload before the custodian verifies that the abandoned candidate's object and wrapped-key prefixes are empty.
        -- The original object allocation orders a saved acquisition plan and closure before acquisition on the same immutable record.
        -- Pre-acquisition closure retires the destination journal and permanently fences the original generation before a late staging writer can recreate it.
        -- Before replacing a recovery publication, the activation authority preserves the previous publication in an immutable record addressed by its predecessor binding.
        -- A retained publication identifies the original chosen recovery and settles the disposition of other candidates derived from the same predecessor.
        -- For an interrupted acquisition, the broker confirms the selected checkpoint with the custodian enrolled for the destination cell.
        -- Interrupted acquisition settlement obtains authorization from the activation broker on every attempt and uses the recorded version plan.
        -- The original custodian keeps the activation intent with the plan so settlement can discover it after the stage receipt is lost.
        -- Receipt-free discovery requires the broker to match the original object allocation enrolled for the destination cell.
        -- The original keeper records a terminal checkpoint custody disposition even when attestation never acquired its hold.
        -- The original object provider confirms every version in the interrupted acquisition's plan is absent before the vault releases its generation hold.
        -- A conditional tombstone keeps any older version hidden when the current version is retired.
        -- Signed installation settlement verifies the registered cell signature and exact signed inventory before releasing provider holds.
        -- Settlement retries use the same operation-exclusive version plan across provider restart.
        -- A published candidate's object custody requires a serving handoff before release.
        -- A published hosted candidate's keeper release matches the journal, generation and object custody recorded in its publication.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities
------------------------------------------------------------

entity Cell {
    id: CellId
    directory: Directory
    log: LogService
    members: Set<MemberRecord>
    -- The engine version a new deployment is pinned to, and the storage format it is created at.
    default_engine_pin: String
    current_format_version: Integer
    -- Derived: the deployments recorded in this cell's directory.
    deployments: Set<DeploymentRecord>

    @invariant WithinEnvelope
        -- A cell holds at most cell_max_deployments deployments and cell_max_nodes nodes.
        -- A cell at its envelope receives no new deployments; the global layer creates them in another cell.
}

entity MemberRecord {
    -- One process lifetime, as the directory records it.
    id: MemberId
    role: MemberRole
    address: String
    availability_zone: String
    engine_version: String
    capacity: LoadVector
    -- Which node pool it belongs to; meaningful for nodes only.
    pool: general | isolation | dedicated
    -- The member renews this every member_heartbeat while alive.
    lease_expires_at: Timestamp
    storage_namespaces: StorageNamespaces?
    state: MemberState
    -- Logkeepers only: present once a draining journal is certified free of custody, and kept once it is decommissioned.
    decommission: LogkeeperDecommission?
    -- Moves on with every change to state or decommission, and never with a lease renewal.
    eligibility_epoch: Integer

    transitions state {
        joining -> eligible      -- prewarmed and admitted by the controller
        joining -> dead          -- lease expired before admission
        eligible -> draining     -- the controller chose it for scale-in, rollout or replacement
        eligible -> dead         -- lease expired, or suspicion confirmed
        draining -> retired      -- holds nothing and serves nothing
        draining -> dead         -- lease expired while draining
        -- Retired and dead are terminal for compute members.
        -- A locked logkeeper journal may reacquire its member record and become eligible again.
        -- A draining logkeeper keeps serving its journal, renews over the drain, and reacquires its record as draining.
        -- The reaper leaves a draining logkeeper's record in place.
        -- A decommissioned logkeeper is retired, and its journal identity never reacquires a lease.
    }

    -- Derived: what it may hold or serve.
    may_hold: role = node and (state = eligible or state = draining)
    may_receive_placements: role = node and state = eligible
    -- Derived: whether a deployment record may newly name it.
    accepts_journal_references: role != log_server or decommission = null
}

value PlacementMove {
    request: String
    target: MemberId
    expires_at: Timestamp
}

value JournalPlacement {
    -- A durability-placement decision: the journals the deployment's next holder moves its log onto.
    operation: String
    destination: List<MemberId>
}

value EnsembleHandoff {
    -- The destination holds the source's retained history from first through through, and the checkpoint covers what precedes first.
    epoch: Epoch
    source: List<MemberId>
    destination: List<MemberId>
    first: Integer
    through: Integer
    checkpoint_boundary: Integer?
}

value JournalCopyRelease {
    -- A retained copy that left a live deployment's custody with nothing depending on it.
    journal: MemberId
    address: String
    incarnation: Integer
    -- The writer epoch at which the copy's stream was retired on the journal.
    epoch: Epoch
    journal_epoch: Integer
    handoff: EnsembleHandoff
    released_at: Timestamp
}

value DecommissionCertificate {
    -- No deployment record retained custody of this journal when the directory was read.
    journal: MemberId
    address: String
    -- A digest of every checked deployment's ID and version, with their count.
    deployments_checked: String
    certified_at: Timestamp
}

value LogkeeperDecommission {
    certificate: DecommissionCertificate
    -- Absent while only certified; set when the decommission retires the member.
    decommissioned_at: Timestamp?
}

value StorageNamespaces {
    objects: String
    keys: String
}

entity DeploymentRecord {
    -- One deployment, as the directory records it.
    -- About 300 bytes when parked.
    id: DeploymentId
    org: OrgId
    lineage: LineageId
    -- Bumped when a deployment is recreated under the same name, so the new one's objects never meet the old one's.
    incarnation: Integer
    -- The fork's parent and the basis it was forked at, absent for a lineage root.
    parent: DeploymentId?
    fork_basis: Basis?
    storage_root: StorageRoot
    storage_namespaces: StorageNamespaces?
    -- The KeyService key wrapping this deployment's object data keys.
    key_id: String
    lifecycle: Lifecycle
    deletion_observation: DeletionObservation?
    deletion_record: DeletionRecord?
    isolation_class: IsolationClass
    -- Members permitted when isolation_class is pinned or dedicated.
    pinned_members: Set<MemberId>
    -- An exact engine version for the holder and its compaction worker.
    engine_pin: String?
    format_version: Integer
    quotas: Quotas

    epoch: Epoch
    holder: MemberId?
    -- The chain of loglets whose records are above the checkpoint.
    -- Empty when the tail is sealed and flushed.
    loglets: List<Loglet>
    -- Cleared in the directory change that records the destination as the ensemble.
    journal_placement: JournalPlacement?
    -- Recorded in that same change: the handoff certificate's source, destination and copied interval.
    ensemble_handoff: EnsembleHandoff?
    -- Retained copies released from copy custody while the deployment lived.
    journal_releases: List<JournalCopyRelease>
    checkpoint: Checkpoint?
    replica_anchor: CertifiedReplicaPrefix?
    replica_replays: Set<ReplicaReplayHold>
    checkpoint_metadata_reads: Set<CheckpointMetadataRead>
    -- A holder confirms its epoch with a quorum for reads.
    linearizable_reads: Boolean
    -- How many ranks of the affinity order may serve reads.
    read_width: Integer
    -- Activations that ended in the holder crashing, within quarantine_window.
    activation_crashes: Integer
    -- A planned move in progress, naming its target and deadline.
    move_intent: PlacementMove?
    -- Whether producers may submit to an intake stream without waiting for a holder.
    intake_enabled: Boolean
    -- When a request last reached this deployment, maintained coarsely by the holder; orders the drainer.
    last_activity: Timestamp

    -- Derived: the affinity key, stable across epochs and residency.
    storage_identity: id.uuid + "/" + incarnation

    transitions lifecycle {
        live -> quarantined      -- activation_crashes reached quarantine_crashes
        quarantined -> live      -- an operator cleared it
        live -> moving           -- a cell move began
        moving -> live           -- the move was abandoned in the source cell
        moving -> deleted        -- the move completed; the source cell keeps a tombstone forwarding to the target
        live -> deleting
        quarantined -> deleting
        deleting -> deleted
        -- deleted is terminal.
    }

    -- Derived.
    -- A holder counts only while its member may hold; a dead member's placements are unheld without any write to them.
    effective_holder: holder when holder != null and members[holder].may_hold
    is_held: effective_holder != null
    active_move_target: move_intent.target when move_intent != null
        and now < move_intent.expires_at
        and members[move_intent.target].may_receive_placements
        and ((lifecycle = live and members[move_intent.target].pool = general)
             or (lifecycle = quarantined and members[move_intent.target].pool = isolation))
    tail_open: loglets != []
    is_parked: not is_held and not tail_open
}

entity Lineage {
    -- A root deployment and every fork descended from it within one organisation.
    id: LineageId
    org: OrgId
    -- Bumped by every fork and every deletion in the lineage; the reaper's deletions are conditional on it.
    version: Integer
    members: Set<DeploymentId>
}

value SealReply {
    server: MemberId
    loglet: Loglet
    synced_epoch: Epoch
    last_lsn: Lsn
    records: List<LogRecord>
}

external entity LiveIndex {
    -- live-index.allium's live index, opaque here.
}

entity Quotas {
    max_tx_per_second: Integer
    max_log_bytes_per_second: Integer
    max_object_requests_per_second: Integer
    max_stored_bytes: Integer
    max_query_memory_bytes: Integer
    cpu_weight: Integer
    max_connections: Integer
}

entity LogServerStream {
    -- One deployment's state on one log server.
    -- Exists only while the server holds a loglet of the deployment above its trim point.
    deployment: DeploymentId
    promised_epoch: Epoch
    -- The epoch whose writer last copied its whole history here; a partial copy by a later writer leaves it unchanged.
    synced_epoch: Epoch?
    last_lsn: Lsn
    commit_lsn: Lsn
    trimmed_through: Lsn
    -- Consecutive retained log positions grouped by their writer epoch.
    retained_epoch_runs: List<EpochRun>
    retained_bytes: Integer
}

rule AdmitJournalMutation {
    when: JournalMutationReceived(server, mutation)

    requires: PendingJournalCapacityAvailable(server, mutation)

    ensures:
        JournalCapacityReservedUntilCompletion(server, mutation)
        MutationMayBeDecided(mutation)
}

entity LogRecord {
    deployment: DeploymentId
    epoch: Epoch
    lsn: Lsn
    -- Hash of the previous record's hash and this record's payload, across epochs.
    -- The chain from a checkpoint's chain_hash is verifiable offline.
    prev_hash: String
    -- Encrypted under the key selected by the journal's custody authority.
    payload: ResolvedTx | EpochStart | BlockUploaded | TriesAdded | TriesReleased | Handoff | Parked
}

variant ResolvedTx : LogRecord {
    -- One resolved transaction, applied by every reader without re-resolution.
    tx_id: Integer
    system_time: Timestamp
    idempotency_key: IdempotencyKey
    -- committed or aborted; an aborted transaction is recorded so that its retry returns the same outcome.
    outcome: committed | aborted
}

variant EpochStart : LogRecord {
    -- The first record a holder appends in its epoch, after recovery.
    -- Everything before it in the log belongs to earlier epochs.
    holder: MemberId
}

variant BlockUploaded : LogRecord {
    -- A flush has written its pack and manifest; readers adopt the block at this record.
    manifest: ObjectRef
    boot: ObjectRef
    through_lsn: Lsn
}

variant TriesAdded : LogRecord {
    -- The holder adopts compaction outputs: from this record on they are live.
    outputs: List<ObjectRef>
    replaced: List<ObjectRef>
}

variant TriesReleased : LogRecord {
    -- Objects this deployment no longer references.
    -- Candidates for the reaper, which deletes them only once no manifest in the lineage references them.
    released: List<ObjectRef>
}

variant Handoff : LogRecord {
    -- The last record a holder appends before a planned move, naming the member that takes over.
    to: MemberId
}

variant Parked : LogRecord {
    -- The last record of a deployment going parked, after its final flush.
    final_lsn: Lsn
}

entity Holding {
    -- One node's holding of one deployment for one epoch.
    deployment: DeploymentRecord
    member: MemberId
    epoch: Epoch
    residency: Residency
    -- Bytes of committed records above the checkpoint held in the tail live index.
    tail_bytes: Integer
    -- Commit time of the oldest record above the checkpoint.
    oldest_unflushed_at: Timestamp?
    last_request_at: Timestamp
    resident_since: Timestamp
    -- Idempotency keys of transactions committed within idempotency_window, with their outcomes.
    recent_keys: Set<IdempotencyKey>
    -- The highest system time committed, carried across epochs through the tail and the checkpoint.
    last_system_time: Timestamp
    credential: StorageCredential
    load: LoadVector
    -- The log position this holding has applied through, and its basis.
    applied_lsn: Lsn
    applied_basis: Basis
    -- Rows above the checkpoint, for FlushOnRows.
    tail_rows: Integer
    -- The live index over the tail, absent while resident_idle.
    live_index: LiveIndex?
    -- The manifest this holding currently reads through.
    manifest: ObjectRef
    -- Seal replies gathered while sealing, consumed by RecoverTail.
    seal_replies: List<SealReply>
    -- Lifecycle within the epoch.
    state: claiming | sealing | recovering | opening | serving | handing_off | deposed

    transitions state {
        claiming -> sealing        -- cas won and an earlier loglet is open
        claiming -> opening        -- cas won and the tail was sealed and flushed
        claiming -> deposed        -- cas lost
        sealing -> recovering      -- a seal quorum answered for every open loglet
        sealing -> deposed         -- the directory moved past this epoch
        recovering -> opening      -- the recovered tail is replicated into this epoch's loglet
        recovering -> deposed
        opening -> serving         -- manifest loaded, tail replayed, EpochStart committed
        opening -> deposed
        serving -> handing_off     -- a move intent names another member and its lag is under handoff_lag_threshold
        serving -> deposed         -- an append was refused for a higher epoch, or the directory moved past this epoch
        handing_off -> deposed     -- Handoff committed
        -- deposed is terminal for the holding; the member may hold the deployment again only under a new epoch.
    }

    transitions residency {
        active -> resident_idle    -- idle deadline, admitted writes drained and checkpoint published
        resident_idle -> active    -- a request claimed a new epoch
        resident_idle -> parked    -- memory pressure chose it, after min_residency
        active -> parked           -- the member drains or is told to park, after a final flush
        -- parked ends the holding: Parked is committed and the holder is cleared.
    }
}

entity Subscription {
    -- A non-holder member serving reads for a deployment from a log-fed tail live index.
    -- Exists only while the deployment's read width admits the member's rank and its demand continues.
    deployment: DeploymentRecord
    member: MemberId
    applied_lsn: Lsn
    tail_bytes: Integer
}

entity Session {
    -- A client session, held by a gateway.
    -- Everything a session needs is reconstructible from the client, so a gateway is stateless in the sense that matters: losing one loses connections, never data.
    -- A stopping pgwire gateway admits no new connection and lets established sessions finish within its shutdown grace before closing them.
    gateway: MemberId
    deployment: DeploymentId
    capability: Capability
    -- Parameters, prepared statements, the transaction buffer up to commit.
    -- The pgwire gateway keeps mutable connection settings at idle command boundaries.
    -- A new backend restores the settings before preparing statements or running another command.
    state: Bytes
    -- The highest basis this session has been acknowledged or has read at.
    -- The pgwire gateway retains the backend's connection await token at idle command boundaries.
    -- A new backend restores that token before preparing statements or running another command.
    basis: Basis?
    freshness: ReadFreshness
    -- Present while a cursor is open; pins the session's reads to that member until the cursor closes.
    pinned_member: MemberId?
}

entity GatewayRoute {
    -- A gateway's cached view of one active deployment: its holder and epoch, pushed for the active set.
    -- A wrong view costs one redirect; a gateway never authorises a write by its view.
    -- A route to a running writer can check that member by ID before considering the eligible fleet.
    -- A gateway follows a redirect after the target confirms its serving endpoint and epoch.
    deployment: DeploymentId
    holder: MemberId?
    epoch: Epoch
    read_width: Integer
}

entity CompactionJob {
    -- One deterministic job, derived from a manifest.
    deployment: DeploymentId
    job_key: String
    -- Output names are fixed by the job, so duplicated work writes the same keys and put-if-absent makes it harmless.
    outputs: List<String>
    state: queued | running | written | adopted | abandoned
}

entity GarbageCandidate {
    -- An object released by some deployment's manifest, awaiting the reaper.
    object: ObjectRef
    lineage: LineageId
    released_at: Timestamp
}

value DeletionObservation {
    leases_expire_at: Timestamp?
    first_empty_at: Timestamp?
    second_empty_at: Timestamp?
}

entity DeletionRecord {
    -- A cell signs the inventory it observed and the key destruction it confirmed.
    deployment: DeploymentId
    epoch: Epoch
    storage_prefix: String
    namespaces: StorageNamespaces
    inventory_scope: current_objects | all_versions | all_versions_and_multipart_uploads
    key_destroyed_at: Timestamp
    prefix_empty_first_at: Timestamp
    prefix_empty_second_at: Timestamp?
    transferred_objects: Integer?
    signature: String
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Given
------------------------------------------------------------

given {
    cell: Cell
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: creation
------------------------------------------------------------

-- A hosting installer can supply the root identifier with the deployment name and organisation.
-- Repeating an installation of the same live authority returns its directory record.
-- A different identifier or organisation cannot take over an installed name.
rule CreateDeployment {
    -- Creation allocates identity and nothing else: no objects, no log state, no compute.
    when: CreateRequested(org, cell)

    requires: count(cell.deployments) < config.cell_max_deployments
    requires: IsStoragePathComponent(org.id)

    ensures:
        let id = DeploymentId.created()
        DeploymentRecord.created(
            id: id,
            org: org,
            lineage: LineageId(uuid: id.uuid),
            incarnation: 1,
            parent: null,
            fork_basis: null,
            storage_root: StorageRoot.created(deployment: id, org: org, lineage: LineageId(uuid: id.uuid), incarnation: 1),
            key_id: KeyCreated(context: id),
            lifecycle: live,
            isolation_class: shared,
            pinned_members: {},
            engine_pin: cell.default_engine_pin,
            format_version: cell.current_format_version,
            quotas: org.default_quotas,
            epoch: Epoch(value: 0),
            holder: null,
            loglets: [],
            checkpoint: null,
            read_width: 1,
            activation_crashes: 0,
            move_intent: null
        )
        CellMapRowWritten(deployment: id, cell: cell.id)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: membership and liveness
--
-- Liveness is per member.
-- No rule here writes to any deployment record: a member's death unholds everything it held through effective_holder.
------------------------------------------------------------

rule RenewLease {
    when: HeartbeatSent(member)

    requires: member.state in {joining, eligible, draining}

    ensures:
        member.lease_expires_at = now + config.member_lease
}

rule ExpireLease {
    when: member: MemberRecord.lease_expires_at <= now

    requires: member.state in {joining, eligible, draining}

    ensures:
        member.state = dead
}

rule ConfirmSuspicion {
    when: ServingProbeFailureObserved(member, endpoints, consecutive_failures)

    requires: member.state in {joining, eligible, draining}
    requires: consecutive_failures >= config.failed_serving_probe_rounds
    requires: endpoints = CurrentServingEndpoints(member)

    ensures:
        member.state = dead
}

rule ProbeMemberLiveness {
    -- A controller pass probes up to sixteen members at a time.
    -- A round succeeds when control answers and the Pgwire listener returns its serving probe response.
    -- During a rollout, the controller checks TCP reachability for a member without the serving probe.
    -- The serving probe is admitted separately from authenticated sessions.
    -- A missed probe during startup-admission overflow gives no failure evidence.
    -- A successful round or a change to either endpoint clears preceding failures.
    when: ControllerProbeDue(cell)

    ensures:
        LiveNodeEndpointsProbed(cell)
}

rule AdmitMember {
    when: MemberPrewarmed(member)

    requires: member.state = joining

    ensures:
        member.state = eligible
}

rule RetireDrainedMember {
    when: MemberEmpty(member)

    requires: member.state = draining
    requires: not exists h in Holdings: h.member = member.id and h.state != deposed
    requires: not exists s in Subscriptions: s.member = member.id

    ensures:
        member.state = retired
        ProcessStopped(member: member)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: becoming the holder
--
-- First activation, failover, reactivation and the target side of a planned move are one protocol: claim, seal, recover, open.
------------------------------------------------------------

rule Claim {
    -- A member takes a deployment under the next epoch.
    -- Triggered by a request the gateway routed to it, by the drainer sweeping open tails, or by a move intent naming it.
    -- A general node claims live deployments, and an isolation node claims quarantined deployments.
    -- A move reserves a placement only while its target is eligible in the deployment's pool.
    when: ClaimAttempted(member, record, expected_epoch)

    requires: members[member].may_receive_placements
    requires: (record.lifecycle = live and members[member].pool = general)
              or (record.lifecycle = quarantined and members[member].pool = isolation)
    requires: record.active_move_target = null or record.active_move_target = member
    requires: EngineSatisfiesPin(members[member].engine_version, record.engine_pin)
    requires: record.isolation_class in {shared, isolation} or member in record.pinned_members
    requires: record.epoch = expected_epoch
    requires: not record.is_held or record.holder = member

    -- A recorded journal placement whose destination is live, over an ensemble that keeps a quorum, supplies the fresh loglet's journals.
    -- The holder then copies the retained history onto them under a handoff certificate before it appends.
    ensures:
        let e = Epoch(value: expected_epoch.value + 1)
        let fresh = Loglet.created(epoch: e, ensemble: InstalledEnsembleFor(record), start_lsn: NextLsn(record))
        Directory.cas(record.id, expected_epoch, change: {
            epoch: e,
            holder: member,
            loglets: record.loglets + [fresh],
            move_intent: null
        })
        Holding.created(
            deployment: record,
            member: member,
            epoch: e,
            residency: active,
            credential: StorageCredentialIssued(record.storage_root, e, config.credential_ttl),
            state: if record.tail_open: sealing else: opening
        )
}

rule ClaimLost {
    when: ClaimRefused(holding)

    requires: holding.state = claiming

    ensures:
        holding.state = deposed
        -- The gateway reads the record and redirects to whoever won.
        RedirectedTo(deployment: holding.deployment.id, holder: holding.deployment.holder)
}

rule SealPriorLoglets {
    -- Every loglet of an earlier epoch that is still above the checkpoint is sealed at the new epoch.
    when: holding: Holding.state becomes sealing

    ensures:
        for loglet in holding.deployment.loglets where loglet.epoch < holding.epoch:
            for server in loglet.ensemble:
                SealSent(server: server, deployment: holding.deployment.id, epoch: holding.epoch)
}

rule SealQuorumReached {
    when: SealRepliesReceived(holding, replies)

    requires: holding.state = sealing
    requires: for loglet in holding.deployment.loglets where loglet.epoch < holding.epoch:
                  count(replies where reply.loglet = loglet) >= config.write_quorum

    ensures:
        holding.state = recovering
}

rule RecoverTail {
    -- Take, per prior loglet, the reply greatest by (synced epoch, last lsn).
    --   Records keep the epoch they were written at, so a record's own epoch does not say whether its server holds a later writer's whole history; the synced epoch does.
    -- Its records above the checkpoint are the tail: every committed record is in it (NothingCommittedIsLost).
    -- Records a deposed holder put on a minority were never acknowledged; recovery may keep or drop them, and a client retry with the same idempotency key settles the outcome either way.
    -- Durable sync acknowledgements can confirm the recovered commit when they already report it on a write quorum.
    -- Opening waits for durable commit acknowledgements from a write quorum.
    -- A replica that does not finish recovery with the quorum can rejoin through background repair.
    when: holding: Holding.state becomes recovering

    ensures:
        let tail = GreatestBySyncedEpochThenLsn(holding.seal_replies)
        VerifyChain(from: holding.deployment.checkpoint.chain_hash, records: tail)
        ReplicatedUnderEpoch(records: tail, loglet: last(holding.deployment.loglets), epoch: holding.epoch)
        holding.state = opening
}

rule OpenHolding {
    when: holding: Holding.state becomes opening

    ensures:
        BootLoaded(holding.deployment.checkpoint.boot)
        TailReplayed(holding, from: holding.deployment.checkpoint.lsn)
        holding.recent_keys = KeysWithin(config.idempotency_window, checkpoint: holding.deployment.checkpoint, tail: holding.tail)
        holding.last_system_time = LastSystemTime(holding.deployment.checkpoint, holding.tail)
        AppendCommitted(holding, EpochStart.created(holder: holding.member))
        holding.state = serving
}

rule RefuseToLeadWithSkewedClock {
    -- A holder whose clock disagrees with the log servers' by more than system_time_skew_bound does not open.
    --   One bad clock would otherwise push a bitemporal timeline into the future for good.
    when: holding: Holding.state becomes opening

    requires: ClockSkew(holding.member, holding.deployment.loglets) > config.system_time_skew_bound

    ensures:
        holding.state = deposed
        ReleasedForReclaim(holding.deployment)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: committing
------------------------------------------------------------

rule SubmitThroughGateway {
    -- The gateway mints the idempotency key once per transaction and reuses it for every retry.
    when: CommitRequested(session, tx)

    ensures:
        let key = session.idempotency_key_for(tx)
        RoutedToHolder(session.deployment, tx, key)
}

rule AnswerRetryFromWindow {
    -- A stored retry identifies its original by key in its own deployment, as of the retry's system time.
    when: TxArrived(holding, tx, key)

    requires: holding.state = serving
    requires: key in holding.recent_keys

    ensures:
        Replied(tx, outcome: holding.recent_keys[key].outcome, basis: holding.recent_keys[key].basis)
}

rule ResolveAndAppend {
    -- The holder resolves against its state, pipelining later transactions against speculative state.
    -- An asynchronous submission returns an authenticated, opaque receipt for the deployment history, writer epoch, local sequence, principal and fresh nonce.
    -- A placed session accepts only receipts and causal tokens from the same deployment history and principal.
    -- The holder keeps intake records until their source positions are installed from the replica log.
    -- It releases the installed intake prefix independently of subsequent submissions.
    -- The resolved replica record carries the exact attempt identity.
    -- Only its durable applied outcome supplies a causal basis.
    -- Each ordinary Transact is exactly one appended record. It carries the admission witness: the attempt identity (idempotency key), the writer's admission time and a commitment to the submitted request bytes.
    -- Resolution never waits for durability. Decisions take consecutive positions; the driver appends them in position order and may group them into one journal request.
    -- A timed write reserves the next position for its Settle or Refuse; later decisions assume it settles and are appended only after that slot is filled.
    -- On Refuse, decisions that assumed the settlement were never appended and are decided again; their outcome is not unknown.
    when: TxArrived(holding, tx, key)

    requires: holding.state = serving
    requires: key not in holding.recent_keys
    requires: WithinQuota(holding.deployment.quotas, tx)

    ensures:
        let system_time = max(now, holding.last_system_time + 1.microseconds)
        let resolved = Resolver.resolve(holding.live_index, tx, system_time)
        AppendSent(holding, ResolvedTx.created(
            tx_id: resolved.tx_id,
            system_time: system_time,
            idempotency_key: key,
            outcome: resolved.outcome
        ))
}

rule ApplyOnCommit {
    -- The applied outcome and its basis are retained against the exact attempt through the receipt window, including at recovery.
    -- The indexed outcome witness is written in the same resolved delta as the transaction result.
    -- Its visible receipt window starts at the logged intake timestamp.
    -- The current writer can redeem an attempt once the installed prefix covers its intake sequence and the exact outcome witness is readable.
    -- To classify a missing outcome as unresolved, the serving attachment installs the recovered prefix that closes the attempt's writer epoch.
    -- A receipt expires one hour after its recorded intake time.
    -- Redemption exchanges the receipt for a causal token naming the installed replica prefix that contains the durable outcome.
    -- Reading at an implicit basis first installs at least the prefix named by the causal token.
    -- Replies are released only here, in position order: a commitment through a position releases every decision up to it and none after.
    when: AppendCommitted(holding, record: ResolvedTx)

    requires: holding.state = serving

    ensures:
        LiveIndexApplied(holding, record)
        holding.last_system_time = record.system_time
        holding.recent_keys = holding.recent_keys + {record.idempotency_key}
        holding.tail_bytes = holding.tail_bytes + record.size
        Replied(record.tx, outcome: record.outcome, basis: Basis(epoch: holding.epoch, lsn: record.lsn))
}

rule DeposeOnRefusedAppend {
    -- A refusal citing a higher promised epoch means a successor has sealed this holder's loglet.
    -- Speculative state is discarded and queued work is forwarded; nothing unacknowledged was ever visible.
    -- Withdrawal removes exactly the unacknowledged suffix of decision order, so no acknowledged decision depends on a withdrawn one; its callers learn the outcome by TxStatus.
    when: AppendRefused(holding, promised)

    requires: promised > holding.epoch

    ensures:
        SpeculativeStateDiscarded(holding)
        QueuedWorkForwarded(holding, to: Directory.read(holding.deployment.id).holder)
        holding.state = deposed
}

rule RejectOverQuota {
    when: TxArrived(holding, tx, key)

    requires: not WithinQuota(holding.deployment.quotas, tx)

    ensures:
        Replied(tx, outcome: throttled)
}

rule AuditRetainedReplicaHistory {
    when: RetainedHistoryAuditRequested(deployment)

    ensures:
        let samples = RespondingReplicas(deployment)
        ReplicaWindowsReported(samples)
        let interval = LatestCommittedRetainedIntervalSharedByQuorum(samples)
        if interval != null:
            let chains = Sha256ChainsForInterval(SamplesCovering(samples, interval), interval)
            ReplicaDigestsReported(chains, interval)
            if DistinctDigests(chains):
                ReplicaDivergenceReported(deployment, interval)
        if StrictAuditRequested(deployment) and not WriteQuorumCompared(deployment, interval):
            AuditRejectedForInsufficientEvidence(deployment)
        if SavedHistoryAnchor(deployment) != null:
            HistoryAnchorReported(deployment)
            if ContiguousSuffixVerifiedByDurableQuorum(deployment):
                ContinuedHistoryDigestReported(deployment)
}

rule VerifyRetainedJournalOffline {
    -- A saved digest for the same stream can supply the chain through the position immediately before the requested interval.
    when: JournalVerificationRequested(logkeeper, stream, interval, expected_digest)

    requires: logkeeper.stopped

    ensures:
        let digest = Sha256ChainForJournalInterval(logkeeper, stream, interval)
        if digest = expected_digest:
            JournalIntervalVerified(logkeeper, stream, interval)
        else:
            JournalMismatchReported(logkeeper, stream, interval)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: optional intake
--
-- A deployment may enable an intake stream for producers that submit and do not wait: bulk loads, CDC, external sources.
-- The intake is an ordinary deployment-scoped stream on the log servers, appended without a holder.
------------------------------------------------------------

rule AppendToIntake {
    when: IntakeSubmitted(deployment, tx, key)

    requires: deployment.intake_enabled

    ensures:
        IntakeAppended(deployment, tx, key)
        if not deployment.is_held:
            ActivationRequested(deployment)
}

rule ConsumeIntake {
    -- The holder resolves intake transactions in intake order, interleaved with routed ones, and records the intake position it has consumed in each ResolvedTx so a successor resumes exactly.
    when: IntakeRecordAvailable(holding, tx, key)

    requires: holding.state = serving

    ensures:
        TxArrived(holding, tx, key)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: flushing
--
-- A flush writes one pack and one manifest under the holder's epoch, commits BlockUploaded, then publishes the checkpoint.
-- The log record is the commit point for readers; the directory checkpoint is the commit point for trimming and cold start.
-- A timestamp fork admits the checkpoint and takes an active-loglet read bound under the same replay hold.
-- It applies the source term fence and waits for contiguous committed delivery up to the bound or an admitted transaction later than the requested time.
-- The fork validates the source frame before removing source-scoped placed outcome and strict acceptance evidence from its new log.
--
-- An owned-point checkpoint world reads one exact transaction from an accepted independent capture.
-- Its locator binds the deployment, capture, manifest digest and complete transaction key.
-- Opening a point read requires a published root digest and an admission in the capture's original retention inventory.
-- The admission holds until the query cursor and its archive I/O drain.
-- The read loads only the checkpoint closure, with no replay of the serving tail.
-- A writable checkpoint world durably pins that capture before ordering its owner-scoped registration.
-- A held child of Root starts at the exact archived Root publication, even if Root has since advanced.
-- A child at its parent's retained Root publication may start its own Held writer at the inherited reading.
-- Its independent pin owns the same Root media and its registry entry binds the original parent registration prefix.
-- The child's registration, its application ownership custody, the request receipt and any Root facts order together.
-- A world opens against an authenticated attachment when the durable pin and ordered registration name the same cut and remain live.
-- Child writes are confined to one world, and the ordered resolver checks that world's expiry before applying any writes.
-- A fork records its owner's declared relation schemas from the exact archived parent.
-- The retention pin binds their digest, and the ordered resolver checks local facts against the retained schemas.
-- A local replacement or tombstone covers only its written half-open business-valid interval.
-- A world point checks the complete local identity at the requested valid instant.
-- The point carries the selected Arrow fields and writes the winning concrete value type for each returned cell.
-- Compatible nested fields write compact values from the winning Arrow positions.
-- The point preserves the distinctions between an absent child, a present null, and a present value.
-- The reply uses the selected document's retained-value size for admission and validates the reached decimal coefficients before encoding.
-- Held search reads text from the selected shadow field after interpreting its field-presence marker.
-- Held validity windows read the timestamp vectors at the admitted coordinates.
-- If no local version exists at that instant the point reads its archived parent at the same valid instant.
-- A retained point view of an exact Held publication independently pins its original Root archive and the certified Held overlay.
-- A child of that retained view pins the same Root and Held positions under its own retention.
-- Ordering checks the immediate source's standing and unchanged archive binding.
-- The ordered registration checks the source Held generation and binds both prepared positions to the new child.
-- A provisional fork pin belongs to its original directory reservation in one incarnation and log epoch.
-- Release on ordered refusal requires confirmation of the original transaction in the same history and an exact directory comparison.
-- A committed fork alternative determines registration at its original source coordinate. An inconsistent read leaves the provisional pin held.
-- A point on the retained child reads the archived Held replacement or tombstone first and consults the pinned Root only for an unshadowed identity.
-- When the winning event carries an assertion ordinal, the point reply carries it with the winning line and event revision.
-- For a positioned Held winner, the point certifies the clock published with that event.
-- Point release validates the child's registration and the current Root purge frontier.
-- An installed live writer can read one exact opaque carrier from its earlier Root parent.
-- A nested hold certifies the frozen edge for the opaque carrier and its journal.
-- An exact frozen overlay publication certifies its Held clock and the physical author of its positive winner.
-- The held predecessor certifies the publication under the original pin and relation declarations, and point release checks current purge coverage.
-- A landed Root carrier opens only against its exact protected journal assertion and current original family authority.
-- A validation carrying an exclusive clock limit checks its exact world and pin at ordering.
-- Held validation compares the accepted Held clock, Proposal validation compares the ordered present, and retained validation compares the fixed inherited clock.
-- One hosted validation can combine covered Root points and ranges, held world witnesses and individual world clock bounds. Each world remains bound to its original pin and placement.
-- Ordinary world publications may include independent read-only worlds under the same ordered guard. Data witnesses and clocks of the observers are checked before any publication is applied.
-- Every publication carries the original held parent pin. An ordinary overlay or explicit clock advance can order from that standing and its expected clock without reading a user fact.
-- A held reading may exceed the physical publication timestamp.
-- A held world read exposes its last local clock publication with both the physical revision and logical reading from the original held catalog.
-- A timed overlay orders a strictly increasing held reading and the overlay facts together.
-- Acceptance-valid overlay facts begin at that publication's held reading.
-- A held create checks absence in the local line and its fixed parent at the held tip.
-- The ordered resolver validates the tip and the original absence witness before creating the row.
-- Acceptance-valid capacity and unique occupancy checks use that same held reading.
-- The physical timestamp bounds archive access and retention leases, and the held reading governs the world's effective time.
-- A valid-time window on the child combines boundaries from both retained cuts and resolves each slice by the same point rule.
-- The child pages the merged identities of both cuts, applying the same tombstone rule before emitting a candidate.
-- The child seeks both retained orders for a declared path only when its definition agrees across the two cuts.
-- The owner merges only the covered prefix and resolves the winning point for each ordered candidate.
-- An exact count starts with the original Root order total and corrects membership for every bounded Held shadow, including tombstones.
-- Text ranking reads the original Root corpus from covered search artifacts or a bounded projection at the same admitted cut.
-- Covered text ranking verifies complete identities and revisions in projected batches of candidates at that cut.
-- The corpus masks each replaced or withdrawn identity with its retained Held shadow before computing statistics.
-- Live Proposal search overlays the installed writer's bounded staged changes before corpus statistics and binds the staged corpus to its continuation.
-- A retained Held child restored from an Original archive retains the separately certified destination Root and Held overlay positions before world readmission.
-- The Held overlay archive keeps its original world, pin, logical publication and source Root coordinate even when the source Held world is dropped or its Root pin is relocated.
-- An explicit retained Root position uses that original Root archive under the child's frame and exact schema and purge guards.
-- Held overlay assertion history reads only exact accepted journal groups in the bounded archived region and resolves them against the same pinned Root.
-- A change journal touch for a positioned owned event carries that event's ordinal within its publication.
-- Ordered history opens the two images around the same accepted Held group and checks that the requested path generation covers both cuts.
-- An earlier effective read uses a certified Held clock publication inside the retained overlay cap and the same pinned Root.
-- A retained descendant can select that earlier publication while independently retaining the same physical archive suffix.
-- All descendant reads use the selected publication as their upper system bound. Original restore relocates the archive end and separately certifies the selected clock.
-- A retained child's own change journal starts at its inherited Held overlay cut and advances through its covered local groups or an issued present.
-- Dropping the source line leaves both independently retained positions available to the child.
-- A complete valid window includes covered absence across both sources.
-- An authenticated placed hold can witness the completed window under its original archive pin and the selected relation's local event and Root purge frontiers.
-- The same placed writer can submit those read conditions with a Root publication.
-- The ordered resolver checks each live world registration and its witness before publishing the Root effects.
-- A hosted Flight upload hands a bounded complete effect schedule and its original guards to one ordered decision.
-- The owner begins that decision after the upload seal and clean stream end.
-- The ordered resolver checks the exact installed writer and custody when it decides between the primary and refusal effects.
-- Recovery after a lost response uses the original ordered attempt evidence.
-- A placed Root writer can put an acceptance-relative timestamp into an admitted binary field.
-- The ordered writer fills declared binary order keys from the same acceptance instant before validating uniqueness and capacity.
-- On final native uniqueness or capacity refusal, a placed Root owner may settle the original registered dispatch in the same transaction.
-- The selected refusal publishes its exact receipt and marker using the original guards, deadline and acceptance reading.
-- No tentative application facts survive the refusal.
-- The ordered resolver fills each disjoint slot from the transaction reading and checks the completed row against its declared schema.
-- A binary slot for the system revision uses the assigned timestamp in nanoseconds. An acceptance-relative slot uses microseconds.
-- Committed events and archive replay carry filled values and require no slot metadata.
-- While a world is retained the archive retirement fence cannot close its capture.
-- A completed world-parent suffix remains available while its preparation lease, the held restore, or a retained world still holds it.
-- The reaper closes an expired suffix after its last world reference and its admitted provider regions have drained.
-- A closed suffix admits no more readers or copies, and another materialization uses a fresh identity.
-- Suffix retirement empties that suffix's provider inventory before releasing its Directory slot.
-- An initial retained fork at the original live Root key may share one ordered publication with checked Root writes and checked hosted controls.
-- A live hold may select its accepted application publication as the initial retained basis.
-- An initial Proposal may inherit that same application basis when its creation and the checked hosted settlement share one ordered attempt.
-- That Proposal has no initial local overlay. Root writes, when present, advance the Root head in the same transaction and leave the Proposal parent at the original publication.
-- A hosted bounded-period Proposal assertion carries the original complete-entity point at its finite valid start and a local timeline frontier for the same world relation and pin.
-- The ordered resolver admits one assertion or tombstone per complete entity key under the inherited bounded-period schema and settles its existing hosted controls with the overlay.
-- A reviewed Proposal landing checks the exact Root and Proposal points, writes the Root effects, and retires the Proposal in one ordered transaction.
-- A landing of one finite period replacement checks both points at its valid start and validates the Root relation and local Proposal timeline frontiers before ordering the reviewed extent.
-- The same order advances the Root head and the public table frontiers carried by the placed writer, and settles the replay head, landing receipt, and proof locator.
-- The placed owner may select the existing certified archive from its Directory and reopens it to compare the held transaction or its exact application publication before ordering.
-- A fork of the held application publication compares the same Root head in the ordered source transaction.
-- Archive admission precedes ordering, and the registration, Root head, writes, control replacements and one immutable attempt marker become visible at one transaction key.
-- A retained registration with checked hosted replacements and no Root fact writes leaves the Root head unchanged.
-- The placed replay inventory binds the new retained registration and exact Root relation set, and separately identifies the admitted hosted tables receiving replacements.
-- Early drop orders a terminal owner-scoped marker before the directory releases its capture pin.
-- An installed owner can order one read-only world drop with existing hosted owner-row replacements checked at the same cut.
-- The terminal registration and checked hosted replacements are bound to the same signed outcome and leave the Root head unchanged.
-- The terminal marker refuses later world opens and writes.
-- A retry may release the original pin after observing the same terminal marker.
------------------------------------------------------------

rule FlushOnTailBudget {
    when: holding: Holding.tail_bytes changes

    requires: holding.state = serving
    requires: holding.tail_bytes >= config.flush_tail_bytes or EstimatedReplay(holding) >= config.flush_replay_budget

    ensures:
        FlushStarted(holding)
}

rule FlushOnAge {
    when: holding: Holding.oldest_unflushed_at + config.flush_max_age <= now

    requires: holding.state = serving

    ensures:
        FlushStarted(holding)
}

rule FlushOnIdle {
    -- Each node bounds the idle flushes it starts in one reconciliation pass.
    when: holding: Holding.last_request_at + config.clean_after_idle <= now

    requires: holding.state = serving
    requires: holding.tail_bytes > 0

    ensures:
        FlushStarted(holding)
}

rule FlushOnDegradedEnsemble {
    -- A degraded ensemble restores durability through object storage rather than by re-replicating the log.
    when: EnsembleDegraded(holding, loglet)

    requires: holding.state = serving

    ensures:
        FlushStarted(holding)
}

rule FlushOnRetention {
    when: SegmentPinnedPastRetention(deployment)

    ensures:
        if deployment.is_held:
            FlushStarted(holdings[deployment])
        else:
            ActivationRequested(deployment, reason: flush)
}

rule FlushOnRows {
    when: holding: Holding.tail_rows changes

    requires: holding.tail_rows >= config.block_max_rows

    ensures:
        FlushStarted(holding)
}

rule WritePackAndManifest {
    -- A small flush writes one pack: every table's trie data, metadata and catalog delta, with a footer index, read by range.
    -- A large flush may write one pack per table.
    -- A versioned pack ties every ranged member read to the physical publication of its index.
    -- Recovery and checkpoint copy planning may verify multiple members against one fresh observation of their containing pack.
    -- A copy reads each member from the publication named by its plan.
    -- A moved pack is verified using the index of its destination publication.
    -- A reference transferred into a fork opens the copied pack through its own index and provider version.
    -- The boot object inlines catalogs, schema and the top trie levels, up to boot_inline_limit.
    -- A flush waiter returns a holder-lost result when its holding is deposed.
    when: FlushStarted(holding)

    ensures:
        let pack = ObjectStorage.put_if_absent(holding.credential, PackKey(holding), PackOf(holding.tail))
        let manifest = ObjectStorage.put_if_absent(holding.credential, ManifestKey(holding), ManifestOf(holding, pack))
        let boot = ObjectStorage.put_if_absent(holding.credential, BootKey(holding), BootOf(holding, manifest))
        AppendSent(holding, BlockUploaded.created(manifest: manifest, boot: boot, through_lsn: holding.applied_lsn))
        CompactionJobsEnqueued(CompactionPlanner.jobs(manifest))
}

rule PublishCheckpoint {
    -- An explicit flush returns after publishing its adopted checkpoint for the current holding.
    -- Publication is a cas conditional on the epoch, batched per node.
    -- A checkpointed store with an independent object inventory can recover its packs by name.
    -- The directory receipt binds the adopted logical root bytes to the published coordinate.
    -- A serving checkpoint commits to its replay inventory in the same directory update as its log boundary.
    -- The inventory is derived from the root and the verified table blocks.
    -- For a table with a complete semantic history directory, the serving inventory binds its directory pages and primary payloads and allows logical search runs and term-directory pages to be rebuilt after startup.
    -- Replay archives verify the complete set of recorded search dependencies when they capture a checkpoint.
    -- A receipt published with the full replay inventory is verified against that inventory.
    -- Each entry binds its logical path and length to the provider version where available.
    -- An unversioned entry uses the immutable-object contract.
    -- The receipt binds the journal epoch and the first replica record required after the checkpoint.
    -- Fresh storage observations must cover every member before the holder can publish.
    -- Park and idle suspension use the same publication rule.
    -- Ordinary startup of a receipted cut protects its root metadata and replay suffix in a single compare-and-replace.
    -- Startup verifies the root bytes against the receipt before following its table pointers.
    -- Startup verifies the inventory commitment under that admission before installing the cut.
    -- A transfer or repair that changes storage custody starts with its separately verified copy.
    -- Opening a replacement after a gap requires a fresh directory admission.
    when: AppendCommitted(holding, record: BlockUploaded)

    ensures:
        Directory.cas(holding.deployment.id, holding.epoch, change: {
            checkpoint: Checkpoint.created(
                manifest: record.manifest,
                boot: record.boot,
                lsn: record.through_lsn,
                chain_hash: record.hash,
                format_version: holding.deployment.format_version,
                recovery_manifest_digest: RecoveryInventoryDigest(holding, record)
            ),
            loglets: LogletsAbove(holding.deployment.loglets, record.through_lsn)
        })
        holding.tail_bytes = TailBytesAbove(holding, record.through_lsn)
        holding.oldest_unflushed_at = OldestAbove(holding, record.through_lsn)
}

rule TrimCertifiedReplicaPrefix {
    -- The prefix owner retains its authenticated journal unit census before trim and withdraws those slots through the original vault.
    -- The checkpoint's boundary record supplies the intake position on reopen.
    -- An active suffix pin retains its first required replica record until its verified archive publication is recorded or the pin expires.
    -- A separately captured suffix retains its capture until explicit archive retirement.
    -- A world-parent suffix protects its capture for the duration of its preparation lease, held restore, and retained world references.
    -- A copy admitted against a pending suffix retains its first required replica record until its provider region drains.
    -- Archive certification covers every replica offset and replay applies only the messages admitted by the checkpoint's term fence.
    -- A semantic history checkpoint retains the authenticated directory pages and the primary payload closure that its events can resolve by origin.
    -- The trim witness binds complete table directory roots to the exact published checkpoint inventory for tables requiring semantic history.
    -- Fenced, discarded and voided records survive a transfer at their original offsets and require no offloaded payload.
    -- Terminal certification and bootstrap preparation hold the same exact source replay interval through their provider reads.
    -- An admitted offloaded body supplies replay bytes after its digest matches the certified outer frame.
    -- The target holds each independently authenticated detached body under its own key before committing the page that names it.
    -- In-flight bootstraps issued in the inline format remain readable under their original ordered receipt.
    -- After transfer, a holder starts intake beyond the exact terminal source position and any possible source positions consumed after its paired replica offset.
    when: ReplicaPrefixCertified(holding, through, digest)

    requires: Directory.read(holding.deployment.id).checkpoint.recovery_manifest_digest != null
    requires: CheckpointHasRequiredSemanticHistory(holding.deployment)
    requires: through < Directory.read(holding.deployment.id).checkpoint.lsn
    requires: not exists replay in Directory.read(holding.deployment.id).replica_replays: replay.first_required <= through
    requires: ArchivedAndPendingSuffixesCoverTrim(holding.deployment, through)

    ensures:
        Directory.cas(holding.deployment.id, holding.epoch, change: {
            replica_anchor: CertifiedReplicaPrefix.created(through: through, digest: digest)
        })
        LogTrimmed(holding.deployment.id, through: through)
}

rule AdmitLatestCheckpointReplay {
    when: LatestCheckpointReplayRequested(deployment, after: Lsn, tip: Lsn, owner)

    ensures:
        let record = Directory.read(deployment.id)
        if record.replica_anchor != null and after < record.replica_anchor.through:
            RefreshCheckpointDiscovery(deployment, after: after)
        else:
            if after < tip:
                let replay = ReplicaReplayHold.created(id: FreshIdentity(), first_required: Successor(after), owner: owner)
                Directory.cas(deployment.id, record.version, change: {
                    replica_replays: record.replica_replays + {replay}
                })
            AdmitReplicaCursor(deployment, after: after)
}

rule ReleaseReplicaReplay {
    -- A close fences an ambiguous registration even when its hold is absent.
    when: ReplicaReplayReleaseRequested(deployment, replay)

    requires: ReplayCaughtUpOrDrained(replay) or ReplayOwnerConfirmedStopped(replay)

    ensures:
        let record = Directory.read(deployment.id)
        Directory.cas(deployment.id, record.version, change: {
            replica_replays: record.replica_replays - {replay}
        })
}

-- A serving cursor admits an offloaded delivery before exposing its pointer.
-- Startup and ordinary delivery use the same directory trim frontier.
-- The delivered pointer retains the replay hold until its provider read drains or the pointer is discarded.
-- An inline delivery after startup consumes no replay hold.
-- A replica payload release identifies the exact publication carried by a certified replica entry.
-- Provider deletion requires the directory to record trim completion for every journal in the authorized inventory.
-- A provider retry names the same publication until settlement is recorded.
-- A local serving attempt registers its slot before provisioning and retains its region through physical publication.
-- Local orphan withdrawal requires region drain and a loose and packed alias census under the lineage reservation.
-- A checkpoint fork references checkpoint objects and gives any replayed payload its own journal custody.
-- A shared hosted or local fork reserves custody membership against its exact directory preparation before copying.
-- Membership binds the inherited checkpoint and certified original suffix before directory publication.
-- The broker waits for directory publication or confirmed disposal of the rejected stage before closing domain membership.

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: compaction
--
-- compaction.allium decides the jobs.
-- Here: where they run and how their outputs take effect.
------------------------------------------------------------

rule RunCompactionJob {
    -- Workers are selected by affinity on (deployment, job_key), so a retry lands on the worker that has the inputs cached.
    -- A placed node shares its configured execution slots across all deployments.
    -- A cancelled job keeps its slot until its admitted writes have finished.
    when: job: CompactionJob.state becomes queued

    ensures:
        let worker = Affinity.select(key: job.deployment.uuid + "/" + job.job_key, pool: workers)
        JobDispatched(job, worker)
        job.state = running
}

rule WriteCompactionOutputs {
    when: JobFinished(job, worker)

    requires: job.deployment.lifecycle = live
    requires: WorkerTargetsCurrentStorage(worker, job.deployment)

    ensures:
        for output in job.outputs:
            ObjectStorage.put_if_absent(WorkerCredential(job), output, bytes)
        job.state = written
        OutputsOffered(job.deployment, job)
}

rule AdoptCompactionOutputs {
    -- Outputs take effect by a committed record, so correctness does not depend on two binaries producing identical bytes.
    when: OutputsOffered(deployment, job)

    requires: holdings[deployment].state = serving
    requires: InputsStillLive(holdings[deployment].manifest, job)

    ensures:
        AppendSent(holdings[deployment], TriesAdded.created(outputs: job.output_refs, replaced: job.input_refs))
        AppendSent(holdings[deployment], TriesReleased.created(released: job.input_refs))
        job.state = adopted
}

rule AbandonStaleCompaction {
    when: OutputsOffered(deployment, job)

    requires: not InputsStillLive(holdings[deployment].manifest, job)

    ensures:
        job.state = abandoned
        -- Its outputs are orphans; the reaper collects them.
}

rule MaintenanceClaim {
    -- A parked deployment with compaction debt is claimed at low priority by a worker-hosting node, which adopts the outputs and parks again.
    -- The claim is an ordinary epoch; client demand pre-empts it through an ordinary move.
    when: CompactionDebtFound(deployment)

    requires: deployment.is_parked

    ensures:
        ClaimAttempted(member: MaintenanceMember(deployment), record: deployment, expected_epoch: deployment.epoch)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: garbage and the reaper
------------------------------------------------------------

rule NoteReleased {
    when: AppendCommitted(holding, record: TriesReleased)

    ensures:
        for object in record.released:
            GarbageCandidate.created(object: object, lineage: holding.deployment.lineage, released_at: now)
}

rule AdmitCheckpointMetadata {
    when: StartupStoreRequested(deployment, requested_checkpoint)

    ensures:
        let record = Directory.read(deployment.id)
        if requested_checkpoint != null and requested_checkpoint != record.checkpoint:
            RefreshCheckpointRequest(deployment)
        else:
            let read = CheckpointMetadataRead.created(id: FreshIdentity(), first_block: PublishedBlockOrOrigin(record))
            Directory.cas(deployment.id, record.version, change: {
                checkpoint_metadata_reads: record.checkpoint_metadata_reads + {read}
            })
            StartupMayReadCheckpointMetadataAtOrAbove(read.first_block)
}

rule ReleaseCheckpointMetadata {
    when: StartupCheckpointReadsDrained(deployment, read)

    ensures:
        let record = Directory.read(deployment.id)
        Directory.cas(deployment.id, record.version, change: {
            checkpoint_metadata_reads: record.checkpoint_metadata_reads - {read}
        })
}

rule ReapUnreachable {
    -- An object is deleted only when no published manifest of any live deployment in its lineage references it, and reaper_grace has passed since it was released.
    -- Shared publications advance the lineage's reference revision.
    -- When the reaper cannot determine reachability, it keeps the affected release records for a later pass.
    -- Withdrawing an inherited reference removes only the selected name and owner from the latest manifest.
    -- The reaper confirms that withdrawal before deleting a local copy that shadows the reference.
    -- A failed physical removal keeps its release record for the next pass.
    -- A local sweep reserves the lineage against its current shared-reference revision after draining local preparation.
    -- Local path erasure is internal to the admitted maintenance task.
    -- Whole-residence erasure uses the prefix admitted by the directory's disposal permit.
    -- Local cleanup of a definitively rejected fork uses the unpublished fork's own prefix.
    -- The reservation advances the preparation generation and holds until the sweep's provider work drains.
    -- The sweep scans published referrers after reserving the lineage and copies retained objects before removing the source copies.
    -- A cell's remote object store admits physical deletion through provider retirement and inventory operations.
    -- A hosted path sweep withdraws eligible inherited references and preserves the provider payloads and release markers.
    -- Owned hosted payloads and markers wait for provider retirement authority.
    -- An S3 loose-file release with an installed publication witness records the exact provider version.
    -- An exact trie or replica payload release can settle as soon as its durable admission and recovery checks allow it.
    -- Path and checkpoint-metadata pack releases still use the grace below.
    -- A retired logical search run carries its provider publication and the installed catalog floor before a hosted release can be recorded.
    -- A directory page release records its observed immutable provider publication and installed catalog floor after fork and archive holds drain.
    -- Every indexed member of a hosted pack needs a mature release for the same publication.
    -- A versioned pack is erased by its exact version.
    -- An enrolled terminal pack is replaced by a conditional tombstone after an entity-tag-bound index read.
    -- The same terminal observation mechanism opens the successor root and checks its published digest before authorization.
    -- Only checkpoint metadata may be covered by this pack collector. A newer published pack must be present in the same store.
    -- The owner CAS checks the pack's block and the greatest covered metadata block against its current checkpoint and holds.
    -- An exact absence observation permits residual member release markers to settle while a replacement pack remains available.
    -- Hosted preparation for a fork, checkpoint capture or cell transfer holds its source lineage until provider work drains.
    when: candidate: GarbageCandidate.released_at + config.reaper_grace <= now

    requires: not exists d in LiveDeploymentsOf(candidate.lineage): candidate.object in ReferencedBy(d.checkpoint.manifest)
    requires: for block in CheckpointMetadataIn(candidate.object):
        block < PublishedBlockOrOrigin(OwnerOf(candidate.object))
        and not exists read in OwnerOf(candidate.object).checkpoint_metadata_reads: read.first_block <= block

    ensures:
        ObjectStorage.delete_all_versions(candidate.object.key)
        candidate.deleted = true
}

-- Original replay pack retirement runs during the local cell sweep or through the hosted provider inventory.
-- A parked root can retire an older original pack while it holds the exact current replay archive and the source lineage.
-- The reaper checks each carried member against the archived bytes and accepts checkpoint metadata for the pack's own older block.
-- The certified replica prefix passes the pack's boundary and ends before the published boundary.
-- Hosted serving readers admitted through that same archive can stay open if the authenticated archive supplies every payload member.
-- Other storage admissions, journal dependencies, and declared descendant references retain the original pack.
-- New serving admissions start at the published checkpoint and acquire the selected replay archive.
-- The hosted reaper binds the pack index and every member observation to the same physical publication.
-- It verifies the published successor root.
-- The directory fences new pack uploads and waits for earlier uploads of that exact key to drain.
-- Durable provider authority fences the exact pack and retains the capture through a reaper restart.
-- In a versioned bucket, the reaper first places a tombstone with the retirement identity as the current version using the selected pack ETag.
-- It reopens the lineage after confirming both the tombstone and absence of the exact old version.
-- An enrolled never-versioned bucket binds each read to the accepted strong entity tag.
-- The terminal collector retains a pack whose index exceeds its verification budget.
-- The reaper conditionally places a tombstone, drains its upload, and reopens the lineage after confirming its retirement token.
-- An unexpected provider observation after authorizing a terminal tombstone keeps the lineage fence and the archive hold.

rule WithdrawForkReferences {
    -- Each manifest update uses the successor of the sequence whose bytes it read.
    -- A fresh lookup confirms the current sequence and the missing selected references before retirement continues.
    -- Hosted withdrawal retains earlier slots.
    -- An admitted local sweep can prune slots below the confirmed head.
    when: ForkReferenceWithdrawalRequested(store, selected)

    ensures:
        let current = ConfirmCurrentReferenceManifest(store)
        let remaining = current.references - selected
        if remaining != current.references:
            WriteImmutableReferenceManifest(store, current.sequence + 1, remaining)
        if ConfirmedCurrentManifestExcludes(store, selected):
            ForkReferenceWithdrawalConfirmed(store, selected)
}

rule RetireOrderedIndexPages {
    -- An independently compacted index publishes its scrubbed replacement before closing earlier page scopes.
    -- The captured catalog closes dropped definitions, including prefixes that only exist in provider inventories.
    -- A new definition has its own physical name. The catalog retains the closure after its page inventory is removed.
    -- Serving replicas register a scope before retaining any decoded page or starting provider I/O.
    -- Each provider upload records its exact upload ID in the same directory authority that closes the scope.
    -- Closure ends new replica registrations and upload admissions. Registered replicas stop taking
    -- new work after seeing closure and release their permit only after their retained copies and I/O drain.
    when: OrderedPageScopeClosed(scope)

    requires:
        EveryServingPermitReleasedOrOwnerStopped(scope)
        and EveryAdmittedProviderUploadSettled(scope)

    ensures:
        GenerationKeyScopeWithdrawn(scope)
        EveryObjectVersionAndUploadRemoved(scope)
        EmptyProviderInventoryRecorded(scope)
}

rule ScrubOrphans {
    -- The scrubber diffs inventory against every manifest in a lineage and enqueues objects older than reaper_grace that nothing references: uploads of deposed holders, abandoned compaction outputs.
    when: ScrubDue(lineage)

    ensures:
        for key in Inventory(lineage) where not ReferencedByAnyManifest(lineage, key) and Age(key) > config.reaper_grace:
            GarbageCandidate.created(object: RefOf(key), lineage: lineage, released_at: now - config.reaper_grace)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: residency
------------------------------------------------------------

rule BecomeIdle {
    -- A configured node keeps the placement while releasing the running database and its watch.
    -- The directory retains the published checkpoint and the time of the last activity.
    -- A move reservation takes precedence over the idle transition.
    -- A Flight SQL call keeps the placement active until its stream closes or the caller cancels.
    -- Caller cancellation ends the wait while the admitted operation keeps those resources.
    when: ResidentIdleDeadlineReached(holding)

    requires: holding.residency = active

    ensures:
        AdmittedWritesApplied(holding)
        CheckpointAdopted(holding)
        WriterEpochAdvanced(holding.deployment)
        holding.residency = resident_idle
        HoldingResourcesReleased(holding)
}

rule WakeOnRequest {
    when: RequestArrived(holding)

    requires: holding.residency = resident_idle

    ensures:
        NewEpochClaimed(holding.deployment, holding.member)
        CheckpointRecovered(holding.deployment)
        holding.residency = active
        holding.last_request_at = now
}

rule ParkResidentIdle {
    -- An idle deadline or drain can release a retained placement from the directory.
    -- The node checks due deadlines in order, without reading the inventory of idle placements.
    -- A parking attempt from an older scan must still match the deployment and idle epoch.
    when: ResidentIdleParkRequested(holding)

    requires: holding.residency = resident_idle

    ensures:
        HolderReleased(holding.deployment)
        WriterEpochAdvanced(holding.deployment)
}

rule ParkUnderPressure {
    -- A node under memory pressure parks its least recently used resident-idle deployments.
    when: MemoryPressure(member)

    ensures:
        for holding in LeastRecentlyUsed(member, residency: resident_idle)
            where holding.resident_since + config.min_residency <= now:
            ParkStarted(holding)
}

rule ReclaimExecutionMemory {
    -- The placed node starts reclamation at 80 percent of its execution budget and targets 75 percent.
    -- It attempts a bounded batch of parks, ordered by last activity across its holdings.
    -- A holding with an open session can be selected under execution-memory pressure.
    -- A newly touched candidate or a candidate already undergoing a holding operation is skipped.
    -- A holding that has been open for less than the node’s configured minimum residency is skipped.
    -- A candidate that lost its placement is detached without flushing.
    -- On activation the node preserves the requested holding and checks headroom again before attempting a claim.
    -- Gateways retry temporary pressure refusals within an activation deadline.
    -- A planned handoff keeps its reservation while retrying a target's pressure refusal.
    when: ExecutionBudgetHigh(member)

    ensures:
        ReclamationPass(member)
}

rule Park {
    -- Parking pauses new submissions and waits for admitted transactions before the final flush.
    -- The holder publishes the final checkpoint in the same cas that clears the placement.
    when: ParkStarted(holding)

    ensures:
        if holding.tail_bytes > 0:
            FlushStarted(holding)
        AppendCommitted(holding, Parked.created(final_lsn: holding.applied_lsn))
        Directory.cas(holding.deployment.id, holding.epoch, change: {
            epoch: Epoch(value: holding.epoch.value + 1),
            holder: null,
            loglets: []
        })
        holding.state = deposed
}

rule ActivateOnDemand {
    -- A request for an unheld deployment claims it on the highest-ranked eligible, unpressured member.
    -- Activations are admitted at most activations_per_node_per_second per node; others queue at the gateway with a deadline, interactive before background.
    -- The local window covers one rolling second and a rate refusal leaves the placement unchanged.
    -- An existing reservation admits its handoff independently of the cold activation budget.
    when: ActivationRequested(deployment)

    requires: not deployment.is_held

    ensures:
        let member = Affinity.first_unpressured(key: deployment.storage_identity, pool: PoolFor(deployment))
        ClaimAttempted(member: member, record: deployment, expected_epoch: deployment.epoch)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: failover
------------------------------------------------------------

rule ReclaimOnDemand {
    -- A request for a deployment whose holder is dead claims it on the next eligible rank.
    -- Rendezvous order spreads a dead member's deployments over every survivor.
    when: RequestFailed(session, holder)

    requires: not members[holder].may_hold

    ensures:
        ActivationRequested(Directory.read(session.deployment))
}

rule DrainOrphanedTails {
    -- A dead member's deployments that have an open tail and no demand are claimed, flushed and parked by the drainer, most recently active first, at a bounded rate.
    -- Deployments with nothing unflushed need nothing.
    when: member: MemberRecord.state becomes dead

    ensures:
        for record in DeploymentsHeldBy(member) where record.tail_open, ordered by record.last_activity desc:
            DrainScheduled(record)
}

rule CountActivationCrash {
    -- Only the current holder's epoch contributes an activation crash.
    -- An unavailable log quorum can release a holding without moving the deployment to isolation.
    when: HolderCrashedDuringOrSoonAfterActivation(record)

    ensures:
        record.activation_crashes = record.activation_crashes + 1
        if record.activation_crashes >= config.quarantine_crashes:
            Directory.cas(record.id, record.epoch, change: { lifecycle: quarantined, isolation_class: isolation })
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: planned handoff
--
-- Rebalancing, scale-in and engine rollout are all planned handoffs.
------------------------------------------------------------

-- A prepared audience handoff retains both full generations, one source and tablet, consecutive writer epochs and the planned slot.
-- Durable preparation leaves the incumbent grant selected.
-- At the switch the directory directly assigns the successor epoch and records the fence coordinate.
-- Recovery on the original quorum precedes the opening coordinate and authenticates the settlement for that exact handoff.
-- Already held reads may continue at the original attachment until its exclusive drain cutoff.
-- Fresh timed dispatch retains its original certificate and journal cutoff and refuses an incumbent cutoff beyond the fence.

rule IntendMove {
    -- A reservation expires, and a refused activation releases its reservation.
    -- Temporary memory pressure or directory contention can be retried while the reservation remains active.
    -- A handoff park names the deployment, holding epoch, and current reservation.
    when: MoveChosen(record, target)

    requires: record.is_held
    requires: members[target].may_receive_placements

    ensures:
        let reservation = PlacementMove.created(request: FreshMoveId(record), target: target, expires_at: now + config.move_reservation)
        Directory.cas(record.id, record.epoch, change: { move_intent: reservation })
        Subscription.created(deployment: record, member: target, applied_lsn: record.checkpoint.lsn)
        HotRangesPrefetched(from: record.holder, to: target)
}

rule HandOff {
    -- The source pauses admission (the gateway holds new transactions), drains in-flight resolution, commits Handoff, and the target claims.
    -- The target has applied through Handoff already, so its recovery is empty.
    when: SubscriptionCaughtUp(subscription)

    requires: subscription.deployment.active_move_target = subscription.member
    requires: SubscriptionLag(subscription) <= config.handoff_lag_threshold

    ensures:
        let source = holdings[subscription.deployment]
        AdmissionPaused(source)
        AppendCommitted(source, Handoff.created(to: subscription.member))
        source.state = deposed
        ClaimAttempted(member: subscription.member, record: subscription.deployment, expected_epoch: source.epoch)
}

rule ParkForReservedMove {
    -- This path allows a successor to recover a short tail from the quorum.
    when: GuardedParkRequested(record, epoch, reservation)

    requires: record.epoch = epoch
    requires: record.active_move = reservation

    ensures:
        AdmissionPaused(record)
        AdmittedWritesApplied(record)
        if not ShortLocalTail(record):
            FlushAndAdopt(record)
        ParkPlacement(record, preserving: reservation)
        SourceStopsServing(record)
        RecoverFromReplicaLog(record, reservation.target)
}

rule AnswerMoved {
    when: TxArrived(holding, tx, key)

    requires: holding.state = deposed

    ensures:
        Replied(tx, moved: Directory.read(holding.deployment.id).holder)
}

rule PlaceJournals {
    -- An operator records a journal placement for a live residence outside a transfer, recovery, staged checkpoint or fork.
    -- The destination names as many distinct eligible journals as the ensemble, at distinct addresses, spread over no fewer availability zones.
    -- A pending placement for the same destination keeps its operation, and one the ensemble already names is cleared.
    -- A held deployment is parked and activated again, so the placement applies at that claim rather than at its next natural one.
    when: JournalPlacementRequested(record, destination)

    requires: record.lifecycle in {live, quarantined}

    ensures:
        Directory.cas(record.id, record.epoch, change: {
            journal_placement: JournalPlacement(operation: Fresh, destination: destination)
        })
}

rule BalanceJournals {
    -- The controller records at most journal_moves_per_pass journal placements per pass and never parks a holder for them.
    -- It first replaces each draining logkeeper in a deployment's planned journals, choosing the least loaded eligible logkeeper that keeps the destination valid.
    -- It then moves one journal at a time from a logkeeper to one carrying at least two fewer deployments.
    -- A deployment's planned journals are its pending placement's destination, else its ensemble, and they count as its load until a claim applies them.
    -- Each placement is a CAS on the version of the record it planned from.
    when: JournalBalancePass(cell)

    ensures:
        for record in DeploymentsToRebalanceJournals(cell) limit config.journal_moves_per_pass:
            JournalPlacementRequested(record, BalancedDestination(record))
}

rule CertifyLogkeeperDecommission {
    -- Each controller pass reads the deployment records once while any logkeeper is draining.
    -- A record retains custody of a journal while any of its fields names the journal's identity or address, until its disposal receipt is recorded.
    -- That covers its ensemble, a pending journal placement, copy custody, replica journals and staged transfer sources.
    -- The certificate is written conditionally on the member record, and kept across lease renewals while the journal stays draining at the same address and eligibility epoch.
    -- A pass that finds a certified journal still unreferenced keeps its certificate, since no record can have gained it since.
    -- A later pass that finds a reference withdraws the certificate.
    when: ControllerPass(cell)

    ensures:
        for member in cell.members where member.role = log_server and member.state = draining:
            if not exists record in cell.deployments: RetainsCustody(record, member):
                member.decommission = LogkeeperDecommission(certificate: DecommissionCertificate(journal: member.id), decommissioned_at: null)
            else:
                member.decommission = null
}

rule JournalReferenceRequiresCurrentEligibility {
    -- The directory has no transaction spanning a member record and a deployment record, so a writer's view of a logkeeper may be stale when its deployment write lands.
    -- A logkeeper is newly named by a write when the written record names its identity or address and the stored record it replaces does not, by the same matching as RetainsCustody.
    -- Every deployment write is fenced this way: creation, replacement, fork staging and publication, retirement settlement and checkpoint transfer steps.
    -- That covers initial placement, journal placement, ensemble repair, copy reservation, claims, forks and restores, whichever path wrote the field.
    -- The directory observes each newly named logkeeper's eligibility epoch and commits the write only while the epoch is unchanged, in the same conditional write.
    -- A member write that changes state or decommission without moving the epoch on is refused.
    when: DeploymentWritten(stored, written)

    requires: for member in Directory.members where member.role = log_server and NamesJournal(written, member) and not NamesJournal(stored, member):
        member.accepts_journal_references
        and member.eligibility_epoch = ObservedEligibilityEpoch(written, member)

    ensures:
        Directory.cas(written.id, stored.version, change: written)
}

rule JournalCopyLeavesCustodyOnlyByRelease {
    -- `rtdb-multi release-journal-copy` releases one copy, and the controller's journal balance pass releases copies on draining journals that deployments have moved off.
    -- The release retires the deployment's stream on the journal at the current writer epoch, so the copy holds no payload a later disposal or erasure would have to reach.
    -- It then drops every endpoint naming the journal from replica_journals and log_copy_servers, appending the release certificate in the same conditional write.
    -- A release that loses that write reads the record again and repeats every check.
    -- Releasing a copy already released returns the record unchanged.
    -- RetainsCustody and JournalReferenceRequiresCurrentEligibility skip ensemble_handoff and journal_releases, which name journals the record no longer retains.
    when: JournalCopyReleaseRequested(record, member)

    requires: member.role = log_server and member.state = draining
    requires: member.eligibility_epoch is unchanged from the release's first observation
    requires: RetainsCopy(record, member) and member.id not in record.ensemble
    -- Every acknowledged entry is durable in the current journals or the checkpoint.
    requires: record.ensemble_handoff != null and record.ensemble_handoff.destination = Sorted(record.ensemble)
    requires: record.ensemble_handoff.checkpoint_boundary = null or record.checkpoint.boundary >= record.ensemble_handoff.checkpoint_boundary
    -- Nothing else depends on the copy.
    requires: not NamesJournal(WithoutCopy(record, member), member)
    requires: record.checkpoint_decision = null or member.id not in record.checkpoint_decision.journals
    requires: record.journal_placement = null and record.active_move = null and record.writer_handoffs = [] and record.cell_transfer = null and no checkpoint transfer is in flight
    requires: no pending checkpoint recovery, serving replay capture, staged checkpoint, replica replay region, unapplied payload trim or checkpoint suffix pin
    requires: record.fork_stage = null and no staged fork names record as its parent
    requires: record.lifecycle in {live, quarantined} and record.deletion_record = null and record.disposal = null
    requires: record is not in installed-journal custody

    ensures:
        Logkeeper.retire(member, record.replica_stream, record.epoch)
        Directory.cas(record.id, record.version, change: {
            replica_journals: record.replica_journals without member,
            log_copy_servers: record.log_copy_servers without member,
            journal_releases: record.journal_releases + [JournalCopyRelease(journal: member.id, epoch: record.epoch, handoff: record.ensemble_handoff)]
        })
}

rule LogkeeperDecommissionRequiresNoCustody {
    -- `rtdb-multi decommission-logkeeper` retires a logkeeper only under a certificate for its own identity and address, issued while it was draining.
    -- It reads the deployment records again and refuses while any retains custody of the journal, so every journal a record references, including one a journal placement moved it off, stays a member until the record finishes disposal or releases the copy.
    -- The retirement is conditional on the member still draining under the same certificate, at the eligibility epoch the certificate was written at.
    -- JournalReferenceRequiresCurrentEligibility keeps any record from gaining the journal once it is certified, so this check sees every reference a record can hold when the retirement lands.
    -- The process serving the journal loses its lease, after which the journal's storage may be destroyed.
    -- No placement, ensemble repair or claim chooses a logkeeper that is not eligible.
    when: LogkeeperDecommissionRequested(member)

    requires: member.role = log_server
    requires: member.state = draining
    requires: member.decommission != null and member.decommission.decommissioned_at = null
    requires: member.decommission.certificate.journal = member.id and member.decommission.certificate.address = member.address
    requires: not exists record in Directory.deployments: RetainsCustody(record, member)

    ensures:
        member.state = retired
        member.decommission.decommissioned_at = now
}

rule DrainMember {
    -- Scale-in, replacement and rollout drain a member by handing off its active deployments and parking its idle ones, at most moves_per_node_per_pass per pass.
    -- The source's identity and epoch still match when the drain reserves a handoff.
    -- A draining member runs one handoff at a time.
    -- A recent holding stays on its source while the placement pool has no eligible destination.
    -- A node shutting down marks itself draining, parks its holdings with bounded concurrency, and retires its lease once they are parked or its drain budget runs out.
    -- A holding still on the node when it retires recovers through a fresh writer epoch, as after a lease expiry.
    when: member: MemberRecord.state becomes draining

    ensures:
        for holding in HoldingsOf(member):
            if RecentlyUsed(holding) and HasEligibleDestination(holding):
                MoveChosen(holding.deployment, Affinity.next_rank(holding.deployment, excluding: member))
            if IdleForDrain(holding):
                ParkStarted(holding)
}

rule Rebalance {
    -- The balancer moves deployments off a node over a watermark on any LoadVector dimension, each to its next eligible rank.
    -- A write rate compares accepted source bytes across observations of the same holding epoch.
    -- Query CPU uses native query opening and result-batch execution time on the holder.
    -- A move using rates uses members with observations for each active holding.
    -- Each eligible target belongs to the deployment's placement pool.
    -- A reservation uses the source holding's identity and epoch from the plan.
    -- A conflicting reservation or a changed source ends the current pass.
    -- Configured byte watermarks use sampled live batch and in-memory object cache charges on active writers.
    -- A target must fit every configured watermark after the proposed move.
    -- A busy source samples only its local active holders.
    when: NodeOverWatermark(member, dimension)

    ensures:
        for holding in LargestBy(member, dimension) limit config.moves_per_node_per_pass:
            MoveChosen(holding.deployment, Affinity.next_rank(holding.deployment, excluding: member))
}

rule ReportPlacementDemand {
    when: PlacementStatusRequested(cell)

    ensures:
        HeldDeploymentsReadByMember(cell)
        HolderCountsReportedByPool(cell)
        ResidentIdleCountsReportedByPool(cell)
        PlacementNodeTargetsReportedByPool(cell)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: reads
------------------------------------------------------------

rule BoundInitialGreeting {
    when: StartupDeadlineElapsed(connection)

    requires: connection.ready = false

    ensures:
        if connection.startup_received:
            ClientMayRetryStartup(connection)
        ConnectionClosed(connection)
}

rule RouteWriterThroughControl {
    when: DirectPgwireStartupUnavailable(gateway, holder)

    requires: GatewayHasCellGrant(gateway)
    requires: PgwireAuthenticationIsPeerAddressIndependent(holder)

    ensures:
        LocalPgwireConnectionOpened(holder)
        StartupSentToHolderForAuthentication(gateway, holder)
        PlacementUnchanged(holder)
}

rule TouchConnectedBackend {
    when: BackendTouchDue(gateway, deployment, address)

    requires: GatewayHasConnectedSession(gateway, deployment, address)

    ensures:
        TouchSent(gateway, deployment, address)
}

rule RouteRead {
    -- The gateway walks the deployment's affinity order within its read width.
    -- The holder is normally rank 1, so the first choice is usually the writer.
    when: ReadRequested(session, query)

    ensures:
        if session.pinned_member != null:
            Dispatched(query, session.pinned_member)
        else if session.freshness = as_of and query.basis <= Directory.read(session.deployment).checkpoint.lsn:
            Dispatched(query, Affinity.select(key: session.deployment.storage_identity, pool: readers, tier: snapshot))
        else:
            Dispatched(query, Affinity.select(key: session.deployment.storage_identity, pool: readers, width: route.read_width))
}

rule SpreadReplicaSessions {
    -- Pgwire read-only sessions try the least occupied replica within the deployment's read width.
    -- Concurrent connection attempts rotate their starting rank before active session counts are available.
    when: ReplicaSessionRequested(gateway, deployment)

    requires: deployment.read_width > 1

    ensures:
        EligibleReplicaAttempted(gateway, deployment)
}

-- A placed query keeps its local database owner while checking authority.
-- If the local owner detached or changed during the check, the node refuses the query before planning.

rule ServeOnWriter {
    when: QueryArrived(holding, query, session)

    requires: holding.state = serving
    requires: session.freshness != linearizable

    ensures:
        Served(query, tier: writer, at: max(session.basis, holding.applied_basis))
}

rule ServeLinearizableReadOnWriter {
    -- A holder serves a linearizable read only after a write quorum confirms its promised and synced epoch in a round that starts after the query arrives.
    -- A busy holder can use positive replies to appends and commits sent during the round, and an idle holder sends one check per server.
    --   Concurrent queries share one confirmation round, at most one is in flight per holder, and a query arriving during one waits for the next.
    when: QueryArrived(holding, query, session)

    requires: holding.state = serving
    requires: session.freshness = linearizable

    ensures:
        let round = NextRoundAcknowledged(holding)
        if not SealedIn(round, holding.deployment):
            Served(query, tier: writer, at: holding.applied_basis)
        else:
            holding.state = deposed
            Replied(query, moved: Directory.read(holding.deployment.id).holder)
}

rule ConfirmHostedSource {
    when: HostedSourceBarrierRequested(holding, session)

    requires: session.deployment = holding.deployment.id
    requires: holding.state = serving
    requires: Directory.read(holding.deployment.id).holder = holding.member
    requires: Directory.read(holding.deployment.id).epoch = holding.epoch
    requires: Directory.read(holding.deployment.id).linearizable_reads
    requires: Directory.read(holding.deployment.id).checkpoint = null
              or holding.applied_lsn >= Directory.read(holding.deployment.id).checkpoint.lsn

    ensures:
        let round = NextRoundAcknowledged(holding)
        if not SealedIn(round, holding.deployment):
            let cut = SampleSource(holding)
            AwaitApplied(holding, cut)
            let applied = LatestAppliedTransaction(holding)
            HostedSourceConfirmed(session, history: holding.deployment.id, applied_transaction: applied)
        else:
            Replied(session, unavailable: true)
}

rule ConfirmHostedSourceOwner {
    when: HostedSourceOwnerRequested(holding, session)

    requires: session.deployment = holding.deployment.id
    requires: holding.state = serving
    requires: Directory.read(holding.deployment.id).holder = holding.member
    requires: Directory.read(holding.deployment.id).epoch = holding.epoch
    requires: Directory.read(holding.deployment.id).linearizable_reads
    requires: Directory.read(holding.deployment.id).checkpoint = null
              or holding.applied_lsn >= Directory.read(holding.deployment.id).checkpoint.lsn

    ensures:
        let round = NextRoundAcknowledged(holding)
        if not SealedIn(round, holding.deployment):
            HostedSourceOwnerConfirmed(session, history: holding.deployment.id)
        else:
            Replied(session, unavailable: true)
}

rule OpenHostedAppliedRead {
    when: HostedAppliedReadRequested(holding, session, floor)

    requires: session.deployment = holding.deployment.id
    requires: ConfirmHostedSourceOwner(holding, session)

    ensures:
        let captured = CaptureAppliedCatalog(holding)
        if CurrentCatalogLease(holding, captured)
           and CoversTransaction(captured.basis, floor):
            HeldReadOpened(session, captured, basis: captured.basis)
        else:
            Replied(session, unavailable: true)
}

rule ConfirmHostedAppliedRead {
    when: HostedAppliedConfirmationRequested(holding, session, original, floor, controls, point, cutoff)

    requires: original.session = session
    requires: controls.all(CheckFromInstalledAllowlist)
    requires: point = null or OrdinaryRootPointAt(point, original.basis)

    ensures:
        let current = CaptureAppliedCatalog(holding)
        if CurrentCatalogLease(holding, current)
           and CoversTransaction(current.basis, original.basis)
           and CoversTransaction(current.basis, floor)
           and MatchesControls(original, controls)
           and MatchesControls(current, controls)
           and (point = null or (MatchesPoint(original, point) and MatchesPoint(current, point)))
           and Clock.now < cutoff:
            HostedAppliedControlsConfirmed(session, basis: current.basis)
        else:
            Replied(session, unavailable: true)
}

rule ServeOnSubscriber {
    when: QueryArrived(subscription, query, session)

    ensures:
        if session.freshness = linearizable and NeedsImplicitBasis(query, session):
            let bound = ConfirmActiveLogletReadIndex(subscription.deployment)
            if bound != null and AwaitApplied(subscription, bound, within: config.subscriber_basis_wait):
                CaptureImplicitBasis(query, from: subscription.applied_lsn)
                Served(query, tier: subscriber, at: subscription.applied_lsn)
            else:
                Replied(query, behind: true)
        else if subscription.applied_lsn >= session.basis.lsn:
            Served(query, tier: subscriber, at: subscription.applied_lsn)
        else:
            if AwaitApplied(subscription, session.basis.lsn, within: config.subscriber_basis_wait):
                Served(query, tier: subscriber, at: session.basis)
            else:
                Replied(query, behind: true)
                -- The gateway retries on the holder.
}

rule RetrySubscriberRefusalOnHolder {
    when: SubscriberRefused(session, query, behind: true)

    requires: NoResponseDelivered(query)
    requires: BackendReportsIdle(session)

    ensures:
        RoutedToHolder(session.deployment, query)
}

rule ServeSnapshot {
    -- The snapshot uses the exact root admitted for this attachment.
    -- The checkpoint confirms a causal token whose history, journal and principal match and whose bound is within the installed cut.
    -- A matching attempt outcome in the checkpoint can be exchanged for a causal token at that cut.
    -- A receipt beyond the cut or an attempt whose outcome is absent receives a retryable checkpoint-behind response.
    when: QueryArrived(member, query, session)

    requires: query.basis.lsn <= Directory.read(session.deployment).checkpoint.lsn

    ensures:
        Served(query, tier: snapshot, at: query.basis)
}

rule WidenOnPressure {
    -- The controller counts authenticated, replica-eligible Pgwire sessions at their serving nodes.
    -- Observed pressure can widen the route when the next affinity rank has reported.
    -- A widening reserves one more eligible read rank and replicas open on demand.
    -- The gateway applies the wider route to subsequent connections and reconnects.
    when: ReadOverflow(deployment)

    requires: config.read_sessions_per_node > 0
              and deployment.read_width < config.max_read_width

    ensures:
        Directory.cas(deployment.id, deployment.epoch, change: { read_width: deployment.read_width + 1 })
}

rule NarrowOnQuiet {
    -- A replica outside the remaining affinity ranks retires when the node observes the narrower width.
    -- Quiet uses half the widening capacity for each serving node.
    -- Consecutive quiet observations of the writer pool must agree on the writer epoch and width for config.read_width_quiet_samples samples.
    when: ReadQuiet(deployment)

    requires: config.read_sessions_per_node > 0
              and deployment.read_width > 1

    ensures:
        Directory.cas(deployment.id, deployment.epoch, change: { read_width: deployment.read_width - 1 })
        SubscriptionEnded(deployment, rank: deployment.read_width)
}

rule RecordBasis {
    when: Replied(tx, outcome, basis)

    ensures:
        tx.session.basis = max(tx.session.basis, basis)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: affinity
--
-- Rendezvous (highest-random-weight) hashing over (key, member identity), as in Bijection's affinity crate, whose score bytes are fixed by shared test vectors.
------------------------------------------------------------

rule Rank {
    -- Every member of a pool, most preferred first, by score(key, member) descending, ties broken by member identity.
    -- Adding or removing a member never reorders the others.
    when: RankRequested(key, pool)

    ensures:
        Ranked(key, SortDescending(pool, by: Score(key, member.id)))
}

rule Select {
    -- Walk the order within width: skip members that may not serve and members the caller excluded after a refusal; set pressured members aside; take the first that grants a slot under its per-key in-flight bound; else the first pressured member that does; else refuse.
    -- Pressure and occupancy gate preference and never reorder it, so a hot key's overflow always warms the same next members.
    when: SelectRequested(key, pool, width, excluded)

    ensures:
        Selected(key, WalkRanked(Rank(key, pool), width, excluded))
}

rule PrewarmOnJoin {
    -- A joining member pulls, from peers before object storage, the hottest ranges of the keys it now ranks first or second for.
    when: member: MemberRecord.state becomes joining

    ensures:
        for key in KeysRankedWithin(member, 2):
            HotRangesPrefetched(from: PreviousRank(key, member), to: member)
        MemberPrewarmed(member)
}

rule RefineKeyForLargeDeployments {
    -- A deployment whose data exceeds one member's cache distributes scan fragments by (storage identity, table, trie prefix), so each member keeps a stable slice warm.
    -- The coordinator keeps its captured snapshot for a local fallback.
    -- A delegated executor must capture the requested transaction head with no private overlays.
    -- The request identifies the coordinator's opened storage residence and replica epoch.
    -- The router and the executor each check that identity against their own authority.
    -- The executor holds the captured snapshot until admitted provider reads have completed.
    -- The coordinator sends a bounded Arrow IPC literal page after the reader confirms support on the same connection.
    -- The peers select the newest literal page version they both support.
    -- The JSON request carries the expression shape.
    -- The reader restores literal expressions in their original positions.
    -- The reader decodes the Arrow IPC arguments from slices of the retained request frame.
    -- The coordinator applies its original predicates to the returned rows.
    -- A reader sends length-framed Arrow IPC when the coordinator advertises binary reply support.
    -- Readers execute concurrently and the coordinator admits each binary reply against query memory before receiving its body in fragment order.
    -- A coordinator can read the earlier base64 reply during a rolling upgrade.
    -- A reader answers an earlier coordinator in its advertised reply format.
    when: ScanPlanned(deployment, scan)

    requires: deployment.cache_bytes > PerMemberCacheShare()

    ensures:
        for fragment in Fragments(scan):
            Dispatched(fragment, Affinity.select(key: deployment.storage_identity + "/" + fragment.table + "/" + fragment.trie_prefix, pool: readers))
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: in-deployment data branches
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The data-plane trunk is main.
-- A branch keeps its base branch and exact base basis in the deployment's transaction stream.
-- The base can itself be a branch.
-- A branch has one name for the lifetime of its incarnation.
-- Branch lineage is exposed through the xt.branch_lineage system table.
-- Data-branch operations apply in the order of the selected transaction effects (tx.allium).
-- Local reads resolve the branch's assertions alone.
-- Merged reads resolve a local assertion or tombstone first for each valid-time region.
-- The remaining regions resolve through the base at its base basis.
-- Counts, aggregates, text matching and spatial ranking consume the same effective row population.
-- A descendant retains its own base cut when an ancestor expires or is dropped.

rule ForkDataBranch {
    when: OrderedDataBranchFork(deployment, child, parent, base, writable, expires_at, basis)
    requires: AvailableBranchAt(deployment, parent, base)
    ensures:
        BranchRegistered(deployment, child, parent, base, writable, expires_at, basis)
}

rule RenewDataBranch {
    when: OrderedDataBranchRenewal(deployment, branch, expires_at, basis)
    requires: ActiveDataBranch(deployment, branch)
    ensures:
        BranchExpirySet(deployment, branch, expires_at, basis)
}

rule MergeDataBranch {
    -- A merge copies the source's effective local assertions and tombstones at their original valid intervals.
    -- A change in the target after the source's fork basis can conflict on an overlapping row and interval.
    when: OrderedDataBranchMerge(deployment, source, target, basis)
    requires: MergeAdmitted(deployment, source, target, basis)
    ensures:
        BranchDeltaApplied(deployment, source, target, basis)
}

rule DropDataBranch {
    when: OrderedDataBranchDrop(deployment, branch, basis)
    requires: ActiveDataBranch(deployment, branch)
    ensures:
        BranchDropped(deployment, branch, basis)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: administrative deployment forks
------------------------------------------------------------

rule ForkDeployment {
    -- ForkDeployment within an organisation creates a new deployment in the parent's lineage.
    -- Its first manifest references the parent's objects at the fork basis, plus one pack holding the parent's tail up to that basis.
    -- The shared key grants are in the same erasure custody domain.
    -- Child publication checks that the parent is still eligible for forks in the writer epoch and storage generation used to prepare the child.
    -- The child, its name and the cell-local shared-reference revision publish atomically.
    -- A fork of a fork advances the same revision as the original lineage.
    -- A local fork acquires a preparation in the current lineage generation before reading storage and consumes it when the child publishes.
    -- A hosted fork holds a preparation in the current lineage generation through provider I/O.
    -- Its child identity and physical custody are recorded before it writes objects, keys or journals.
    -- Each provider upload is recorded on its staged child before submission.
    -- Publication checks the exact staged child, the current preparation and a drained storage region.
    -- An intervening retirement therefore requires fresh preparation.
    -- Local preparation, publication and reclamation hold the same volume maintenance lock through their admitted provider work.
    when: ForkRequested(parent, basis, org)

    -- The child and its name publish at a version that still admits forks.
    -- The writer epoch and storage generation match the staged basis.
    requires: org = parent.org
              and parent.lifecycle in {live, quarantined}

    ensures:
        let child = DeploymentId.created()
        BumpLineageVersion(parent.lineage)
        DeploymentRecord.created(
            id: child,
            org: org,
            lineage: parent.lineage,
            incarnation: 1,
            parent: parent.id,
            fork_basis: basis,
            storage_root: StorageRoot.created(deployment: child, org: org, lineage: parent.lineage, incarnation: 1),
            key_id: KeyCreated(context: child),
            lifecycle: live,
            checkpoint: ForkCheckpoint(parent, basis),
            epoch: Epoch(value: 0),
            holder: null,
            loglets: []
        )
        KeyGrantIssued(to: child, on: parent.key_id)
}

rule ForkAcrossOrganisations {
    -- A fork into another organisation creates its own lineage and key.
    -- Its independent copy leaves the original lineage's shared-reference revision unchanged.
    -- Its checkpoint closure and its selected tail are copied under the child's key before the directory publishes the child.
    -- The destination root follows the required data, metadata, retained level-zero sources and recorded usable artefacts.
    -- The copy checks the table bytes used to discover those dependencies against the root manifest when present.
    -- Each planned member binds its logical path, length and observed provider publication.
    -- A packed member's physical dependency names the containing pack, including an inherited pack.
    -- This copy has independent key custody and its own erasure authority.
    -- Publication checks the parent in the writer epoch and storage generation used to prepare the copy.
    when: ForkRequested(parent, basis, org)

    requires: org != parent.org
              and parent.lifecycle in {live, quarantined}

    ensures:
        let child = CreateDeployment(org)
        CopiedAndReencrypted(from: ForkCheckpoint(parent, basis), to: child)
}

rule RecoverUnpublishedHostedFork {
    -- A staged child reserves its identity and can acquire its serving name only during publication.
    -- A holder's definitive rejection follows the drain of its preparation I/O.
    -- A reaper can close an abandoned stage after the source preparation has been released.
    -- Attested task stoppage can release an abandoned preparation.
    -- The stage version orders reaper recovery against publication.
    -- Closing the stage closes its storage region to further publication and retains unresolved upload IDs.
    -- The reaper verifies the physical namespace and settles each retained upload before provider inventory disposal.
    -- An unpublished stage contributes no references to the served lineage.
    when: HostedForkCleanupRequested(stage)

    requires: DefinitiveRejectionAfterDrain(stage) or SourcePreparationReleased(stage)

    ensures:
        if Directory.cas(stage.id, stage.version, change: { lifecycle: deleting, epoch: Next(stage.epoch) }):
            SettleStagedProviderPublications(stage)
            if ServingPublicationsSettled(stage):
                DisposeStagedResidence(stage)
}

rule ForkReadsAreCapped {
    -- A fork at a past basis reads inherited objects with a system-time cap at its fork basis, until its own compaction has rewritten them into its prefix.
    when: QueryArrived(member, query, session)

    requires: session.deployment.parent != null

    ensures:
        InheritedObjectsCappedAt(session.deployment.fork_basis)
}

rule ReadTransferredForkObject {
    when: ForkObjectRead(record, object)

    requires: object in InheritedReferences(record)

    ensures:
        ReadLocalCopy(record, object)
        if LocalCopyMissing(record, object):
            ReadReferencedCopy(record, object)
            if ReferencedCopyMissing(record, object):
                ReadLocalCopy(record, object)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: deletion
------------------------------------------------------------

rule LocalStorageMaintenance {
    when: AdministrativeObjectMutationRequested(cell, operation)

    requires: LocalStorage(cell)
    requires: NoOtherLocalMaintenanceHoldsLock(cell)

    ensures:
        LocalMaintenanceLockHeldThroughMutation(cell, operation)
}

rule BeginDeletion {
    -- The epoch bump fences every writer before anything is destroyed.
    -- The holder's claim binds the object and key namespaces before it writes.
    -- The deletion uses those namespaces.
    -- A pristine root can bind its namespaces in the deletion CAS.
    -- An unbound deletion started before namespace binding resumes once the live nodes all report the same configuration.
    when: DeleteRequested(record)

    requires: record.lifecycle in {live, quarantined}

    ensures:
        Directory.cas(record.id, record.epoch, change: {
            epoch: Epoch(value: record.epoch.value + 1),
            holder: null,
            lifecycle: deleting
        })
        if RecordUsesS3(record):
            RecordLeaseHorizon(record, now + config.credential_ttl)
        for loglet in record.loglets:
            SealSent(loglet, epoch: Epoch(value: record.epoch.value + 1))
        StreamDropped(record.id)
        DeletionScheduled(record, after: now + config.credential_ttl)
}

rule ClaimStoreRoot {
    -- Local nodes can prepare a durable locked witness before admitting a storage region.
    -- Each local region consumes its witness once and keeps the lock until its operations have drained.
    -- The region removes its witness file only once its directory release settles; a refused or cancelled release drops the lock and leaves the witness for reconciliation.
    when: DeploymentStoreOpened(record, storage_prefix, writer_epoch)

    requires: record.lifecycle in {live, quarantined}
    requires: storage_prefix = record.storage_root.prefix
    requires: writer_epoch = null or writer_epoch = record.epoch

    ensures:
        if writer_epoch != null:
            StoreRootClaimed(record.id, storage_prefix)
        else:
            ExistingRootOwnerVerified(record.id, storage_prefix)
}

rule VerifyWriterDuringWatchStartup {
    when: StoreWriteRequested(record, epoch)

    requires: WatchHasNoObservationAtOrAfter(record.id, epoch)

    ensures:
        let current = Directory.read(record.id)
        if current.epoch = epoch and current.holder != null and current.lifecycle in {live, quarantined}:
            WriteMayProceed(record)
        else:
            WriteRefused(record)
}

rule EnrollTerminalS3Allocation {
    -- The allocation names a provider root disjoint from legacy storage.
    -- The provisioning authority attests the provider policy for that exact root from its allocation onward.
    when: TerminalAllocationEnrollmentRequested(operator, allocation, qualification)

    requires: QualificationAuthenticatesExactAllocation(qualification, allocation)
    requires: ServingBucketNeverVersioned(allocation.namespace)
    requires: ProviderFencesOrdinaryWritesUntilActivation(allocation)
    requires: ProviderEnforcesCreateOnceForAllocationLifetime(allocation)

    ensures:
        if StoredQualificationAndActivationMarkerAuthenticate(allocation):
            TerminalAllocationEnrolled(allocation)
        else if FreshTerminalInventoryContainsOnlyEnrollmentResidue(allocation):
            StoreImmutableQualification(allocation, qualification)
            InstallAllocationActivationMarker(allocation)
            TerminalAllocationEnrolled(allocation)
}

rule OpenTerminalS3Allocation {
    when: TerminalAllocationAccessRequested(requester, allocation)

    requires: StoredQualificationAndActivationMarkerAuthenticate(allocation)
    requires: AdapterUsesExactProviderRoot(allocation)

    ensures:
        OpenEnrolledTerminalAllocation(allocation)
}

rule RefreshScopedS3Session {
    when: S3WriteCredentialsRequested(record, writer_epoch)

    requires: DeploymentStillUsesStoragePrefix(record)
    requires: WriterOrCompactorAuthorized(record, writer_epoch)
    requires: TerminalStorageUsesScopedConditionalCreation(record)

    ensures:
        let session = ProviderAssumedWriteRole(prefix: record.storage_root.prefix, max_age: config.credential_ttl)
        if WriterOrCompactorAuthorized(record, writer_epoch):
            if DirectoryRecordsMaximumWriteExpiry(record, session.expiration):
                ScopedWriteSessionIssued(session)
        else:
            WriteRefused(record)
}

rule TransferSharedObjects {
    -- Disposal closes fork publication under the current version of the drained physical residence.
    -- The close advances the lineage generation and survives interrupted attempts.
    -- The referrer inventory is loaded after the close.
    -- Each surviving referrer receives the bytes under its own prefix.
    -- Hosted disposal enumerates declared fallback references into the admitted prefix, including sources absent from the current object listing.
    -- An unavailable payload leaves disposal pending until every surviving referrer has a readable copy.
    -- Release bookkeeping that has already disappeared can remain absent.
    when: DeletionDue(record)

    requires: ServingStorageRegionsDrained(record)

    ensures:
        for object in OwnedBy(record) where ReferencedByAnotherLiveMember(record.lineage, object):
            for referrer in LiveReferrers(record.lineage, object):
                CopyInto(referrer.storage_root.prefix, object)
}

rule EraseAndProve {
    -- If the inventory still contains objects, the deployment remains deleting and can retry with the same epoch.
    -- A destroyed key cannot be installed or allocated again under the same deployment identity in that cell.
    -- The terminal transaction keeps the signed receipt with the tombstone and reopens the exact lineage disposal together.
    -- A retry returns the same receipt.
    -- Hosted removal uses the multipart and version inventory of the admitted prefix after reference transfer.
    when: SharedObjectsTransferred(record)

    requires: DeploymentPrefixIsDisjoint(record)
              and ServingStorageRegionsDrained(record)
              and ServingPublicationsSettled(record)

    ensures:
        RetireReplicaJournals(record.id, record.epoch, AllRecordedJournals(record))
        AbortMultipartUploadsUnder(record.storage_root.prefix)
        DeleteAllVersionsUnder(record.storage_root.prefix)
        AwaitEmptyInventory(record.storage_root.prefix, twice_separated_by: config.credential_ttl)
        KeyService.destroy(record.key_id)
        let proof = DeletionRecord(deployment: record.id, epoch: record.epoch, inventory_scope: all_versions_and_multipart_uploads, signature: SignedByCell())
        Directory.cas(record.id, record.epoch, change: { lifecycle: deleted, deletion_record: proof })
}

rule PublishInventoryReceipt {
    -- A local store attests to the current inventory.
    -- An S3 store attests to its multipart and version inventories after aborting uploads and removing data versions and delete markers.
    -- The receipt and the reopening of the matching physical disposal are atomic.
    when: InventoryEmptied(record, namespaces, inventory_scope)

    requires: record.lifecycle = deleting
    requires: record.storage_namespaces = namespaces
    requires: RecordedWriteLeasesExpired(record)
    requires: LocalStorage(record) or record.deletion_observation.second_empty_at != null

    ensures:
        KeyService.destroy(record.key_id)
        let proof = DeletionRecord(deployment: record.id, epoch: record.epoch, namespaces: namespaces, inventory_scope: inventory_scope, signature: SignedByCell())
        Directory.cas(record.id, record.epoch, change: { lifecycle: deleted, deletion_record: proof })
}

rule SettleObservedS3Version {
    -- Every retry sends the immutable identifier retained by the retirement authority.
    -- The hosted trie release binds the marker version from the same response as the release body.
    -- The physical owner fences both paths while the installed release is in flight.
    -- Data and marker retirement each need an exact absence observation.
    -- The directory clears the owner fence and reopens the lineage atomically.
    -- Settlement uses a fresh, complete lookup scoped to the exact key and version.
    when: ExactVersionRetirementContinued(retirement)

    requires: ExactVersionRetirementAuthorized(retirement)
    requires: PhysicalNamespaceMatches(retirement)
    requires: PhysicalKeyMatches(retirement)
    requires: ImmutableS3Version(retirement.version)

    ensures:
        if FreshVersionInventoryConfirmsAbsence(retirement.namespace, retirement.key, retirement.version):
            ExactVersionRetirementSettled(retirement)
        else if FreshVersionInventoryAllowsRemovalWithoutExposingOlderData(retirement):
            DeleteExactS3Version(retirement.namespace, retirement.key, retirement.version)
            if FreshVersionInventoryConfirmsAbsence(retirement.namespace, retirement.key, retirement.version):
                ExactVersionRetirementSettled(retirement)
}

rule AdmitNeverVersionedTombstoneUpload {
    -- An empty provider upload acquires one slot in the authorized retirement before payload submission.
    when: NeverVersionedTombstoneUploadReserved(retirement, key, upload)

    requires: ExactVersionRetirementAuthorized(retirement)
    requires: RetirementKeyMatches(retirement, key)
    requires: NoRetainedTombstoneUpload(retirement, key)

    ensures:
        RecordTombstoneUpload(retirement, key, upload)
        AllowConditionalTombstoneCompletion(retirement, key, upload)
}

rule SettleNeverVersionedRetirement {
    -- The reaper can resume both exact upload identities after interruption.
    -- Serving reads treat a tombstone as an absent object, and the provider retains the key against create-if-absent writes.
    when: NeverVersionedRetirementContinued(retirement)

    requires: ExactVersionRetirementAuthorized(retirement)
    requires: ServingBucketVersioningProhibited(retirement.namespace)
    requires: PhysicalOwnerFencedForObject(retirement.data_key)
    requires: PhysicalOwnerFencedForObject(retirement.release_key)

    ensures:
        for upload in RetainedTombstoneUploads(retirement):
            DrainExactTombstoneUpload(upload)
            if FreshProviderObservationConfirmsUploadClosed(upload):
                ForgetTombstoneUpload(retirement, upload)
        if NoRetainedTombstoneUploads(retirement) and FreshTombstoneObservationsMatch(retirement) and ServingBucketNeverVersioned(retirement.namespace):
            SettleOwnerFenceAndReopenLineage(retirement)
}

rule AuthorizeTerminalS3Retirement {
    when: HostedLooseFileReleaseDue(release)

    requires: InstalledCatalogFloorCovers(release)
    requires: TerminalAllocationMatchesPublication(release)
    requires: CurrentProviderKeyAndTagMatchInstalledPublication(release)
    requires: CurrentReleaseMarkerMatchesReservedRetirement(release)
    requires: PublishedCheckpointAndAdmittedRecoveriesCoverRelease(release)
    requires: LineageReservedFor(release)
    requires: NoCurrentManifestReferences(release.physical_key)
    requires: PhysicalOwnerRevisionMatchesValidatedFence(release)

    ensures:
        ExactVersionRetirementAuthorized(release)
}

rule AuthorizeHostedVersionErasure {
    -- The retirement reservation binds the physical version, release marker and source residence together.
    -- The reservation closes fork preparation and publication while the reaper scans the current manifests of the lineage.
    -- The physical owner's storage region orders new inherited reads of the affected keys against the reservation.
    -- Authorization compares the same owner revision and fence that passed the retention checks.
    -- The source's committed recovery inventory covers the installed removal.
    -- A startup or replay hold may retain an older cut.
    -- An admitted metadata reader without a replay bound retains the removal until its provider work drains.
    -- Settlement reopens admission after a complete provider inventory confirms absence of the selected version.
    when: HostedLooseFileReleaseDue(release)

    requires: InstalledCatalogFloorCovers(release)
    requires: PublishedCheckpointAndAdmittedRecoveriesCoverRelease(release)
    requires: LineageReservedFor(release)
    requires: NoCurrentManifestReferences(release.physical_key)
    requires: PhysicalOwnerRevisionMatchesValidatedFence(release)

    ensures:
        ExactVersionRetirementAuthorized(release)
}

rule RecoverHostedReleaseWitness {
    -- An older release can use the authenticated installation receipt for its exact catalog cut and object.
    -- The original release remains durable when the provider hides the released version from current-name listing.
    -- The source fence drains inherited reads of the protected object and the older marker before removal.
    when: LegacyHostedReleaseDue(release)

    requires: InstalledCatalogFloorCovers(release)
    requires: AuthenticatedReceiptNamesPublication(release.residence, release.cut, release.object, version)

    ensures:
        HostedVersionReleasePrepared(release, version, ReceiptOf(release))
}

rule HostedCheckpointCapture {
    -- A new archive receipt covers the verified replay inventory.
    -- Shared group artefacts are recorded once. Required files must be available before the receipt is published.
    -- A historical stage carries the inventory format of its source archive.
    -- An archive copy from serving storage registers a metadata hold on the selected checkpoint.
    -- A new hold may cover the published checkpoint or inherit uninterrupted coverage from an older admission.
    -- The hold persists until its provider work drains. A verified existing archive can be returned after the source hold ends.
    -- The source plan is checked against the full exact body observation before resealing each member.
    -- Discovery runs under a lineage preparation.
    -- A complete versioned plan registers its exact publications with their physical owners before releasing the lineage preparation.
    -- Those holds last through provider drain.
    -- A plan without complete version evidence retains the lineage preparation.
    -- Unrelated provider versions remain eligible for retirement during the copy.
    -- A serving read supplied by an archive carries the archive's physical identity.
    -- A legacy archive range is sliced from the bytes resealed for the serving key.
    -- The capture region retains its destination and its serving archive source independently.
    -- A local source copy holds the cell maintenance witness through admitted I/O.
    -- A cell transfer that materializes repair data retains the archive in its original directory and custody.
    when: AuthenticatedRecoveryCapture(authority, name, capture_id)

    requires: ConfiguredOrganizationOwns(authority, name)
              and RootInstallation(authority)
              and CaptureEligibleOrCompleted(authority, capture_id)

    ensures:
        if RetainedCaptureReceipt(authority, capture_id, ConfiguredArchive):
            ReturnOriginalCaptureReceipt(authority, capture_id)
        else:
            RegisterAndVerifyCheckpointArchive(authority, capture_id, ConfiguredArchive)
            BindOriginalArchiveKeyAuthority(authority, capture_id)
            SealArchiveWithCaptureKey(authority, capture_id)
            RetainAndReturnExactCheckpointAndManifestDigest(authority, capture_id)
}

rule PrepareCheckpointCaptureDiscard {
    -- A preparation names one capture in its original archive authority.
    -- It closes further admission while admitted storage regions retain provider access through drain.
    -- The capture and its dependent suffixes share one archived media key.
    when: AuthenticatedCaptureDiscard(operator, authority, capture)

    requires: OriginalArchiveAuthority(authority, capture)
              and NoRetainedWorldOrRepairDependsOn(capture)
              and NoLiveSuffixPinDependsOn(capture)

    ensures:
        CommitCaptureAdmissionFence(capture)
        RetainCaptureDiscardPreparation(capture)
}

rule ScheduleCheckpointCaptureRevocation {
    -- The original operation authority preserves the exact order and its due time in one durable admission.
    -- A due schedule closes further cryptographic operations, including through already issued handles.
    when: AuthenticatedCaptureRevocationScheduled(operator, authority, capture, due_at)

    requires: ExactOriginalOperationOnlyKeyAuthority(authority, capture)
              and NoRetainedWorldOrRepairDependsOn(capture)
              and NoLiveSuffixPinDependsOn(capture)

    ensures:
        CommitCaptureAdmissionFence(capture)
        RecordExactAuthorityRevocationSchedule(capture, due_at)
}

rule CloseArchiveRevocationAdmission {
    -- The native vault scopes its inventory to the original installation and incarnation.
    -- The same vault serializes closure against admission and retains the exact admitted set across restart.
    -- An unattributed prior withdrawal or schedule prevents an exhaustive result.
    when: AuthenticatedArchiveRevocationClose(operator, installation, incarnation)

    requires: ConfiguredOrganizationOwnsOriginalInstallation(operator, installation)
              and ExactOriginalOperationOnlyKeyAuthorityForScope(installation, incarnation)
              and AllPriorVaultRevocationsHaveExactOrders(installation, incarnation)

    ensures:
        SealVaultRevocationAdmission(installation, incarnation)
        RejectNewVaultRevocationOrders(installation, incarnation)
        PreservePreviouslyAdmittedOrdersAndTheirOutcomes(installation, incarnation)
}

rule ReadClosedArchiveRevocationOutcomes {
    when: AuthenticatedArchiveRevocationOutcomes(operator, installation, incarnation, cursor)

    requires: ConfiguredOrganizationOwnsOriginalInstallation(operator, installation)
              and ExactOriginalOperationOnlyKeyAuthorityForScope(installation, incarnation)
              and VaultRevocationAdmissionSealed(installation, incarnation)

    ensures:
        ReturnOrderedPageOfExactAdmittedOrdersAndCurrentOutcomes(installation, incarnation, cursor)
}

rule CommitCheckpointCaptureRevocation {
    -- A brokered handle invokes the original authority every time it opens or seals protected bytes.
    -- Revocation serializes with broker operations and fences later calls through already issued handles.
    -- The original archive vault binds the capture ID and exact request digest before closing its operation key.
    -- The vault retains its observation only after the operation key and its enrolled unit scope are withdrawn.
    -- An exact retry reads back the same provider observation after either process restarts.
    when: AuthenticatedCaptureRevocationContinued(operator, authority, capture)

    requires: CaptureAdmissionFenced(capture)
              and AdmittedCaptureRegionsDrained(capture)
              and ExactOriginalOperationOnlyKeyAuthority(authority, capture)

    ensures:
        IrrevocablyCloseCaptureKey(capture)
        RetainOriginalVaultRevocation(capture)
        PublishOriginalVaultObservation(capture)
}

rule SettleCheckpointCaptureDiscard {
    when: CheckpointCaptureDiscardContinued(capture)

    requires: CaptureAdmissionFenced(capture)
              and AdmittedCaptureRegionsDrained(capture)
              and OriginalCaptureKeyVaultAvailable(capture)

    ensures:
        ObserveCaptureKeyVaultWithdrawal(capture)
        if OriginalCaptureMediaWitnessMatches(capture) and ArchiveProviderInventoryEmpty(capture):
            RetainCaptureDiscardCompletion(capture)
}

rule HostedCheckpointRecovery {
    -- An installation with a selected replay archive restores from a complete replay capture and selects that capture when the restore completes.
    -- A pending restore retains its input while the selected serving capture retains current history.
    when: AuthenticatedRecoveryRestore(authority, name, capture_id, manifest_digest)

    requires: ConfiguredOrganizationOwns(authority, name)
              and RootInstallation(authority)
              and AcceptedArchiveManifest(authority, capture_id, manifest_digest)
              and ExactCheckpointOrCompletedRecovery(authority, capture_id)

    ensures:
        if CompletedRecovery(authority, capture_id, manifest_digest):
            ReturnOriginalRecoveryReceipt(authority, capture_id)
        else:
            RecoverUnderWriterFence(authority, capture_id)
            RetainAndReturnRecoveryReceipt(authority, capture_id, manifest_digest)
}

rule RetainServingReplayArchive {
    -- A parked root can select an independently verified replay capture of its exact current checkpoint.
    -- The installation publishes the capture and the recovery inventory digest observed in its archive custody together.
    -- A new region opens that source under its own admission before rechecking the published inventory.
    -- A retained capture preserves its accepted manifest and envelope for each object.
    -- A capture with whole-object authenticated trie data supplies the same logical contents through a bounded whole-object read after framed writes begin.
    -- A fresh capture authenticates whole-object trie data in an older serving pack under its original logical key.
    -- The fresh capture publishes authenticated frames over the same logical bytes at the original checkpoint.
    -- A framed file publication binds every frame to the digest and length of the complete plaintext file.
    -- A completed repair can be replaced by an independently verified replay archive of the same exact checkpoint before any replay archive has been selected.
    -- The source retains its residence while its selected capture still requires an envelope conversion for cell transfer.
    -- A held stage can serve whole-object reads before conversion, and requires the current serving envelope before terminal transfer.
    -- Later captures can inherit the admitted source and a newer verified checkpoint can replace it.
    -- A completed restore can advance to a newer replay capture after the root parks at that checkpoint.
    -- A superseded archive stays available to readers admitted under its identity until their work drains.
    when: AuthenticatedReplayArchiveRetention(authority, name, capture_id, manifest_digest)

    requires: ConfiguredOrganizationOwns(authority, name)
              and RootInstallation(authority)
              and AcceptedArchiveManifest(authority, capture_id, manifest_digest)
              and ExactParkedServingReplayCut(authority, capture_id)

    ensures:
        SelectServingReplayArchiveAndInventory(authority, capture_id, manifest_digest)
}

rule StageHistoricalCheckpoint {
    -- Primary members and the sealed inventory have separate slots in the stage vault.
    when: HistoricalCheckpointStageRequested(capture, target, operation)

    requires: ExactRetainedCapture(capture)
              and FreshHeldInstallation(target, operation)

    ensures:
        ReadArchiveUnderOriginalCustody(capture)
        BindTargetStorageAndKeyNamespaces(target)
        MaterializeVerifiedClosureWithFreshKey(capture, target)
        RetainStageInventory(target, operation)
        KeepPlacementAndLogsClosed(target)
}

rule ReadHeldCheckpoint {
    when: HeldStageReadRequested(target, operation, inventory_digest)

    requires: ExactHeldStage(target, operation, inventory_digest)

    ensures:
        AcquireHeldStageStorageRegion(target)
        VerifyAndReadFrozenInventory(target, inventory_digest)
        AuthenticateReaderAsOriginalCapture(target)
        DrainReadersBeforeReleasingStageStorage(target)
}

rule InspectHeldCheckpointOwnedRelations {
    when: AuthenticatedHeldStageOwnedInventoryRequested(source, capture, target, operation, inventory_digest)

    requires: ExactHeldStage(target, operation, inventory_digest)
              and OriginalStageCapture(target, source, capture)

    ensures:
        AcquireHeldStageStorageRegion(target)
        VerifyAndReadFrozenInventory(target, inventory_digest)
        ReturnOriginalTransactionAndSingleOwnedNamespaceInventory(target)
        DrainReadersBeforeReleasingStageStorage(target)
}

rule StreamHeldCheckpointOwnedEventFiles {
    when: AuthenticatedHeldStageOwnedEventsRequested(source, capture, target, operation, inventory_digest)

    requires: ExactHeldStage(target, operation, inventory_digest)
              and OriginalStageCapture(target, source, capture)
              and CompleteReplayInventory(target)

    ensures:
        AcquireHeldStageStorageRegion(target)
        VerifyAndReadFrozenInventory(target, inventory_digest)
        StreamCurrentOwnedTrieEventFilesAtOriginalTransaction(target)
        ConfirmStageHeldBeforeCompletingStream(target)
        DrainReadersBeforeReleasingStageStorage(target)
}

rule ReplayHeldNativeRootEffects {
    when: HeldDestinationOrdersNativeArchiveTransaction(target, archive, original, predecessor)

    requires: CurrentPlacedRestoreWriter(target)
              and ActiveRestoreHoldForArchive(target, archive)
              and ExactNativeReplayPredecessor(target, predecessor)
              and AbsentOriginalReplayReceipt(target, original)

    ensures:
        PreserveOriginalValidIntervalsAndCreationExpiry(original)
        CoOrderRootEffectsAndOriginalReplayReceipt(target, original)
        AdvanceNativeReplayFrontier(target, original)
}

rule RetireCheckpointArchives {
    when: RecoveryRetirementAuthorized(record)

    requires: ServingDisposalRecorded(record)
              and RecoveryHoldsReleased(record)

    ensures:
        RetireRegisteredArchiveCopies(record)
        WithdrawOnlyRegisteredCaptureKeys(record)
}

rule ObserveS3Inventory {
    when: S3DeletionContinued(record)

    requires: record.lifecycle = deleting
    requires: RecordUsesS3(record)
    requires: RecordedWriteLeasesExpired(record)
    requires: S3LeaseHorizonElapsed(record)
    requires: StreamDropped(record.id)

    ensures:
        if record.deletion_observation.first_empty_at = null:
            if RequiredSharedObjectsMaterialized(record):
                EraseS3Inventory(record.storage_root.prefix)
                if S3InventoryIsEmpty(record.storage_root.prefix) and ServingObjectsAreAbsent(record.storage_root.prefix):
                    Directory.cas(record.id, record.version, change: { deletion_observation: DeletionObservation(first_empty_at: now, second_empty_at: null) })
        else if record.deletion_observation.second_empty_at = null and now >= record.deletion_observation.first_empty_at + config.credential_ttl:
            if S3InventoryIsEmpty(record.storage_root.prefix) and ServingObjectsAreAbsent(record.storage_root.prefix):
                Directory.cas(record.id, record.version, change: { deletion_observation.second_empty_at: now })
            else:
                Directory.cas(record.id, record.version, change: { deletion_observation: DeletionObservation(leases_expire_at: now) })
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: upgrades
------------------------------------------------------------

rule RollEngine {
    -- An engine rollout drains members of the old version cell by cell, and moves each deployment to a member that satisfies its engine pin.
    -- A deployment pinned to the old version stays on the old pool until its pin is advanced.
    when: EngineRolloutStarted(cell, version)

    ensures:
        for member in cell.members where member.engine_version != version and member.may_receive_placements:
            if EligibleTargetOfVersionAndPoolExists(member, version) and EveryHoldingHasEligibleTargetOfVersion(member, version) and ParkedPinsRetainEligibleMembers(member):
                DrainScheduled(member, target_engine: version)
}

rule SetEnginePin {
    when: EnginePinRequested(record, version)

    requires: record.lifecycle in {live, quarantined}
    requires: version = null or HolderAndActivePlacementMatchEngine(record, version)
    requires: version = null or EligibleEngineExists(record, version)

    ensures:
        Directory.cas(record.id, record.version, change: { engine_pin: version })
}

rule UpgradeFormatLazily {
    -- Members read format versions N-2 to N and write N.
    -- A flush or compaction rewrites what it touches; a trickle of maintenance claims rewrites the rest.
    when: FlushStarted(holding)

    requires: holding.deployment.format_version < CurrentFormatVersion()

    ensures:
        holding.deployment.format_version = CurrentFormatVersion()
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: moving between cells
------------------------------------------------------------

rule BeginCellMove {
    -- The source and target have distinct object and key namespaces.
    -- Each cell's object namespace lies outside the other's cleanup prefix for the deployment.
    -- A retry holds the original source fence and resumes the registered destination prefix.
    -- A retry authenticates existing destination publications and compares their plaintext to the source before reusing them.
    -- The source selects one copy attempt at a time, binding its destination generation and namespaces, selected checkpoint, sealed source journals, and route intent.
    -- A copy that meets conflicting immutable bytes closes its staging attempt, waits until the attempt's admitted writes and uploads are accounted for, and continues in a fresh generation selected by the source.
    -- The destination follows only the successor of the attempt it closed, and retains each closed generation until the provider shows its exact prefix empty, on remote storage again after the scoped write horizon from the first empty observation.
    -- A replacement is triggered only by conflicting bytes found under the destination generation's own prefix.
    -- A prepared attempt is never closed, and only its own receipt can activate it.
    -- The prepared destination retains the exact certified replica cut, source progress and outstanding receiver reservation for replay in the new residence.
    -- A record staged before attempts were recorded continues as attempt zero.
    -- A source whose selected replay archive still overlays unframed original members reports the envelope mismatch before fencing.
    -- Only the attempt recorded as prepared can be published.
    when: CellMoveRequested(record, target_cell)

    requires: record.lifecycle = live
    requires: DestinationDeploymentPrefixIsDisjoint(record, target_cell)
    requires: DestinationCanServeEnginePin(record, target_cell)

    ensures:
        Directory.cas(record.id, record.epoch, change: {
            epoch: Epoch(value: record.epoch.value + 1),
            holder: null,
            lifecycle: moving
        })
        ForkedInto(target_cell, from: record, basis: CurrentBasis(record))
}

rule MoveCutover {
    -- The destination accepts placements only after the cell map switches to it.
    -- The destination authenticates each inherited or loose serving unit and reseals it into an independent destination publication slot.
    -- Pack resealing preserves the predecessor links and publication time.
    -- The destination verifies the certified retained interval and reseals each frame into its own slots before it can serve.
    -- A retry after the switch finishes publication and leaves the source tombstone.
    -- Forks in the source cell retain access to every object they share.
    -- Verify the destination's root owner, checkpoint catalog, required trie metadata and trie envelopes against its own storage generation.
    -- Record preparation after all destination storage regions drain.
    -- Before activating an already prepared destination, recheck that same checkpoint in its recorded namespace.
    -- Activation requires the destination to name the generation of the attempt the source selected.
    -- Disposal of a residence first empties its closed transfer generations.
    when: DestinationPrepared(record, target_cell)

    requires: record.lifecycle = moving
    requires: DestinationCanServeEnginePin(record, target_cell)

    ensures:
        CellMapRowWritten(deployment: record.id, cell: target_cell)
        Directory.cas(record.id, record.epoch, change: { lifecycle: deleted })
        DestinationOpenedForPlacement(record, target_cell)
}

rule CleanUpMovedSource {
    -- The source validates its object namespace and any supplied key namespace against the move's binding.
    when: MoveGraceElapsed(record)

    requires: record.lifecycle = deleted
    requires: record.source_namespaces != null
    requires: StorageNamespacesMatchSource(record)
    requires: DeploymentPrefixIsDisjoint(record)
    requires: RecordedWriteLeasesExpired(record)

    ensures:
        EraseSourceCopyAfterTransferringSharedObjects(record)
        if SourceUsesS3(record):
            EmptyMultipartInventoryObserved(record.storage_root.prefix)
            EmptyVersionInventoryObserved(record.storage_root.prefix)
        SourceCleanupRecorded(record, objects_erased)
        if SourceKeyServiceConfigured(record):
            SourceKeyCopyDestroyed(record.key_id)
            SourceCleanupRecorded(record, objects_and_key_erased)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: fairness
------------------------------------------------------------

rule ChargeQuery {
    -- Query CPU is scheduled by weighted deficit round robin across deployments, on a runtime separate from the write path.
    -- Memory is granted per query from the deployment's quota within the node's budget, and a query over its grant spills or is refused.
    -- A protected answer keeps its original delivery cutoff through the frontend and gateway buffers.
    -- Each socket handoff checks the cutoff and expiry closes a partly delivered reply.
    when: QueryArrived(member, query, session)

    ensures:
        Scheduled(query, weight: session.deployment.quotas.cpu_weight)
        MemoryGranted(query, within: session.deployment.quotas.max_query_memory_bytes)
}

rule EvictFairly {
    -- One node-wide cache, keyed by storage identity, object key and byte range.
    -- A writer, replica and snapshot of the same physical deployment reuse its immutable cached data.
    -- A new storage generation has an independent cache identity.
    -- Each deployment keeps a small reserved floor; above it, eviction takes from the deployment furthest over its weighted share, least recently used first within it.
    when: CacheFull(member)

    ensures:
        Evicted(member, from: FurthestOverShare(member.cache))
}

rule BoundTailMemory {
    -- Tail live indexes share one node budget; under pressure the node flushes the largest tails first.
    when: TailMemoryPressure(member)

    ensures:
        for holding in LargestTails(member):
            FlushStarted(holding)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

invariant CostFollowsActivity {
    -- A parked deployment holds no memory on any member, no log-server state, no timer, no task and no connection.
    -- Its cost is its directory record and its objects.
    -- A node releases the holding's trim watermark with the holding and the operation lock with its last user.
    for record in DeploymentRecords:
        record.is_parked implies
            (not exists h in Holdings: h.deployment = record and h.state != deposed)
            and (not exists s in LogServerStreams: s.deployment = record.id)
            and (not exists sub in Subscriptions: sub.deployment = record)
}

invariant OneEffectiveWriterPerEpoch {
    -- For every deployment and epoch, at most one member has a holding that is not deposed.
    for record in DeploymentRecords:
        for e in Epochs:
            count(h in Holdings where h.deployment = record and h.epoch = e and h.state != deposed) <= 1
}

invariant NoTwoWritersHaveEffect {
    -- Effect takes five forms, and each is closed by the epoch.
    --   1. A committed log record: AppendIsConditionalOnEpochAndPosition and SealIsAPromise.
    --   2. A client acknowledgement: ApplyOnCommit acknowledges only committed records.
    --   3. A visible object: WritersCarryTheirEpoch and VisibilityIsByReference.
    --   4. A deletion: OnlyTheReaperDeletes.
    --   5. A durable state change: EpochBoundChangesAreConditional.
    -- A stale read is the remaining risk, and ServeLinearizableReadOnWriter closes it for linearizable reads; at_basis and as_of reads are correct on any member because history is immutable.
    for record in DeploymentRecords:
        CommittedRecordsFormOneChain(record)
        and for r1, r2 in CommittedRecords(record): r1.lsn < r2.lsn implies r1.epoch <= r2.epoch
}

invariant SafetyIgnoresFailureDetection {
    -- No rule's safety depends on a member's lease, a suspicion verdict, or a clock agreeing.
    -- A wrong verdict costs one seal and one cache move.
}

invariant SystemTimeRises {
    -- Within a deployment, system time strictly increases with lsn, across epochs and members.
    for record in DeploymentRecords:
        for r1, r2 in CommittedResolvedTxs(record): r1.lsn < r2.lsn implies r1.system_time < r2.system_time
}

invariant CheckpointNeverAheadOfLog {
    for record in DeploymentRecords:
        record.checkpoint != null implies record.checkpoint.lsn <= LastCommittedLsn(record)
}

invariant TailIsBounded {
    -- A serving holding's tail stays within its flush triggers, up to one flush in flight.
    for holding in Holdings where holding.state = serving:
        holding.tail_bytes <= 2 * config.flush_tail_bytes
}

invariant ObjectsStayInTheirLineage {
    -- A manifest references only objects whose owner is a deployment of its own lineage.
    for record in DeploymentRecords where record.checkpoint != null:
        for object in ReferencedBy(record.checkpoint.manifest):
            deployments[object.owner].lineage = record.lineage
}

invariant OneDatabasePerSession {
    -- A session's catalog contains exactly its capability's deployment; the planner cannot name another.
    -- A parked deployment with a pinned compatible engine can opt into the versioned placed outcome format.
    -- The directory preserves this setting through placement changes and parking.
    -- The pinned engine must support the format on every attachment that replays the deployment.
    -- Original-format readers refuse the versioned attempt identity before installing that record.
    -- Placed session transaction inspection reports only the deployment's progress.
    -- A node session is bound to the placement it opened against.
    -- A replacement with the same name requires a new backend session.
    -- Placed Flight SQL selects its deployment on the first authenticated request.
    -- A session can redeem tickets and use handles only within its deployment.
    -- A transaction or prepared handle requires its authenticated owner and local attachment.
    -- A session waits for the positions in its own deployment when applying an await token.
    -- Cross-deployment reads exist only through an explicit grant, served on the snapshot tier.
    -- For a configured user grant list, an exact database rule takes precedence over the user's default rule, including during gateway reconnection.
    for session in Sessions:
        Catalog(session) = { session.capability.deployment } + GrantedSnapshots(session)
}

invariant KeysNameTheirOwner {
    -- Every object key begins with its owner's StorageRoot prefix.
    for object in Objects:
        StartsWith(object.key, deployments[object.owner].storage_root.prefix)
}

invariant QuarantineIsolates {
    for record in DeploymentRecords where record.lifecycle = quarantined:
        record.isolation_class = isolation
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Actor Declarations
------------------------------------------------------------

actor Gateway {
    -- Terminates client connections, owns sessions, authenticates, mints capabilities and idempotency keys, and routes by affinity.
    -- A gateway configured with a PostgreSQL TLS key store completes the client's TLS handshake before it routes startup.
    -- A configured gateway refuses plaintext startup before any holder receives credentials.
    -- A Flight SQL gateway forwards the authenticated transport peer using a proof for the deployment and writer epoch.
    -- The writer authenticates the session with the peer in that proof.
    -- Each Flight SQL call using a session token resolves its original writer placement.
    -- A displaced Flight SQL session must establish a new authenticated session.
    identified_by: MemberRecord where role = gateway
}

actor Node {
    -- Holds deployments, serves reads in every tier, hosts subscriptions.
    identified_by: MemberRecord where role = node
}

actor LogServer {
    -- Holds loglets and enforces the epoch promise.
    -- A durable log server's advertised endpoint identifies its log storage across process restarts.
    -- An ensemble counts each storage endpoint once.
    identified_by: MemberRecord where role = log_server
}

actor Worker {
    -- Runs compaction jobs and scan fragments; holds nothing.
    identified_by: MemberRecord where role = worker
}

actor Reaper {
    -- The only principal that deletes objects.
    identified_by: MemberRecord where role = reaper
}

actor Controller {
    -- Confirms suspicion, admits and drains members, rebalances, autoscales, drains orphaned tails, rolls engines.
    -- Holds no state of its own: every fact it acts on is in the directory.
    -- If it is down, placed deployments keep working; only membership changes and moves wait.
    identified_by: MemberRecord where role = controller
}

actor Operator {
    -- Creates cells, sets quotas and isolation classes, clears quarantine.
    identified_by: String
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

surface ClientAccess {
    -- What a client sees.
    facing client: Client

    context session: Session

    exposes:
        session.basis
        session.freshness

    provides:
        CommitRequested(client, session, tx)
        ReadRequested(client, session, query)
        IntakeSubmitted(client, session.deployment, tx, key)
        ForkRequested(client, session.deployment, basis, org)
        DeleteRequested(client, session.deployment)

    @guarantee MovesAreInvisible
        -- A planned handoff stalls writes for one log round plus a redirect, and touches no client connection.
        -- A failover stalls writes until detection and claim complete.
        -- A successful replay of a previously committed permitted write recovers the original committed outcome.
        -- A dispatch failure preserves any complete command response the gateway has received.
        -- A writable pgwire pipeline waits for ReadyForQuery between command cycles so each autocommit cycle has its own retry key.
        -- A copy-in transfer can send its input frames before ReadyForQuery.
        -- The gateway can resend unanswered extended-protocol setup frames before execution.
        -- The gateway can ask a replacement to replay an unanswered simple query under its original command permit.
        -- A replacement admits replay of a single autocommit statement before executing any part of it.
        -- Transaction control and batches return an unknown outcome after an ambiguous failure.
        -- A writer installs a key before executing the command it guards.
        -- The writer preserves the gateway key through changes to SQL session state.
        -- An unanswered write can be replayed only when both writers support the same private permit contract.
        -- The permit contract protects a command even if a legacy resolver inherits its admitted source record.
        -- The gateway waits for the replacement writer to acknowledge the original permit before replaying the command.
        -- The dispatch and replay share an exclusive submission cutoff issued when the command starts, within replay_permit_age.
        -- Both writers refuse a permitted submission at or after that cutoff before appending it.
        -- Every committed permitted write records its command key with its effects, and retains the marker through the cutoff.
        -- An aborted attempt leaves the existing marker state intact, and another admitted attempt can still commit if that state has no marker.
        -- Closed storage before the first command reply allows a keyed retry on a newer writer epoch.
        -- A holder's driver shutdown during a command leaves the outcome unknown until a keyed retry returns it.
        -- An authenticated idle session retains its acknowledged named prepared statements across holder changes.
        -- SQL PREPARE and named pgwire Parse retain the search path and timezone used when they were registered.
        -- Named pgwire Parse also retains the supplied parameter type identifiers.
        -- Acknowledged DISCARD ALL clears the prepared statements eligible for replay.
        -- An open pgwire session renews idle protection for its current backend and stops renewing it when that connection closes.
        -- A failed startup ends the client's authentication exchange.
        -- A finite reconnect window bounds the time an idle session waits for a replacement holder.
        -- Recovery of a pending command uses one command_replay_window across replacement writers until the gateway takes a reply for forwarding to the client.
        -- After the window elapses without a reply, the command's outcome is reported as unknown.
        -- A placement notification can start the next reconnect attempt before its fallback timer elapses.
        -- A replacement connection must resolve the original deployment identity.
        -- When startup specifies an installation, the backend verifies the identity of its attached database before the gateway confirms readiness.
        -- Replacement backends verify the same installation before the gateway replays settings or commands.

    @guarantee GatewayConnectionLimits
        -- Each gateway bounds its accepted sockets and the sessions routed to any deployment.
        -- A saturated deployment can refuse a new session while another deployment still has a slot.
        -- Closing a session frees its reserved capacity.
        -- A gateway admits buffered frame bytes before reading the body and releases the charge when their last owner drops them.

    @guarantee WriterDialRecovery
        -- A failed dial to a confirmed writer is retryable within the enclosing startup or replay window.
        -- A gateway attempt follows placement changes while that window remains open.

    @guarantee ReadYourWrites
        -- Within a session, every read sees every transaction the session was acknowledged.

    @guarantee AtMostOneCommitPerPermit
        -- Retries sharing one private permit produce at most one committed effect.
        -- A permitted write uses its own engine-owned marker under the command key.
        -- The writer refuses a permit if the transaction supplies a different application key.

    @guarantee OrdinaryRetryReceipts
        -- The ordinary retry identity is the database, the authenticated principal and the key.
        -- Its receipt records a digest of the transaction's operations and effect-bearing options.
        -- A retry with the same body is answered with the original's outcome; a different body under the identity aborts with rtdb/idempotency-conflict.
        -- The resolver decides from engine-owned receipts in xt.idempotency_receipts, which SQL cannot write, valid for idempotency_window of system time.
        -- A string application key takes precedence over the gateway key and must be stable across attempts for deduplication.
        -- A transaction without a usable application key uses the gateway key and its transaction ordinal.
}

external entity ScopedDatabaseClient {
    -- A client whose verified credential carries label sets for the selected database.
}

actor ScopedRowReader {
    identified_by: ScopedDatabaseClient
}

surface ScopedRowVisibility {
    facing client: ScopedRowReader

    @guarantee ScopedAdmission
        -- The serving route verifies the host-issued credential before admitting a scoped call.
        -- A route advertises scoped visibility only when each operation it admits for scoped credentials preserves the scope.
        -- Plain SQL and legacy endpoints require separate admission and cannot widen a scoped credential's authority.

    @guarantee VisibleVersions
        -- A table declares the column containing each row version's opaque label.
        -- A read resolves branch lineage and the winning bitemporal version before applying the read label set.
        -- An excluded winning version masks the older or inherited row throughout the interval where it wins.
        -- Query operators, ranking, change images and aggregates consume the resulting visible view.
        -- Scoped catalog access and query metadata describe only the admitted view.

    @guarantee ScopedCoordinates
        -- A scoped basis orders the authorized effects of the selected view within the database incarnation and read scope.
        -- A scoped change position and through value use the same coordinate as the read basis.
        -- Scoped system time is logical and derived from the authorized effect sequence.
        -- A scoped position, pagination token or claim can be redeemed only in its issuing incarnation and scope.
        -- Recovery and routing preserve the meaning of admitted coordinates.
        -- A waiting feed advances when its scope observes an effect or its requested coverage is complete.
        -- A hidden winner can affect the authorized sequence by masking an already visible row.
        -- Histories with the same authorized effect sequence produce the same row results, projected time and change coverage for the same scoped reads.
        -- This property holds for successful calls at corresponding admitted prefixes and a common valid-time observation.
        -- Explicit guard outcomes, resource use and service latency have their own contracts.

    @guarantee ProjectedRetention
        -- The writer orders a bounded lease for a selected projected cut and a clear publication row at its fixed valid observation.
        -- The lease belongs to its verified issuing scope and database incarnation.
        -- Both serving layouts retain the projected cut in the native recoverable history until exclusive expiry, release or applicable erasure.
        -- A hidden-only erasure preserves the visible effect sequence and previously issued projected coordinates.
        -- An erasure affecting retained visible history closes that exact view and redacts its historical images.

    @guarantee ScopedProtectedRelease
        -- The issuer separately authorizes scoped protected reads.
        -- A protected Query or Changes page captures provenance from the same projected input and returns an opaque complete proof, including when its contributor set is empty.
        -- Only the checked Original owner resolves the proof into exact enrolled occurrences and native receipts.
        -- An application response selects its exact promoted registrations and frame through the checked Original provider.
        -- The original writer orders nonempty response admission and retains each occurrence in the drain through a positive terminal.
        -- Managed Original release and disposal use separately issued credentials pinned to the database, Original owner and provider authority.
        -- Managed admission derives the exact hold registrations from the checked Original response and enrolled receipts.

    @guarantee NativeDependentReadEvidence
        -- A commissioned destination owner selects one physical branch creation, valid point and enrolled placement at an exact native restore and current destination generation.
        -- The destination owner matches the selected native member to its independently retained enrollment before supplying the application key to the Original read.
        -- The current Original writer confirms the same occurrence at the pinned source cut and current point, the applied carrier receipt and each copied group's live fence.
        -- The independently commissioned destination registry recovers the exact retained enrollment, registered frame, selected application key and exclusive cutoff for the response part.
        -- The release proof rechecks the Original writer, journal epoch and key generation and the destination generation after observing the registered part.
        -- A transport enqueues protected data only under fresh ordered Original admission and retains the part through its positive disposition.

    @guarantee NativeInheritedCarrierDelivery
        -- The destination independently recovers its witnessed enrollment before carrier extraction.
        -- The Original family authority confirms the current application generation, exact reader admission and complete linked occurrence receipts for the held point.
        -- The exact destination event and its pinned Original event share one protected envelope identity.
        -- The Original opens that envelope and releases the enrolled sealed carrier and link under the held reader.
        -- The returned inventory includes the selected occurrence and its complete retained native source ancestry through the Original.
        -- A dedicated transport owner reserves one part and nonce and durably registers the exact serialized frame and the held read commitment before admission.
        -- The Original orders that frame against all promoted contributors under the current writer, and each contributor remains held through verified disposition.
        -- A successor Original writer can dispose the exact historical registration under the same installation and positive terminal evidence from both custody authorities.

    @guarantee ScopedWrites
        -- The transaction resolver enforces the write scope and any SingleLabelPerTx declaration in both modes.
        -- dev/doc/tx.allium defines label checks, claim validation and guard outcomes.
}

surface HolderToLog {
    -- What a holder exchanges with its loglet's ensemble.
    facing node: Node

    context holding: Holding

    provides:
        AppendCommitted(node, holding, record)
        AppendRefused(node, holding, promised)
        SealRepliesReceived(node, holding, replies)
        EnsembleDegraded(node, holding, loglet)

    contracts:
        demands LogService

    @guarantee OneDurableWritePerTransaction
        -- A committed transaction costs one quorum append, shared with every other deployment on the same writer within group_commit_window.
}

surface ControlPlane {
    facing controller: Controller

    context cell: Cell

    provides:
        MoveChosen(controller, record, target)
        NodeOverWatermark(controller, member, dimension)
        EngineRolloutStarted(controller, cell, version)
        CellMoveRequested(controller, record, target_cell)
        AuthenticatedReplayArchiveRetention(authority, name, capture_id, manifest_digest)

    contracts:
        demands Directory
        demands CellMap

    @guarantee FailoverIsBulkAndCheap
        -- A member's death changes one member record.
        -- No deployment record is written for it, and only deployments with demand or an open tail are claimed.

    @guarantee PlacementFollowsLoad
        -- A deployment is claimed by the highest-ranked eligible, unpressured member, and moved only to a next rank when a node is over a watermark.
}

surface StorageAccess {
    facing node: Node

    context holding: Holding

    provides:
        TerminalAllocationAccessRequested(node, allocation)

    contracts:
        demands ObjectStorage

    @guarantee ZombiesCannotDamageStorage
        -- A deposed holder can create only orphans under its own epoch's prefix until its credential expires, and can delete nothing.

    @guarantee FramedObjectsAuthenticateTheirContents
        -- A framed publication authenticates the plaintext length and digest and binds each frame to the logical object key and its position.
        -- A portable publication selects its original key slot from the origin carried by its bytes.
        -- An immutable retry that compares plaintext authenticates the existing payload before accepting a match.
}

surface CheckpointArchiveDisposal {
    facing operator: Operator

    provides:
        AuthenticatedCaptureDiscard(operator, authority, capture)
        AuthenticatedCaptureRevocationContinued(operator, authority, capture)
        AuthenticatedCaptureRevocationScheduled(operator, authority, capture, due_at)
        CheckpointCaptureDiscardContinued(capture)

    contracts:
        demands Directory
}

surface TerminalStorageEnrollment {
    facing operator: Operator

    provides:
        TerminalAllocationEnrollmentRequested(operator, allocation, qualification)

    contracts:
        demands ObjectStorage
}

surface TerminalStorageMaintenance {
    facing reaper: Reaper

    provides:
        TerminalAllocationAccessRequested(reaper, allocation)
        HostedLooseFileReleaseDue(release)

    contracts:
        demands ObjectStorage
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

open question "What does an idle RTDB database cost in memory today, and what is the least it can be made to cost?"
    -- resident_idle_target_bytes is a target, not a measurement.
    -- If a realistic deployment's catalogs and schema cost megabytes rather than kilobytes, warm density falls tenfold and ParkUnderPressure parks far more often.

open question "Is losing submit-and-walk-away during a holder gap acceptable as the default?"
    -- Routed commits are retried by the gateway across a failover; only deployments with an intake stream accept work while no holder exists.
    -- If clients rely on durable-before-resolution submission, the intake becomes mandatory for them.

open question "How is per-deployment cryptographic erasure reconciled with objects shared across a lineage?"
    -- TransferSharedObjects re-wraps shared objects' keys to a surviving referrer, so deleting one member is proved by absence and key destruction of what it alone owned.
    -- Erasing a row (ERASE) applies to every member of the lineage that can see it; the compliance contract has to say so.

open question "What lies beyond one holder's write rate?"
    -- One installation's appends share one version line.
    -- Today one holder bounds ordering, resolution and its resident set while object storage stays elastic.
    -- RTDB's target splits an ordering tier from place-sharded resolvers and storage, with epoch-fenced place handoff and no change to commit order (modeled in verification/tla/ShardedOrder.tla).
    -- Generic Bound { key, delta, min, max } in rtdb.store.v1.Append evaluates atomic bounded-counter updates at the ordering point without read-set conflicts between concurrent spenders.

open question "Is the batched seal check the right price for linearizable reads?"
    -- It adds up to one group-commit round to a linearizable read, and involves no clock.
    -- A clock-bound read lease would be cheaper and would bring clocks into read safety.

open question "How far may placement depart from rank 1 before routing needs the directory on every request?"
    -- Every off-rank placement makes gateways depend on pushed routes and leaves a cold cache behind.

open question "Can cooperative scheduling isolate tenants well enough inside one process?"
    -- A query that fails to yield, or memory accounting that undercounts Arrow buffers, defeats weighted fairness; a process or microVM boundary per tenant costs density.

open question "How do pins longer than max_snapshot_lifetime keep their objects and tail reachable?"
    -- Pinned reviews and pinned Flight readers in Bijection hold a basis for hours or days.
    -- A deployment record would carry pins, each a basis and an expiry; ReapUnreachable would keep everything a pinned manifest references, and a flush would keep a pinned tail reachable.

open question "How does a subscriber that has fallen below the trim point resume its change feed?"
    -- The log keeps only the tail above the checkpoint.
    -- Exports, watches and Bijection's `changes` need an unbroken feed: a scan of blocks ordered by system time between two bases, then the log.

open question "What is the node-library form of a holder embedded in another process?"
    -- Bijection's Conductor would embed the holder role, call it in process instead of through the gateway RPC, and lend it its memory budget and NVMe cache.
    -- The rules here assume a node is its own process.

open question "Does the directory's envelope count forks?"
    -- Each durable branch is a DeploymentRecord; a deployment with thousands of branches multiplies directory rows, and cell_max_deployments has to say which it bounds.


-- Each incoming cell transfer generation owns a distinct destination replica stream.
-- The detached publisher enrolls its broker namespace before admitting the first provider upload.
-- Closing the generation withdraws access to its journal pointers.
-- After admitted work drains, the enrolled broker settles every detached version and key in that exact closed generation, including versions already adopted.
